= Chemical substance
{wiki}

= Hydrofluoric acid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{HF(aq)}$}

An aqueous solution of <hydrogen> fluoride. In silicon-processing mixtures it can dissolve oxidized silicon-containing surface material; its role should not be confused with an oxidant's role in creating that material.

= Diisobutylaluminium hydride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{(iBu)_2AlH}$}

= DIBAL-H
{synonym}

$\mathrm{(iBu)_2AlH}$, a hydride reducing reagent. Low-temperature, controlled-stoichiometry reduction can convert an <ester> into an <aldehyde> or a <lactone> into a <lactol>; conditions must prevent further reduction.

= Tributyl phosphate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{OP(OBu)_3}$}

A neutral oxygen-donor extractant, $\mathrm{OP(OBu)_3}$, often abbreviated TBP. It can solvate metal nitrate species in <solvent extraction>. Selectivity depends on acidity, metal <oxidation state>, diluent and subsequent stripping conditions.

= Zinc chloride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{ZnCl_2}$}

A <zinc> salt used as a <Lewis acid> and coordination reagent.

= Titanium tetrachloride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{TiCl_4}$}

A molecular titanium(IV) <chloride> used as a <Lewis acid>.

= Zinc borohydride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{Zn(BH_4)_2}$}

A <hydride> reagent that can coordinate nearby <oxygen> donors while reducing a <carbonyl>.

= Lithium fluoride
{parent=Chemical substance}

The ionic compound LiF, a discharge product of a <lithium–carbon monofluoride battery>.

= Zinc oxide
{parent=Chemical substance}

An <oxide> <semiconductor> whose common wurtzite phase is piezoelectric.

= Lead zirconate titanate
{parent=Chemical substance}

= PZT
{c}
{synonym}

A ferroelectric solid solution widely used for piezoelectric transduction.

= Naphthalene
{parent=Chemical substance}

A fused two-ring aromatic hydrocarbon.

= Ureidopyrimidinone
{parent=Chemical substance}

= UPy unit
{c}
{synonym}

A hydrogen-bonding unit capable of forming a strongly associated, self-complementary <dimer> through a four-hydrogen-bond array.

= Calixarene
{parent=Chemical substance}

A macrocyclic scaffold constructed from phenolic rings and connecting methylene groups.

= Tert-butyldimethylsilyl chloride
{parent=Chemical substance}

= TBSCl
{c}
{synonym}

The silylating reagent $\mathrm{tBuMe_2SiCl}$, commonly abbreviated <TBSCl>. <nucleophilic substitution> at silicon converts an <alcohol> into a <silyl ether>.

= Hydroperoxide
{parent=Chemical substance}

A compound containing $\mathrm{R-O-O-H}$. Its <oxygen>–<oxygen> bond distinguishes it from the corresponding <alcohol>.

= Methyl fluorosulfonate
{parent=Chemical substance}

The methylating reagent $\mathrm{FSO_2OMe}$. Nucleophilic attack at methyl displaces fluorosulfonate.

= Trimethyloxonium tetrafluoroborate
{parent=Chemical substance}

The oxonium salt $\mathrm{Me_3O^+BF_4^-}$, a strong methylating reagent. <carbonyl> O-methylation followed by alpha-deprotonation can form an <enol ether>.

= Sodium cyanoborohydride
{parent=Chemical substance}

The <hydride> reagent $\mathrm{NaBH_3CN}$. Its comparatively mild reactivity permits selected <imine> and iminium <reductions>.

= Tributyltin hydride
{parent=Chemical substance}

The organotin <hydride> $\mathrm{Bu_3SnH}$, a hydrogen-atom or <hydride> source depending on the reaction system.

= Carbanion
{parent=Chemical substance}

An anionic species with a negatively charged <carbon> centre. Resonance stabilization and substituents control its basicity and nucleophilicity.

= Macrocycle
{parent=Chemical substance}

A molecule containing a large ring. Multiple inward-facing donor groups can preorganize it for metal-ion binding.

= Anion-templated macrocyclization
{parent=Macrocycle}

Ring formation favored by binding an anionic template that organizes the reactive precursors.

= Aziridinium ion
{parent=Chemical substance}

A positively charged three-membered <nitrogen> heterocycle. Intramolecular formation from a tertiary amino <alcohol> with an activated hydroxyl can be followed by regioselective nucleophilic ring opening and net ring expansion.

= Hypochlorous acid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{HOCl}$}

The <chlorine> oxyacid $\mathrm{HOCl}$. In polar heterogeneous chemistry it can react with <hydrogen chloride> to release molecular <chlorine> and <water>, helping convert reservoir <chlorine> into photolabile forms.

= Nitrone
{parent=Chemical substance}

An imine N-oxide with a three-atom C–N–O dipolar electronic system.

= Potassium hexamethyldisilazide
{parent=Chemical substance}

A strong potassium <amide> base.

= Trimethylsilyl triflate
{parent=Chemical substance}

A strongly electrophilic silylating reagent.

= Trimethylsilyl chloride
{parent=Chemical substance}

A chlorosilane used to trap oxygen nucleophiles as trimethylsilyl ethers.

= Lithium hexamethyldisilazide
{parent=Chemical substance}

A strong lithium <amide> base used for <enolate> formation.

= Acid anhydride
{parent=Chemical substance}

A compound containing two acyl groups joined through oxygen.

= Cyclic anhydride
{parent=Acid anhydride}

An <acid anhydride> whose two acyl groups belong to the same ring.

= Maleic anhydride
{parent=Cyclic anhydride}

The five-membered cyclic anhydride of cis-butenedioic acid.

= Organic azide
{parent=Chemical substance}

An organic compound bearing a covalently attached N3 group.

= TMEDA
{parent=Chemical substance}
{c}

Tetramethylethylenediamine, a bidentate nitrogen-donor <Ligand in coordination chemistry>.

= Cyclobutadienyl dianion
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_4H_4^{2-}}$}

The six-pi-electron ion $\mathrm{C_4H_4^{2-}}$. In an ideal equal-coupling four-site Hückel ring, one bonding and two nonbonding pi orbitals are filled, leaving the highest antibonding orbital empty.

= Hexagonal boron nitride
{parent=Chemical substance}

A layered insulating boron–nitrogen material used as a substrate or encapsulation layer for two-dimensional materials. Assembly defects and exposed edges can compromise an otherwise effective cap.

= Molybdenum disulfide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{MoS_2}$}

A layered compound $\mathrm{MoS_2}$. Atomically thin films are used in electronic and optical devices; crystal phase, defects, thickness and the local environment affect their response.

= Norbornane
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_7H_{12}}$}

= Bicyclo\[2.2.1\]heptane
{synonym}

A seven-<carbon> bicyclic framework with three paths between its bridgeheads containing two, two and one internal atoms. Mapping these paths is useful when checking a tandem Michael cyclization product.

= Nitromethane
{parent=Chemical substance}

The nitroalkane $\mathrm{CH_3NO_2}$. Its <nitronate> can add to a <carbonyl> in a <Henry reaction>.

= Nitroethane
{parent=Chemical substance}

The nitroalkane $\mathrm{CH_3CH_2NO_2}$. Alpha <deprotonation> gives a <nitronate> that can form <carbon>–<carbon> bonds.

= Sodium amide
{parent=Chemical substance}

The ionic compound $\mathrm{NaNH_2}$, commonly used as a <strong base>. <ketone> <deprotonation> produces an <enolate> and <ammonia>.

= Silicon nitride
{parent=Chemical substance}

A silicon–nitrogen material used as an insulating, protective and optical coating. Device films may be nonstoichiometric and hydrogenated; bulk $\mathrm{Si_3N_4}$ is not a complete description of every deposited film.

= Borate
{parent=Chemical substance}

An oxygen-containing <anion> of <boron>. <borates> can contain trigonal $\mathrm{BO_3}$ and tetrahedral $\mathrm{BO_4}$ units joined by bridging <oxygen>.

= Cyclic triborate
{parent=Borate}
{title2=$\mathrm{[B_3O_6]^{3-}}$}

The <anion> $\mathrm{[B_3O_6]^{3-}}$ formed from three trigonal $\mathrm{BO_3}$ units sharing corners in an alternating six-membered B–O ring. Each <boron> also has one terminal <oxygen>.

= Thiocyanate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{SCN^-}$}

The <anion> $\mathrm{SCN^-}$, which can coordinate through <nitrogen> or <sulfur>. Linkage changes donor identity without changing its formal charge.

= Thiocyanate coordination mode
{parent=Thiocyanate}

<thiocyanate> can coordinate through <nitrogen>, <sulfur>, or both ends, and can bridge several <metal> centres. A linkage label specifies donor atoms, whereas the bridging index $\mu_n$ specifies the number of connected <metals>. A short C–N bond and long C–S bond favour an $\mathrm{N\equiv C-S^-}$ description; greater C–S and smaller C–N <bond orders> give more $\mathrm{^-N=C=S}$ character. Internal distances alone need not determine how many <metals> are attached.

= Triply bridging thiocyanate
{parent=Thiocyanate coordination mode}

A $\mu_3$ <thiocyanate> connecting three <metal> centres. In layered first-row divalent <metal> <thiocyanates>, its <nitrogen> coordinates one <metal> and its <sulfur> coordinates two others, giving an $\mathrm{MN_2S_4}$ octahedral coordination environment.

= Pyridine-2,5-dicarboxylate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_7H_3NO_4^{2-}}$}

The dianion of <pyridine-2,5-dicarboxylic acid>, useful as a <bridging ligand> because its N,O donor pair and its more remote <carboxylate> can bind different <metal> centres.

= Biphenyl
{parent=Chemical substance}

Two phenyl rings joined directly by a <carbon>–<carbon> bond. Restricted rotation and appropriate <substituents> can produce <axial chirality>.

= Methyl iodide
{parent=Chemical substance}

The compound $\mathrm{CH_3I}$, an <electrophile> for SN2 <alkylation>. The <carbon> is attacked as <iodide> leaves.

= Allyl bromide
{parent=Chemical substance}

The compound $\mathrm{CH_2{=}CHCH_2Br}$. <nucleophilic substitution> at its bromomethylene <carbon> introduces an <allyl group> without consuming the <alkene>.

= Hydrogen halide
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Hydrogen iodide
{parent=Hydrogen halide}
{title2=$\mathrm{HI}$}

The <diatomic molecule> containing one <hydrogen> atom and one <iodine> atom. Its measured <spectroscopic wavenumbers> include anharmonic corrections beyond the two-term <Morse oscillator> model.<Hydrogen iodide: Constants of diatomic molecules by Klaus P. Huber and others>[Ground-state diatomic constants and higher anharmonic coefficients]{ref}

= Hydrogen bromide
{parent=Hydrogen halide}
{title2=$\mathrm{HBr}$}

The gaseous <hydrogen halide> consisting of one <hydrogen> atom and one <bromine> atom. Its <molecular vibration> can be studied through its <vibrational fundamental> and weak <vibrational overtones>. The aqueous acid and the isolated <diatomic molecule> should be distinguished when specifying a dissociation process.<Hydrogen bromide: Constants of diatomic molecules by Klaus P. Huber and others>[Ground-state diatomic constants]{ref}

= Hydroiodic acid
{parent=Hydrogen halide}

Aqueous hydrogen iodide is strongly acidic, and iodide can also participate in reductive transformations. These roles must be separated when explaining a reaction.

= Ozonide
{parent=Chemical substance}

A peroxide-containing product of ozone chemistry. In classical alkene ozonolysis, a secondary ozonide is converted to carbonyl products during a suitable workup.

= Carbonyl oxide
{parent=Chemical substance}

A three-atom dipolar species formed in the classical Criegee account of ozonolysis. Recombination with a carbonyl compound can form a secondary ozonide.

= Molozonide
{parent=Chemical substance}

A short-lived primary ozonide formed by ozone cycloaddition to an alkene. Fragmentation generates a carbonyl compound and a carbonyl oxide.

= Tosyl chloride
{parent=Chemical substance}

= p-Toluenesulfonyl chloride
{synonym}

An organic sulfonyl chloride used to activate alcohol or oxime oxygen as a leaving group.

= Hydroxylamine
{parent=Chemical substance}

The compound $\mathrm{NH_2OH}$. Its nitrogen can add to carbonyl compounds, followed by dehydration to an oxime.

= Oxime
{parent=Chemical substance}

An oxime contains a $\mathrm{C=N{-}OH}$ group, commonly formed from a carbonyl compound and hydroxylamine. Unsymmetrical ketoximes can have distinct geometrical isomers.

= Oxime O-tosylation
{parent=Oxime}

Formation of an oxime O-sulfonate increases leaving-group ability at oxygen. Subsequent anti migration can proceed with N–O bond cleavage.

= Ketoxime
{parent=Oxime}

An oxime derived from a ketone. The two carbon substituents and the N–OH orientation determine which group can migrate in a stereospecific Beckmann rearrangement.

= Pyrazine
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_4H_4N_2}$}

A planar six-membered <aromatic ring> with nitrogen at opposite ring sites. Its molecular <point group> is the <D2h point group>. The six ring $p_z$ <atomic orbitals> form a six-<electron> <conjugated system>; the in-plane nitrogen lone pairs are outside this $\pi$ basis.

= Tetracyanonickelate(II)
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$[\mathrm{Ni(CN)_4}]^{2-}$}

A <coordination complex> with four carbon-bound <cyanide> ligands and a formal nickel(II), $d^8$, centre. <Square planar molecular geometry> permits a <low-spin complex> with no <unpaired electrons>.

= Boron trifluoride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{BF_3}$}

An isolated <boron trifluoride> <molecule> has <trigonal planar molecular geometry> and the <D3h molecular point group>. Its three equal fluorine contributions give $I_\parallel=3m_{\rm F}R^2$ and $I_\perp=3m_{\rm F}R^2/2$ in the <rigid rotor> approximation.

= Phenazine
{parent=Chemical substance}

A nitrogen-containing aromatic scaffold; redox-active derivatives produced by bacteria can affect ecological interactions and electron transfer.

= Pyocyanin
{parent=Phenazine}

A blue, redox-active phenazine produced by Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Its biological effects depend on redox conditions, concentration and local electron acceptors.

= Acyl-CoA
{parent=Chemical substance}

A coenzyme-A thioester carrying an acyl group, used in lipid metabolism and many other pathways.

= Dhurrin
{parent=Chemical substance}

A cyanogenic glucoside from sorghum whose pathway includes two P450 enzymes and a glucosyltransferase.

= Acyl chloride
{parent=Chemical substance}

An <acyl chloride> contains $\mathrm{R{-}C(O)Cl}$. Addition of a <nucleophile> and <chloride> elimination is a common substitution pathway.

= Benzoyl chloride
{parent=Acyl chloride}

$\mathrm{PhCOCl}$.

= Acetyl chloride
{parent=Acyl chloride}

$\mathrm{CH_3COCl}$, an acylating reagent that converts an <alcohol> into an acetate <ester> when HCl is suitably removed.

= 2-Methylpropanoyl chloride
{parent=Acyl chloride}

= Isobutyryl chloride
{synonym}

The <acyl chloride> $\mathrm{(CH_3)_2CHCOCl}$, with equivalent <methyl groups> in an achiral environment.

= Methanesulfonic anhydride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ms_2O}$}

<Methanesulfonic anhydride> is $\mathrm{(MeSO_2)_2O}$, a reagent for making mesylates from alcohols.

= Mesylate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{OMs}$}

A <mesylate> is a methanesulfonate, $\mathrm{ROSO_2Me}$. Converting an alcohol to its <mesylate> improves leaving-group ability without initially cleaving the original <carbon>–<oxygen> bond.

= Alkenyl aluminate
{parent=Chemical substance}

An <alkenyl aluminate> has an anionic aluminium-containing unit attached to an <alkene> <carbon>. Intramolecular <oxygen> coordination can close a five-membered ring in directed <propargylic alcohol> reductions.

= Red-Al
{parent=Chemical substance}
{c}
{title2=$\mathrm{NaAlH_2(OCH_2CH_2OMe)_2}$}

<Red-Al> is sodium bis(2-methoxyethoxy)aluminium hydride. Suitable <propargylic alcohols> undergo <oxygen>-directed trans <hydroalumination> to cyclic <alkenyl aluminate> species; aqueous workup gives E allylic alcohols.

= Osmium tetroxide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{OsO_4}$}

<Osmium tetroxide> adds to an <alkene> to form a <cyclic osmate ester>. Hydrolysis releases a vicinal diol; regeneration of Os(VIII) requires a reoxidant in a catalytic cycle.

= Metachloroperbenzoic acid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$m\text{-}\mathrm{ClC_6H_4C(O)OOH}$}

= mCPBA
{synonym}
{c}

<Metachloroperbenzoic acid> is a peroxycarboxylic acid used for concerted <oxygen> transfer to <alkenes>. A neighboring allylic hydroxyl can direct its approach through <hydrogen bond>[hydrogen bonding].

= Decalin
{parent=Chemical substance}

<Decalin> contains two fused six-membered <carbon> rings. Trans fusion permits a relatively rigid double-chair geometry, while cis fusion admits interconverting double-chair conformers.

= Allenyl phosphine oxide
{parent=Chemical substance}

An <allenyl phosphine oxide> contains an <allene> attached to a phosphine-oxide group. Attack at the central <allene> <carbon> can generate a carbanion on the adjacent <phosphorus>-bearing <carbon>, which is stabilized by the phosphoryl substituent.

= Nitrobenzene
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{PhNO_2}$}

<Nitrobenzene> is benzene bearing a <nitro group>. The <nitro group> withdraws <electron> density and stabilizes certain anionic aromatic addition intermediates.

= Triflic anhydride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{Tf_2O}$}

<Triflic anhydride> is $\mathrm{(CF_3SO_2)_2O}$, an electrophilic sulfonylating reagent. <carbonyl> <oxygen> attack can form an O-triflylated intermediate; adjacent <nitrogen> donation gives an imidoyl-<triflate> description.

= Carbamate
{parent=Chemical substance}

A <carbamate> contains the unit $\mathrm{O{-}C(O){-}N}$. The <nitrogen> <lone pair> conjugates with the <carbonyl>; cyclic carbamates include <2-oxazolidinone>.

= Ammonium carbamate chain
{parent=Carbamate}

A chain arrangement implicated in cooperative carbon-dioxide capture in appropriate diamine-appended metal–organic frameworks. Framework spacing and proton transfer determine the actual adsorption mechanism.<Cooperative insertion of CO2 in diamine-appended metal-organic frameworks by Thomas M. McDonald and others>[Adsorption mechanism]{ref}

= 2-Oxazolidinone
{parent=Carbamate}

<2-Oxazolidinone> is the five-membered cyclic <carbamate> with ring path $\mathrm{O{-}C(O){-}NH{-}CH_2{-}CH_2}$. Electrophilic activation of its <carbonyl> <oxygen> can generate an imidoyl-type <electrophile>.

= Chloroformate
{parent=Chemical substance}

A <chloroformate> has the connectivity $\mathrm{ClC(O)OR}$. Nucleophilic addition at its <carbonyl> followed by <chloride> elimination makes a carbonate or <carbamate>.

= Allyl chloroformate
{parent=Chloroformate}

<Allyl chloroformate> is $\mathrm{ClC(O)OCH_2CH{=}CH_2}$. <oxygen> acylation of an <enolate> makes an allyl <enol carbonate>.

= Dimethyl sulfoxide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{Me_2SO}$}

= DMSO
{synonym}
{c}

<Dimethyl sulfoxide> is a polar aprotic solvent. It supports reactions of anions with many organic <electrophiles> without providing an O–H <proton>.

= Hexamethylphosphoramide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{OP(NMe_2)_3}$}

= HMPA
{synonym}
{c}

<Hexamethylphosphoramide> has a strongly donating phosphoryl <oxygen> that coordinates <lithium>. It changes <ion pair>[ion pairing] and aggregate structures, often increasing the reactivity of anionic <oxygen> donors. It does not universally produce free monomeric anions.

= Organolithium reagent
{parent=Chemical substance}

An <organolithium reagent> contains a <carbon>–<lithium> interaction with substantial carbanionic <carbon> reactivity. Such reagents can act as bases or <nucleophiles>, and their solution aggregation affects both roles.

= Lithiated enamide
{parent=Organolithium reagent}

= Lithiated enamine
{synonym}

The lithium salt of an N-deprotonated <enamine>, stabilized by conjugation and often by aggregation.

= n-Butyllithium
{parent=Organolithium reagent}

An organolithium reagent used as a strong base and carbon nucleophile.

= Organolithium aggregation
{parent=Organolithium reagent}

<Organolithium aggregation> associates multiple organolithium or related <lithium>-anion units, often through bridging donor atoms. Strong donor solvents can change aggregate structure without necessarily making only monomers.

= Tetrahydrofuran
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{THF}$}

= THF
{synonym}
{c}

<Tetrahydrofuran> is a cyclic <ether> solvent. Its <oxygen> can coordinate <lithium> ions and alter the aggregation of <organolithium reagents>.

= Lithium diisopropylamide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{LiN(iPr)_2}$}

= LDA
{synonym}
{c}
{disambiguate=chemistry}

<Lithium diisopropylamide> is a strong, sterically hindered base used to generate <enolates>. Its low <nucleophilicity> toward many <carbonyl> substrates helps separate <deprotonation> from <carbonyl> addition.

= Porphyrin
{parent=Chemical substance}

= 2,4-Diazadiboretidine
{parent=Chemical substance}

A <2,4-diazadiboretidine> contains an alternating four-membered B–N–B–N ring. <nitrogen> <lone pairs> provide sigma-donor sites, while the ring can bind an <electron>-rich <metal> through a pi interaction involving both B and N.

= Borazine
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{B_3N_3H_6}$}

<Borazine> is the alternating six-membered $\mathrm{B_3N_3H_6}$ ring. Planar boron and <nitrogen> centers permit N-to-B pi donation, but unequal orbital energies strongly polarize the bonding. It undergoes addition and <hydrolysis> much more readily than <benzene>; the structural analogy does not give <benzene>-like chemical inertness.

= Borazine dearomatization
{parent=Borazine}

<Borazine dearomatization> interrupts the planar pi system, for example when a <nucleophile> makes one boron tetrahedral. In a <metal> pi complex, this can change ring <hapticity> and produce a charged ligand with a different donor count.

= B-Tribromoborazine
{parent=Borazine}
{c}
{title2=$\mathrm{B_3Br_3N_3H_3}$}

<B-Tribromoborazine> is $\mathrm{B_3Br_3N_3H_3}$, retaining all three N–H groups. Ring-retaining bromination replaces B–H groups, whereas simple hydrogen-halide addition also changes ring saturation.

= Hexamethylborazine
{parent=Borazine}
{title2=$\mathrm{B_3N_3Me_6}$}

<Hexamethylborazine> is $\mathrm{B_3N_3Me_6}$, with one methyl on every ring atom. Its <nitrogen> atoms can form <Lewis acid-base adducts>, while <nucleophiles> can attack its boron atoms.

= Triphenyl phosphite
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{P(OPh)_3}$}

<Triphenyl phosphite> is $\mathrm{P(OPh)_3}$, a neutral <phosphorus> donor used as a <phosphite ligand>.

= Trifluorophosphine
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{PF_3}$}

<Trifluorophosphine>, $\mathrm{PF_3}$, binds through <phosphorus> as a neutral two-<electron> donor and is an effective <pi-acceptor ligand>.

= Methyllithium
{parent=Chemical substance}

$\mathrm{CH_3Li}$, an organolithium reagent supplying a strongly nucleophilic/basic methyl equivalent.

= Grignard reagent
{parent=Chemical substance}

An organomagnesium reagent commonly written $\mathrm{RMgX}$, with organic group R and halide X. Its carbon group can act as a <nucleophile>; actual solution structures depend on solvent and aggregation.

= Vinylmagnesium halide
{parent=Grignard reagent}

A <grignard reagent> in which <magnesium> is attached directly to an alkenyl <carbon>, $\mathrm{RCH{=}CHMgX}$. Addition to a <carbonyl compound> can form an allylic <alcohol> while retaining the geometry of the pre-existing <alkene>.

= Cyclohexylmagnesium bromide
{parent=Grignard reagent}

$\mathrm{C_6H_{11}MgBr}$, a cyclohexyl-transfer <grignard reagent>.

= Butylmagnesium bromide
{parent=Grignard reagent}

The straight-chain four-carbon <grignard reagent> $\mathrm{CH_3(CH_2)_3MgBr}$ when the n-butyl form is specified.

= Methylmagnesium bromide
{parent=Grignard reagent}

$\mathrm{CH_3MgBr}$, a methyl-transfer <grignard reagent> in the usual organometallic accounting.

= Phenylmagnesium bromide
{parent=Grignard reagent}

$\mathrm{C_6H_5MgBr}$, a phenyl-transfer reagent that can open an <epoxide> by attack at carbon.

= Methylmagnesium iodide
{parent=Grignard reagent}

$\mathrm{CH_3MgI}$, a methyl-transfer reagent in carbonyl addition.

= Carbonic acid
{parent=Chemical substance}

$\mathrm{HO-C(=O)-OH}$, formed by hydration of <carbon dioxide>. Molecular acid and dissolved carbon dioxide are distinct species in aqueous equilibrium.

= Alkyne
{parent=Chemical substance}

A compound containing a carbon–carbon triple bond. A terminal alkyne has a hydrogen directly attached to one of its sp carbons.

= Acetylide
{parent=Alkyne}

An anion formed by removing the terminal proton of an <alkyne>, $\mathrm{RC{\equiv}C^-}$. It can act as a <carbon> <nucleophile>, for example in addition to a <ketone>.

= Hexa-1,5-diyne
{parent=Alkyne}

The <hydrocarbon> $\mathrm{HC\equiv C-CH_2-CH_2-C\equiv CH}$. Its triple-bond fragments are linear, but the entire <carbon> skeleton need not be. The extended anti conformation has an <inversion centre> at the central bond midpoint and belongs to the <C2h molecular point group>.

= Benzyne
{parent=Alkyne}

The simplest aryne, $\mathrm{C_6H_4}$. Its extra in-plane bonding interaction is distinct from the out-of-plane aromatic pi system.

= Propyne
{parent=Alkyne}

$\mathrm{CH_3-C\equiv CH}$, a terminal <alkyne>.

= Lactose
{parent=Chemical substance}

A disaccharide composed of glucose and galactose. Its uptake and conversion to <allolactose> connect substrate availability to <lac operon> regulation.

= Imatinib
{parent=Chemical substance}

A small-molecule inhibitor of ABL-family and certain other tyrosine kinases. Its inhibition of BCR-ABL signalling illustrates treatment targeted to a deregulated cancer driver, with resistance and off-target effects requiring consideration.

= Haem
{parent=Chemical substance}

An iron-containing porphyrin prosthetic group. Protein ligands and the surrounding pocket tune its ligand-binding and redox properties.

= Allolactose
{parent=Chemical substance}

A lactose-derived disaccharide that induces the <lac operon> by binding <LacI>. It links uptake and metabolism of the substrate to transcriptional control.

= Peroxide
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Benzoyl peroxide
{parent=Peroxide}
{title2=$\mathrm{PhC(O)OOC(O)Ph}$}

= BPO
{c}
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{synonym}

An organic peroxide that can initiate <radical polymerization> by O–O bond homolysis. Benzoyloxy radicals, and radicals formed by their further reaction, can initiate monomer addition.

= Cyclopentane
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Sulfonamide
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Carbinolamine
{parent=Chemical substance}

A carbinolamine has an <amino group> and a <hydroxy group> on the same <carbon>. It can occur between <carbonyl group> addition and <iminium ion> formation.

= Vitamin A
{parent=Chemical substance}

A group of nutritionally important retinol-related compounds with developmental and physiological roles. Its experimental effects depend on chemical form, dose and tissue context.

= Retinoid
{parent=Chemical substance}

A member of the vitamin-A-related compound class. Experimental retinoid treatment can alter developmental identity, but that does not by itself establish the complete endogenous patterning mechanism.

= 4-nitroquinoline 1-oxide
{parent=Chemical substance}

A chemical carcinogen used in experimental models of oesophageal carcinogenesis. Differences in detoxification and DNA damage can influence which epithelial populations persist during exposure.

= Nitric acid
{parent=Chemical substance}

$\mathrm{HNO_3}$, treated as a strongly dissociated acid in dilute aqueous solution.

= Deuterated chloroform
{parent=Chemical substance}

$\mathrm{CDCl_3}$, a common <NMR> <solvent>. Its carbon resonance is near $77$ ppm under ordinary conditions.

= Acetal
{parent=Chemical substance}

A structure with two alkoxy groups attached to the same saturated carbon. <Acetal hydrolysis> restores a carbonyl compound.

= 1,3,5-Trioxane
{parent=Acetal}

The cyclic trimer of <formaldehyde>, $\mathrm{(CH_2O)_3}$. Acid-catalysed acetal cleavage can regenerate <formaldehyde>, which is strongly hydrated in water.

= Ketal
{parent=Acetal}

An <acetal> derived formally from a <ketone>, with two <carbon> substituents and two <oxygen> substituents at its <acetal> <carbon>.

= Benzylidene acetal
{parent=Acetal}

An <acetal> formed with <benzaldehyde>, containing a Ph–CH unit attached to two oxygens. It can protect a diol and is acid-labile.

= Thioglycoside
{parent=Acetal}

A cyclic sugar-derived acetal analogue with an anomeric carbon–sulfur bond. Its anomeric configuration and conformational preference must be distinguished from one another.

= 2,2-Dimethoxypropane
{parent=Acetal}
{title2=$\mathrm{Me_2C(OMe)_2}$}

<2,2-Dimethoxypropane> is the dimethyl acetal of <acetone>. Reaction with a diol under acid catalysis can form an <acetonide> and two <methanol> molecules.

= Acetonide
{parent=Acetal}

An <acetonide> is an isopropylidene acetal formed from a diol and <acetone> or an <acetone> equivalent. Formation of its O–CMe2 bonds leaves the original stereogenic <carbon>–<oxygen> bonds intact.

= Spiroacetal
{parent=Acetal}

= Lithium aluminium hydride
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Lithium aluminum hydride
{synonym}

$\mathrm{LiAlH_4}$, a strong <hydride> donor capable of reducing <ketones> to <alcohols> and <amides> to <amines>, with oxygen removal in the latter transformation.

= Sodium borohydride
{parent=Chemical substance}

$\mathrm{NaBH_4}$, a <hydride> donor that commonly reduces <aldehydes> and <ketones> while leaving ordinary <amides> unchanged under mild conditions.

= Evans–Saksena reduction
{parent=Sodium borohydride}
{c}

A hydroxyl-directed reduction of a β-hydroxy <ketone> with an acetoxyborohydride reagent, favouring an anti-1,3-diol through intramolecular hydride delivery.

= Hydride
{parent=Chemical substance}

The hydrogen anion $\mathrm H^-$, or a hydride equivalent transferred from a reagent with its bonding electron pair. Bonded hydride transfer does not imply freely solvated $\mathrm H^-$ is present.

= Bridging hydride
{parent=Hydride}

A <hydrogen> ligand bonded to more than one skeletal centre. A B–H–B bridge in a <borane> is commonly represented as a <three-centre two-electron bond>; it is not two independent ordinary B–H single bonds.

= Pyridine
{parent=Chemical substance}

$\mathrm{C_5H_5N}$, an aromatic nitrogen heterocycle whose nitrogen <lone pair> can accept a proton.

= Ether
{parent=Chemical substance}

A compound containing an $\mathrm{R-O-R^\prime}$ linkage between two carbon groups.

= Enol ether
{parent=Ether}

An <ether> with <oxygen> directly attached to an <alkene> <carbon>. Electrophilic activation and <hydrolysis> can convert an appropriate <enol ether> back to a <carbonyl compound>.

= Enol ether hydrolysis
{parent=Enol ether}

Acid-catalyzed conversion of an <enol ether> into a <carbonyl> compound and an <alcohol>.

= Tetrahydropyranyl ether
{parent=Ether}

= THP ether
{synonym}
{c}

An <alcohol> protecting group that is often stable to strong bases and organometallic reagents but can be removed under mild acid conditions.

= Vinyl ether
{parent=Ether}

An <ether> with <oxygen> directly attached to a vinylic <carbon>, $\mathrm{R-O-CH=CH_2}$ in the simplest terminal example. <oxygen> <resonance donation> strongly differentiates the two alkene <carbon> shifts.

= 2-Chloroethyl vinyl ether
{parent=Vinyl ether}

The <vinyl ether> $\mathrm{CH_2=CH-O-CH_2-CH_2Cl}$. Its terminal alkene hydrogens are <diastereotopic>.

= Silyl ether
{parent=Ether}

A <silyl ether> contains an O–Si bond, commonly protecting an alcohol or <phenol>. Cleavage at electrophilic <silicon> releases an <oxygen>-centered anion under suitable conditions.

= Epoxide
{parent=Ether}

= Ethylene oxide
{parent=Epoxide}

= Oxirane
{synonym}

The simplest <epoxide>, a three-membered $\mathrm{C_2H_4O}$ ring. Opening by a <carbon> nucleophile, followed by protonation, adds a two-carbon primary-alcohol unit.

= Epoxide ring opening
{parent=Epoxide}

Nucleophilic cleavage of one <epoxide> C–O bond, with inversion at the attacked <carbon> under an SN2-like mechanism.

= Peracid epoxidation
{parent=Epoxide}

Concerted oxygen transfer from a peroxycarboxylic acid to an <alkene>. <Hydrogen bonding> to a nearby <alcohol> can influence reaction site and facial selectivity.

= Cyclohexene oxide
{parent=Epoxide}

The <epoxide> across adjacent carbons of a cyclohexane ring. Backside nucleophilic opening produces an anti relationship between the introduced group and retained <oxygen>.

= 2,2-Dimethyloxirane
{parent=Epoxide}

An unsymmetrical <epoxide> with two methyl substituents at one ring <carbon>. Acidic opening can favour nucleophile attachment to that more substituted <carbon>.

= Glycal epoxide
{parent=Epoxide}

A 1,2-anhydro sugar derivative obtained by epoxidizing a glycal. Its anomeric carbon can undergo direct nucleophilic opening or ionization assisted by the ring oxygen.

= Ethyl triphenylmethyl ether
{parent=Ether}

$\mathrm{Ph_3C-O-CH_2CH_3}$, an <ether> formed by trapping a triphenylmethyl <carbocation> with <ethanol>, followed by deprotonation.

= Hexanenitrile
{parent=Chemical substance}

$\mathrm{CH_3(CH_2)_4C\equiv N}$. The nitrile carbon is included in the six-carbon count.

= Cyanide
{parent=Chemical substance}

$\mathrm{CN^-}$. Its carbon end can act as a <nucleophile> toward an alkyl leaving group to form a nitrile with one additional carbon.

= Methyldiazonium
{parent=Chemical substance}

$\mathrm{CH_3N_2^+}$, the carbon-protonated <conjugate acid> of <diazomethane>, commonly represented as $\mathrm{H_3C-N^+\equiv N}$.

= Diazomethane
{parent=Chemical substance}

$\mathrm{CH_2N_2}$, with a linear C–N–N skeleton and a planar carbon environment. Important <resonance contributors> are $\mathrm{H_2C=N^+=N^-}$ and $\mathrm{H_2C^--N^+\equiv N}$.

= Alkene
{parent=Chemical substance}

A compound containing a carbon–carbon <double bond>.

= Nitroethylene
{parent=Alkene}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_2{=}CHNO_2}$}

$\mathrm{CH_2{=}CHNO_2}$, an electron-poor <alkene> that can serve as a dienophile. Conversion of a resulting secondary nitro group to a <ketone> can connect a Diels–Alder reaction to a <Nef reaction>.

= Norbornene
{parent=Alkene}

Bicyclo\[2.2.1\]hept-2-ene, a strained bicyclic alkene.

= Diene
{parent=Alkene}

A compound containing two <carbon>–<carbon> double bonds; conjugation is a separate structural qualification.

= Cyclopentadiene
{parent=Diene}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_5H_6}$}

A five-carbon cyclic conjugated diene. Cycloaddition across its two <double bonds> produces a norbornene framework while retaining the original five-membered <carbon> ring.

= Buta-1,3-diene
{parent=Diene}

= 1,3-Butadiene
{synonym}

$\mathrm{CH_2=CHCH=CH_2}$.

= Ketene dithioacetal
{parent=Alkene}

An <alkene> bearing two thioalkoxy groups on one <carbon>, $\mathrm{R_2C=C(SR\prime)_2}$. <pi conjugation> to a <carbonyl> can allow addition–elimination at that beta <carbon> with <thiolate> departure.

= Cyclohexene
{parent=Alkene}

A six-membered carbocycle with one <carbon>–<carbon> <double bond>.

= Cyclohexene half-chair
{parent=Cyclohexene}

A puckered <conformation> with an approximately planar <alkene> end and a nonplanar saturated part. Bulky substituents commonly favor pseudoequatorial positions.

= Terminal alkene
{parent=Alkene}

= Styrene
{parent=Alkene}

= Nitroalkene
{parent=Alkene}

An alkene bearing a nitro group can undergo conjugate addition. Addition at its beta carbon generates a nitronate that can subsequently react at carbon.

= Carbocation
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Tertiary carbocation
{parent=Carbocation}

A <carbocation> whose positively charged <carbon> is bonded to three <carbon> substituents.

= Secondary carbocation
{parent=Carbocation}

A <carbocation> whose positively charged <carbon> is bonded to two <carbon> substituents.

= 1,2-hydride shift
{parent=Carbocation}

Migration of a neighboring C–H bonding pair onto an electron-deficient <carbon>, moving the <carbocation> site.

= Oxocarbenium ion
{parent=Carbocation}
{wiki}

= Oxocarbenium
{synonym}

An <oxocarbenium ion> is a cationic species stabilized by adjacent oxygen donation, commonly represented by carbon-cation and oxonium resonance contributors. Reversible acetal opening can expose a locally planar carbon for attack from either face.

= Glycosyloxocarbenium ion
{parent=Oxocarbenium ion}

An <oxocarbenium ion> at the <anomeric carbon> of a cyclic glycosyl fragment. Positive charge is delocalized between that <carbon> and the ring <oxygen>. A counterion can remain nearby and influence which face a <nucleophile> approaches.

= Enolate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

An <enolate> is the conjugate base formed by removing an alpha proton from a suitable carbonyl compound. Oxygen-centred and carbon-centred resonance contributors help explain its ability to react at either site.

= Vinylogous enolate
{parent=Enolate}

An <enolate> whose nucleophilic <carbon> is conjugated to a <carbonyl> through an additional unsaturated unit.

= Ester enolate
{parent=Enolate}

An <enolate> derived by deprotonation alpha to an <ester> <carbonyl>.

= Thermodynamic enolate
{parent=Enolate}

An <enolate> favoured after equilibration under the stated conditions. It is often more substituted, but aggregation, solvent and counterion can change the preference.

= Kinetic enolate
{parent=Enolate}

An <enolate> preferentially formed under conditions where deprotonation site selection is not equilibrated, often at a less hindered alpha <carbon> with a bulky strong base.

= Enolate regioselectivity
{parent=Enolate}

Preference for forming or reacting one <enolate> of a <carbonyl> compound with more than one alpha site. A less substituted <kinetic enolate> and an equilibrated more substituted <enolate> can give different <carbon> skeletons.

= Enolate acylation
{parent=Enolate}

Reaction of an <enolate> with an acyl <electrophile>. <carbon> acylation of a <ketone> <enolate> gives a <1,3-diketone>; O-acylation gives a different connectivity.

= Carboxylic acid dianion
{parent=Enolate}

A carboxylic acid treated with sufficient strong base can lose both its acidic O–H proton and an alpha C–H proton. Carbon alkylation of the resulting dianion followed by protonation yields an alpha-substituted acid.

= Enolate alkylation
{parent=Enolate}

<Enolate alkylation> forms a <carbon>–<carbon> bond by attack of an <enolate> <carbon> on an alkyl <electrophile>, commonly through an <SN2 reaction>. Solvent, <counterion>, aggregation and <electrophile> identity affect competition with <oxygen> alkylation.

= Reductive alpha-alkylation of an enone
{parent=Enolate alkylation}

Conjugate reduction followed by carbon-electrophile trapping of the resulting <enolate>. The original enone alpha position determines the alkylation site before any later equilibration.

= O-acylation of an enolate
{parent=Enolate}

Oxygen-centred attack of an <enolate> on an acylating reagent forms an enol ester or carbonate. Carbon acylation gives a different connectivity, so the ambident nucleophile's two resonance representations should not be confused with two equivalent products.

= Iminium
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Iminium ion
{synonym}

= Enamine
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

An <enamine> contains an amino nitrogen bonded to an alkene carbon. Delocalization between the nitrogen lone pair and the alkene makes its carbon nucleophilic; carbon alkylation can give an <iminium ion> that hydrolyses to a carbonyl compound.

= Enamine regioisomerization
{parent=Enamine}

An iminium intermediate can lose a proton at either adjacent carbon, generating different enamine double-bond positions. Reaction selectivity can depend on which regioisomer is populated.

= Gallium trichloride
{parent=Chemical substance}

= GaCl3
{c}
{synonym}

= Chloroacetyl chloride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Imidazole
{parent=Chemical substance}

A nitrogen-containing heterocyclic compound. It competes with histidine side-chain contacts for metal binding in <immobilized metal affinity chromatography>.

= Beta-lactam
{parent=Chemical substance}

A four-membered cyclic <amide>. Hydrolytic opening of this ring is a major mechanism by which <beta-lactamases> inactivate susceptible antibiotics.

= Acetate
{parent=Chemical substance}

The conjugate base of <acetic acid>.

= Phosphocreatine
{parent=Chemical substance}

<Phosphocreatine> buffers ATP supply through reversible phosphate transfer catalysed by creatine kinase. Its depletion and recovery during muscle exercise can be followed using phosphorus NMR spectroscopy.

= Fructose-1-phosphate
{parent=Chemical substance}

<Fructose-1-phosphate> is a product of fructose phosphorylation. It can antagonize the inhibitory glucokinase–regulatory-protein interaction, linking hepatic glucose handling to fructose metabolism.

= Fructose-6-phosphate
{parent=Chemical substance}

<Fructose-6-phosphate> is a phosphorylated sugar involved in glycolysis and metabolic regulation. In liver glucose sensing it favours the inhibitory glucokinase–regulatory-protein interaction.

= Glucose-6-phosphate
{parent=Chemical substance}

<Glucose-6-phosphate> is glucose phosphorylated at carbon 6. It links glucose trapping to pathways including glycolysis and glycogen synthesis; accumulation of this product and the incoming phosphorylation flux are different quantities.

= Dimethylformamide
{parent=Chemical substance}

<Dimethylformamide> is a polar organic solvent, abbreviated DMF. Enzyme performance in concentrated DMF can depend on folding, stability and catalytic chemistry, making it useful for separating several goals in protein engineering.

= Biotin
{parent=Chemical substance}

<biotin> is a small biological <molecule> used as a covalent tag in several <protein> labeling methods. Strong capture of biotinylated material enables enrichment before <mass spectrometry>.

= Samarium hexaboride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{SmB}_6$}
{wiki}

<Samarium hexaboride> is a <Kondo insulator> whose narrow samarium $4f$-derived states mix with more dispersive conduction states. It motivates effective models of <band hybridization> between a nearly <flat band> and a light dispersive band.

= Gallium nitride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{GaN}$}

= GaN
{c}
{synonym}

= Indium gallium nitride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{In}_x\mathrm{Ga}_{1-x}\mathrm N$}

= InGaN
{c}
{synonym}

= Aluminium nitride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{AlN}$}

= Aluminium gallium nitride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Al}_x\mathrm{Ga}_{1-x}\mathrm N$}

= Manganese(II) oxide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{MnO}$}

= Corundum
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Al}_2\mathrm O_3$}

= Sapphire
{parent=Corundum}
{wiki}

= Ruby
{parent=Corundum}
{wiki}

= Cerium magnesium nitrate
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Anthracene
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_{14}\mathrm{H}_{10}$}

= Diethyl ether
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_4\mathrm{H}_{10}\mathrm O$}

= Gelatin
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Resin
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Gamboge
{parent=Resin}
{wiki}

= Metal
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Tantalum
{parent=Metal}

= Palladium
{parent=Metal}

= Transition metal
{parent=Metal}

A <transition metal> has an incompletely filled d subshell in its atom or a relevant cation. Its <metal>–ligand orbital interactions support varied <oxidation states>, magnetic states and coordination geometries.

= Rhenium
{parent=Transition metal}
{title2=$\mathrm{Re}$}

The group-7 <transition metal> with atomic number 75.

= Alloy
{parent=Metal}
{wiki}

A material combining a <metal> with one or more other <chemical elements>, while retaining metallic properties.

= Superalloy
{parent=Alloy}

An alloy developed to sustain mechanical load at high <temperatures> while resisting <creep (deformation)> and environmental attack.

= Nickel-based superalloy
{parent=Superalloy}

A high-temperature alloy based on a nickel-rich disordered $\gamma$ matrix, commonly strengthened by ordered $\gamma^{\prime}$ precipitates and substitutional solutes.

= Single-crystal turbine blade
{parent=Nickel-based superalloy}

A turbine blade cast as one crystal to remove high-temperature <grain boundary> sliding and <grain-boundary cavitation> pathways. This does not eliminate <dislocations> or all sources of <creep (deformation)>.

= Gamma prime phase
{parent=Nickel-based superalloy}
{title2=$\gamma^{\prime}$}

The ordered $L1_2$ phase based on $\mathrm{Ni_3Al}$ in many <nickel-based superalloys>. A matrix <translation> $a\langle110\rangle/2$ disrupts its order and can create an <antiphase boundary>.

= Zirconium alloy
{parent=Alloy}

An alloy based on <zirconium>. Low <neutron> absorption and <corrosion> behaviour motivate <nuclear fuel cladding> applications; <irradiation growth>, <creep (deformation)> and hydrogen uptake remain constraints.

= Zircaloy
{parent=Zirconium alloy}
{c}

A family of zirconium-based alloys developed for nuclear applications. Specific alloy and heat treatment determine <corrosion>, hydrogen uptake and irradiation response.

= Surface segregation
{parent=Alloy}

Enrichment of one component at a material surface relative to its bulk composition. Surface free energies, adsorbates, elastic <strain> and the energetic cost of changing bulk bonding jointly determine equilibrium enrichment.

= Chemical ordering in alloys
{parent=Alloy}

Preferential occupation of distinct crystal sublattices by different chemical elements. Favourable unlike-neighbour bonding can stabilize a periodic <alloy> arrangement despite its lower <configurational entropy> than a random <alloy>.

= Precipitation hardening
{parent=Alloy}

Heat treatment that develops a fine dispersion of a new <solid> phase in an <alloy>, impeding <dislocation> motion. It is distinct from precipitation from a liquid <chemical solution>. A <solid solution> may first be formed at high temperature, quenched to retain supersaturation, and then aged to promote solid-state precipitation.

= Precipitate diameter from fraction and spacing
{parent=Precipitation hardening}
{title2=$d=L(6f/\pi)^{1/3}$}

For one spherical particle per representative volume $L^3$, a <volume fraction> $f$ implies $d=L(6f/\pi)^{1/3}$. A mean nearest-neighbor distance in a random population does not determine this <number density> exactly, and an atomic <thermodynamic phase> fraction requires a molar-volume conversion before it is used as $f$.

= Orowan bypass
{parent=Precipitation hardening}
{c}
{title2=$\Delta\tau\simeq2T_\ell/(b\ell)$}

A <dislocation> unable to shear an obstacle can bow around it and leave a loop. Balancing the Peach-Koehler force against the tension of a curved <dislocation> gives the critical scale $\Delta\tau\simeq2T_\ell/(b\ell)$, where $\ell$ is the free obstacle span on the slip plane. Centre pitch and free span are not interchangeable when particle size is appreciable.

= Tensile conversion of an obstacle shear stress
{parent=Orowan bypass}
{title2=$\Delta\sigma=M\Delta\tau$}

An obstacle calculation produces a resolved shear-stress increment. A single crystal needs its <Schmid factor>, while a polycrystal needs a deformation-geometry or <Taylor factor>. The relation $\Delta\sigma=M\Delta\tau$ therefore requires an explicit orientation assumption rather than a universal tensile prefactor.

= Order strengthening
{parent=Precipitation hardening}

Resistance to <dislocation> motion caused by the energetic cost of disrupting chemical order, for example creating an <antiphase boundary> in a <gamma prime phase> precipitate.

= Solid-state precipitate
{parent=Precipitation hardening}

A new solid phase formed within an existing solid matrix. Its morphology reflects <anisotropic> <interfacial energy>, <coherency strain> and growth kinetics.

= Precipitate coarsening
{parent=Solid-state precipitate}

Evolution toward larger characteristic precipitate sizes, often by diffusional growth of larger particles at the expense of smaller ones. The associated changes of spacing and shape can alter strengthening.

= Precipitate morphology
{parent=Solid-state precipitate}

The shape of a <solid-state precipitate>. Thin plates can result when broad faces have much lower <interfacial energy> than edge regions.

= Aluminium scandium alloy
{parent=Alloy}

= Bronze
{parent=Alloy}
{wiki}

= Manganin
{parent=Alloy}
{wiki}

= Steel
{parent=Alloy}
{wiki}

= Austenitic steel
{parent=Steel}

A <steel> with an austenitic, <face-centered cubic> matrix in the specified condition. Alloying and <temperature> determine its stability.

= Austenitic stainless steel
{parent=Austenitic steel}

A <stainless steel> with an austenitic matrix. Some grades are used in nuclear components, where <void swelling> and <helium embrittlement> can limit performance.

= AISI 316 stainless steel
{parent=Austenitic stainless steel}
{c}

A chromium–nickel–molybdenum stainless-steel grade. Annealed and cold-worked conditions can have different irradiation <microstructures>.

= Stainless steel
{parent=Steel}

A chromium-bearing <steel> whose protective surface film can confer <corrosion> resistance. Composition, <microstructure> and environment determine whether that protection persists.

= Steel microstructure
{parent=Steel}

= Nital
{parent=Steel microstructure}

A nitric-acid/alcohol metallographic etchant. In pearlitic steel it can preferentially remove <ferrite>, leaving <cementite> standing higher. In an <atomic force microscopy> height image, darker then means lower surface height, not an optical reflectivity or atomic-number contrast.

= Tempering
{disambiguate=metallurgy}
{parent=Steel microstructure}

= Steel tempering
{synonym}

Heating quenched steel below the austenitizing range to adjust its microstructure and relieve stresses. Carbon redistribution and carbide formation can improve <toughness> at a cost in <hardness>.

= Tempered martensite
{parent=Tempering (metallurgy)}

A microstructure in which <carbon> redistribution and carbide formation reduce the <supersaturation> of a martensitic matrix. Plate or lath morphology can remain while local <strain> and <thermodynamic phase> composition evolve. The term describes a material state, not a universal guarantee of a particular toughness.

= Isothermal transformation diagram
{parent=Steel microstructure}

= Time-temperature-transformation diagram
{synonym}

= TTT diagram
{c}
{synonym}

A chart of the time needed to reach specified transformation fractions after cooling to, and holding at, a fixed temperature. Its curves are not themselves continuous-cooling histories; estimating a critical cooling rate from their nose is an approximation.

= Martensite
{parent=Steel microstructure}
{wiki}

A product of a <displacive transformation>. In carbon steel, rapid cooling can trap <carbon> in a distorted iron lattice, producing high <hardness> and low as-quenched <toughness>.

= Pearlite
{parent=Steel microstructure}
{wiki}

A lamellar two-phase microconstituent of <ferrite> and <cementite>, rather than a single thermodynamic phase. Shorter diffusion distances at greater undercooling generally produce finer lamellae before diffusional transformation is bypassed.

= Pearlite phase fraction
{parent=Pearlite}

The proportions of <ferrite> and <cementite> within the two-phase <pearlite> microstructure. A <lever rule> on a weight-percent-carbon diagram gives mass fractions; converting those to volume fractions requires the phase densities. Proeutectoid phases outside <pearlite> must be treated separately.

= Cementite
{parent=Steel microstructure}
{title2=$\mathrm{Fe_3C}$}

= Austenite
{parent=Steel microstructure}

The face-centred cubic iron-rich solid phase of steel. Its transformation products depend on composition and cooling history.

= Ferrite
{parent=Steel microstructure}

The body-centred cubic iron-rich solid phase of ordinary low-temperature steel, with low equilibrium <carbon> solubility.

= Invar
{c}
{parent=Alloy}
{wiki}

A nickel–iron <alloy> with unusually small <thermal expansion> over a limited <temperature> range. Composition and treatment matter; its name does not mean every specimen has zero expansion. The <Guillaume thermal expansion experiment> explains how the effect was measured.<Nobel Lectures, Physics 1901–1921/Invar and Elinvar by Charles-Édouard Guillaume>[Nobel Lectures, Physics 1901–1921; Invar and elinvar, lecture 11 December 1920, pp. 451–453: expansion minimum and dependence on composition and treatment]{ref}

= Paraffin wax
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Copper(II) sulfate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CuSO_4}$}

In the <catalysed peroxide comparison>, <copper(II) sulfate> supplies dissolved <ions> before addition of <sodium hydroxide> forms a solid by <precipitation (chemistry)>. The comparison tests that solid-forming treatment rather than assuming the dissolved salt is itself an effective <catalyst>.

= Amine
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$R\mathrm{NH}_2,\ R_2\mathrm{NH},\ R_3\mathrm N$}

A simple neutral <amine> has <nitrogen> bonded to one, two, or three groups attached through <carbon>, with <hydrogen> filling the remaining positions in the neutral three-bond form. These are the primary, secondary, and tertiary forms respectively. An <amide> has different connectivity to a <carbonyl group>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/24-1-naming-amines[McMurry: classification of amines]{ref}

= Cyclohexylamine
{parent=Amine}

A primary alkylamine, $\mathrm{C_6H_{11}NH_2}$, that binds through the nitrogen lone pair as a neutral sigma donor.

= Tertiary amine
{parent=Amine}
{title2=$\mathrm{R}_3\mathrm{N}$}

An <amine> with three organic substituents on its nitrogen atom in the ordinary neutral amine classification.

= Trimethylamine
{parent=Tertiary amine}

A tertiary amine with three methyl groups. Its nitrogen accepts hydrogen bonds but the molecule has no N–H donor.

= Secondary amine
{parent=Amine}
{title2=$\mathrm{R}_2\mathrm{NH}$}

An <amine> with two organic substituents on its nitrogen atom in the ordinary neutral amine classification.

= Primary amine
{parent=Amine}
{title2=$\mathrm{RNH}_2$}

An <amine> with one organic substituent on its nitrogen atom in the ordinary neutral amine classification.

= Methylamine
{parent=Primary amine}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3NH_2}$}

$\mathrm{CH_3NH_2}$. <reductive amination> with an <aldehyde> can make an N-methyl secondary amine.

= Isopropylamine
{parent=Primary amine}

A primary amine with both nitrogen lone-pair acceptor ability and N–H donor groups. Self-association differs from that of its constitutional isomer trimethylamine.

= Diisopropylethylamine
{parent=Amine}
{title2=$\mathrm{iPr_2NEt}$}

= DIPEA
{synonym}
{c}

<Diisopropylethylamine> is a sterically hindered tertiary <amine> base. It can remove <protons> and trap acid while competing relatively weakly as a <nucleophile>.

= 2-Picolylamine
{parent=Amine}

<2-Picolylamine> is pyridin-2-ylmethanamine, with a <pyridine> <nitrogen> and an aminomethyl <nitrogen> adjacent on the ring. It binds as an unsymmetrical neutral bidentate ligand, forming a five-membered <chelate ring>.

= Amino group
{parent=Amine}

= Ammonium group
{parent=Amine}

= Aniline
{parent=Amine}

= Dimethylaniline
{parent=Aniline}

<Dimethylaniline> here denotes N,N-<dimethylaniline>, $\mathrm{PhNMe_2}$. Its neutral amino group donates into the ring, but strong acid protonates <nitrogen> and changes the directing behavior.

= Pyrrolidine
{parent=Amine}

= Triethylamine
{parent=Amine}
{title2=$\mathrm{NEt_3}$}

<Triethylamine> is a tertiary <amine>, $\mathrm{NEt_3}$, often used to capture acid generated during substitution. Protonation gives triethylammonium.

= 4-Nitroaniline
{parent=Amine}
{wiki}

= p-Nitroaniline
{synonym}

= Triethanolamine
{parent=Amine}
{wiki}

= Amide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$R-\mathrm C(=\mathrm O)-\mathrm{NR'R''}$}

Here an <amide> means a <carbonyl group> whose <carbon> is bonded directly to <nitrogen>; each of $R$, $R'$ and $R''$ may be <hydrogen> or a group attached through <carbon>. A <peptide bond> contains this linkage. <Chemical resonance> distinguishes its <nitrogen> from that of an <amine>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/24-3-basicity-of-amines[McMurry: amide resonance]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/20-4-amines-and-amides[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 20.4: amide and peptide connectivity]{ref}

= Weinreb amide
{parent=Amide}
{c}

An N-methoxy-N-methyl <amide> that can form a chelated tetrahedral intermediate on organometallic addition. Controlled <hydrolysis> gives a <ketone> rather than immediate repeated addition.

= Enamide
{parent=Amide}

An N-acyl-substituted <enamine>. Metalated <enamines> are also called enamides in organometallic usage; a <lithiated enamide> denotes that distinct anionic form.

= Amide bond rotation
{parent=Amide}

Interconversion of amide rotamers requiring loss of favourable nitrogen–carbonyl overlap near a twisted transition region. Solvation can change the barrier relative to the starting planar amide.

= Amide resonance
{parent=Amide}

Delocalization of a nitrogen lone pair into a carbonyl group, represented by neutral and charge-separated resonance contributions. It gives the C–N linkage partial double-bond character.

= Acetamide
{parent=Amide}

= Lactam
{parent=Amide}

A cyclic <amide>. Its nitrogen–carbonyl connectivity distinguishes it from an ordinary cyclic <ketone>.

= BAPNA
{c}
{parent=Amide}

Nα-benzoyl-DL-arginine-p-nitroanilide, a synthetic <substrate> releasing <p-nitroaniline> in the <trypsin colorimetric assay>.<Enzymatic Assay of APROTININ - Sigma Quality Control Test Procedure>[Enzymatic Assay of APROTININ, p. 1: Principle and abbreviation]{ref}

= Hydrogen peroxide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}_2$}
{wiki}

= Acyl group
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}}$}

For the <carboxylic acid>-derived examples here, an <acyl group> is the group remaining after formal removal of $\mathrm{OH}$ from $\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}OH}$. It includes the <oxygen> at the end of the <fatty acid>-derived chain, so it is more than just a <hydrocarbon> region. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/class/carba.html[IUPAC class names: carboxylic acids and derivatives].

= Acetyl group
{parent=Acyl group}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3C(=O){-}}$}

= Sphingosine
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Zwitterion
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Zwitterionic
{synonym}

A <zwitterion> has opposite formal <electric charges> in different regions of a <molecule>, with zero net <electric charge>. <Phosphatidylcholine> provides a membrane-related example. This is stronger than merely having <chemical polarity> through fractional <partial atomic charges>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/Z06752[IUPAC Gold Book: zwitterions].

= Lipid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

<Lipids> are a chemically diverse family of <chemical substances>, conventionally associated with biological origin and solubility in <nonpolar> <solvents>. They include <triglycerides>, <phospholipids>, and <cholesterol>. The name does not specify one <molecular formula>, one repeating <monomer>, or one kind of <polymer>. Some <lipids> have both <hydrophilic> and <hydrophobic> regions rather than being uniformly <nonpolar>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/L03571[IUPAC Gold Book: lipids].

Structure explains the different jobs. A <triglyceride> has three <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> attached to <glycerol> and serves as an <energy> store. A typical <glycerophospholipid> instead combines two such chains with a <phosphate>-containing head, making it an <amphiphile> suited to a <lipid bilayer>. <Cholesterol> has a different structure and also contributes to <cell membrane> properties. Thus a <lipid> is a chemical building material, while a <lipid bilayer> is an organization of many <molecules>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 3.3: lipid structures and functions].

= Plant cuticular wax
{parent=Lipid}

The mixture of hydrophobic surface lipids, often including very-long-chain fatty-acid derivatives, that contributes to plant surface barriers.

= Suberin
{parent=Lipid}

A chemically heterogeneous plant barrier polymer with aliphatic and aromatic components, important in tissues such as roots and cork.

= Cutin
{parent=Lipid}

A plant extracellular polyester formed largely from oxygenated fatty-acid derivatives, contributing to the cuticular barrier.

= Sterol
{parent=Lipid}

A lipid class based on a steroid skeleton with a hydroxyl group; plant sterols contribute to membrane properties and biosynthetic regulation.

= Galactolipid
{parent=Lipid}

A lipid with a galactose-containing headgroup; galactoglycerolipids are major components of plant photosynthetic membranes.

= Digalactosyldiacylglycerol
{parent=Galactolipid}

A diacylglycerol with a digalactosyl headgroup, often abbreviated DGDG. Phosphate deprivation can increase its abundance in extraplastidial membranes.

= Monogalactosyldiacylglycerol
{parent=Galactolipid}

A diacylglycerol with one galactose headgroup, often abbreviated MGDG.

= Sphingolipid
{parent=Lipid}

= Ceramide
{parent=Sphingolipid}

= Acylcarnitine
{parent=Lipid}

= Lipid peroxidation
{parent=Lipid}

<lipid peroxidation> oxidatively damages lipids, particularly susceptible unsaturated chains. Oxidation products can alter membrane properties and chemical stability; higher unsaturation therefore creates potential tradeoffs as well as functional opportunities.

= Acyl editing
{parent=Lipid}

<acyl editing> exchanges acyl groups between membrane-lipid and acyl-CoA pathways. Its substrate selectivity can affect flux into engineered long-chain polyunsaturated lipids.

= Substrate dichotomy
{parent=Lipid}

<substrate dichotomy> in fatty-acid engineering describes incompatible carrier preferences of sequential reactions, especially lipid-linked desaturation followed by acyl-CoA-dependent elongation. Limited transfer between pools can block flux even when both catalysts are active.

= Dolichol phosphate
{parent=Lipid}

<dolichol phosphate> is a phosphorylated lipid carrier used in glycan assembly. Lipid-linked sugar intermediates permit some lumenal transfer steps that do not directly use free <nucleotide> sugars.

= Dolichol
{parent=Lipid}

<dolichol> is a long-chain isoprenoid lipid whose phosphorylated derivatives carry sugars or oligosaccharides during glycan assembly.

= Diacylglycerol
{parent=Lipid}
{wiki}

= Triglyceride
{parent=Lipid}

= Triacylglycerol
{synonym}

A <triglyceride> contains <glycerol> joined to three <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> through <ester> linkages. Compare the three chains with the two-chain, <phosphate>-headed <glycerophospholipid> illustrated below. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: fats and oils].

= Cholesterol
{parent=Lipid}

<Cholesterol> is a <lipid> with four fused rings and a small <hydrophilic> region. It helps regulate the fluidity of animal <cell membranes>; it is structurally distinct from a <phospholipid>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: cholesterol].

= Phospholipid
{parent=Lipid}
{wiki}

<Phospholipids> are <lipids> containing a <phosphate> group as part of their structure linked by <covalent bonds>. The familiar membrane-forming <phospholipids> are <amphiphilic>: a <hydrophilic> head region with <chemical polarity> coexists with a <hydrophobic>, largely <nonpolar> <hydrocarbon> region. These descriptions refer to different parts of the same <molecule>, not to two separate substances. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04559[IUPAC Gold Book: phospholipids] and https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00303[IUPAC Gold Book: amphiphilic].

A typical two-chain <glycerophospholipid> provides a useful example: a <glycerol> scaffold carries two <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> through <ester> linkages, plus a <phosphate>-containing head region. <Phosphatidylcholine> is one example. This scaffold describes an important subclass, rather than all <phospholipids>: <sphingomyelin> has a different backbone, and even <glycerophospholipids> need not all have two <acyl groups>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/lipid/lip1n2.html[IUPAC–IUB, Nomenclature of Lipids (1976), Lip-1.5 and Lip-2.1–2.6].

\Image[phospholipid-amphiphilic-schematic.png]
{title=Polar head and <hydrophobic> chains of an illustrative <glycerophospholipid>}
{description=Colored regions represent a <phosphate>-containing head, a <glycerol> scaffold, and two <fatty acid>-derived chains. The small paired-chain symbol is shorthand for this illustrative subclass. Shapes and connectors show organization, not <chemical atoms>, exact <chemical bonds>, chain lengths, or a measured <molecular geometry>. The head is not assigned a universal <electric charge>.}

Polar does not mean negatively charged overall. At near-neutral <pH>, <phosphatidylcholine> is <zwitterionic>: its <phosphate> region carries negative <electric charge> and its <choline> region positive <electric charge>, with zero net <electric charge> for the <molecule>. Counting those opposite charges gives $(-1)+(+1)=0$; it does not remove their local interactions with <water>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21726/[Lodish et al., Molecular Cell Biology: membrane lipids].

In a <lipid bilayer>, two <membrane leaflets> orient their <hydrophilic> heads toward the <water> on either side and their <hydrophobic> chains toward the shared interior. The <hydrophobic effect> helps explain this organization through the <free energy> of the <water> and <lipids> together. It is not a special repulsive <chemical bond> between chains and <water>. Which organization is favored also depends on <molecular geometry>, <temperature>, <concentration>, and the surrounding <chemical solution>; a <phospholipid> label alone does not guarantee a <lipid bilayer>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26871/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the lipid bilayer].

\Image[phospholipid-bilayer-organization.png]
{title=Two <membrane leaflets> shelter a <hydrophobic> interior between water-facing heads}
{description=An illustrative <phospholipid>-based <lipid bilayer> has <water> on both sides. Individual <phospholipids> can undergo <diffusion> within a fluid <membrane leaflet>. The heads and chains are schematic regions; spacing, arrows, and chain shapes are unscaled. This is a local cross-section, without assigning either side to a particular <biological cell> compartment.}

This organization supplies a flexible barrier for compartments. A fluid <lipid bilayer> allows <lipids> to undergo <diffusion> within each <membrane leaflet>; barrier function therefore coexists with motion of <molecules>, as in the <fluid mosaic model>. It is not completely impermeable: small <nonpolar> <molecules> such as <molecular oxygen> and <carbon dioxide> cross much more readily than <ions>. In a <cell membrane>, <ion channels> and other <membrane transport proteins> provide selective routes; the existing <membrane transport> article explains their driving forces. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26815/[Alberts et al.: principles of membrane transport].

In 1925, E. Gorter and F. Grendel extracted <lipids> from <red blood cells> and compared the <surface area> of a one-<molecule>-thick film on <water> with the estimated total <surface area> of the <red blood cells>. Their table gave ratios near two, supporting a two-layer covering; they also proposed heads facing the aqueous surroundings on both sides. The inference depends on recovering the relevant <lipids>, estimating the <red blood cell> <surface area> correctly, and comparing compatible packing in the film and the <cell membrane>. It was indirect evidence, not an image of the <lipid bilayer>. The paper itself notes possible losses during evaporation. See https://doi.org/10.1084/jem.41.4.439[Gorter and Grendel, On Bimolecular Layers of Lipoids on the Chromocytes of the Blood, Journal of Experimental Medicine 41 (1925), 439–443].

= Phosphatidylinositol
{parent=Phospholipid}

<phosphatidylinositol> is a <glycerophospholipid> with an inositol headgroup. <phosphorylation> of that headgroup produces distinct <phosphoinositides>.

= Phosphoinositide
{parent=Phospholipid}

A <phosphoinositide> is a phosphorylated derivative of <phosphatidylinositol>. Phosphate position changes the recognition surface of the headgroup, allowing distinct species to recruit different membrane-associated <proteins>.

= Phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate
{parent=Phosphoinositide}

A phosphoinositide enriched in particular membrane domains, including Golgi-associated compartments.

= Phosphatidylinositol 3,5-bisphosphate
{parent=Phosphoinositide}
{title2=$\mathrm{PI}(3,5)\mathrm P_2$}

<phosphatidylinositol 3,5-bisphosphate> is a doubly phosphorylated <phosphoinositide> involved in later endolysosomal organization and function. Perturbing it need not imply that initial surface uptake is defective.

= Phosphatidylinositol 3-phosphate
{parent=Phosphoinositide}
{title2=$\mathrm{PI}(3)\mathrm P$}

<phosphatidylinositol 3-phosphate> is a <phosphoinositide> associated with endosomal membrane identity and recruitment of specific effectors.

= Phosphatidylinositol 3,4-bisphosphate
{parent=Phosphoinositide}
{title2=$\mathrm{PI}(3,4)\mathrm P_2$}

<phosphatidylinositol 3,4-bisphosphate> has phosphates at positions 3 and 4 of the inositol ring. Local production can contribute to timed recruitment of endocytic membrane-remodeling factors.

= Lysophospholipid
{parent=Phospholipid}

A <lysophospholipid> is a phospholipid derivative missing an acyl chain relative to its corresponding diacyl form. The changed headgroup-to-tail geometry can alter curvature preference and bilayer stability.

= Phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate
{parent=Phospholipid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{PIP}_2$}

= PIP2
{c}
{synonym}

= Glycerophospholipid
{parent=Phospholipid}
{wiki}

<Glycerophospholipids> are <phospholipids> built on a <glycerol>-<phosphate> scaffold. The illustrated two-<acyl group> form is common, but the subclass also includes other substitutions and one-chain derivatives. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/lipid/lip1n2.html[IUPAC–IUB lipid nomenclature, Lip-2.2 and Lip-2.6].

= Phosphatidylserine
{parent=Glycerophospholipid}

<phosphatidylserine> is a <glycerophospholipid> with a serine-containing headgroup that is normally net negatively charged near physiological <pH>. Its leaflet distribution contributes to membrane surface properties and protein recruitment.

= Phosphatidylethanolamine
{parent=Glycerophospholipid}

<phosphatidylethanolamine> is a <glycerophospholipid> with an ethanolamine headgroup. Its relatively small headgroup can favor negative monolayer <spontaneous curvature>, depending on chain composition and conditions.

= Phosphatidylcholine
{parent=Glycerophospholipid}
{wiki}

= PC
{c}
{synonym}

<Phosphatidylcholine>, abbreviated <PC>, is a <glycerophospholipid> with a <phosphate>-<choline> head. Its <zwitterionic> head illustrates why a <hydrophilic> region need not have a nonzero net <electric charge>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21726/[Lodish et al.: membrane lipids].

= Sphingomyelin
{parent=Phospholipid}
{wiki}

<Sphingomyelin> is a <phospholipid> whose backbone is based on <sphingosine>, rather than <glycerol>. One long <hydrocarbon> region belongs to that backbone and another comes from an attached <fatty acid>. It is a counterexample to defining every <phospholipid> by the illustrated <glycerol> scaffold. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper, The Cell: the molecular composition of cells].

= Aldehyde
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

An <aldehyde> has a group $\mathrm R\mathrm C(=\mathrm O)\mathrm H$, written $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top of the illustrated open-chain <Fischer projections>; $R$ denotes the attached remainder of the <molecule>.

= Benzaldehyde
{parent=Aldehyde}

$\mathrm{PhCHO}$.

= Acrolein
{parent=Aldehyde}

The conjugated <aldehyde> $\mathrm{CH_2{=}CHCHO}$. A <carbon> <nucleophile> can add at its <carbonyl> <carbon> or, in a <conjugate addition>, at the terminal beta <carbon>. Atom mapping distinguishes the two <product (chemistry)>s.

= Formaldehyde
{parent=Aldehyde}
{wiki}

= Methanal
{synonym}

The planar molecule $\mathrm{H_2CO}$. With the C=O bond along $z$ and the molecule in the $xz$ plane, its $C_{2v}$ vibrational representation is $3A_1+2B_1+B_2$. Interchanging the $x$ and $y$ axes interchanges the $B_1$ and $B_2$ labels; physical activity is unchanged.

= Carbohydrate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

<Carbohydrates> are a family of <chemical substances> encompassing <monosaccharides>, combinations of <monosaccharides>, and their derivatives. <Glucose> is a small <carbohydrate>; <cellulose> is a long-chain <polysaccharide>. <Deoxyribose> illustrates why composition alone does not define the family: replacing a <hydroxy group> in <ribose> by <hydrogen> still leaves a <carbohydrate>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00820[IUPAC Gold Book: carbohydrates].

Many familiar <monosaccharides> have a <molecular formula> of the form $\mathrm C_n\mathrm H_{2n}\mathrm O_n$, equivalently $\mathrm C_n(\mathrm H_2\mathrm O)_n$. This explains the historical name, “hydrate of <carbon>”, but does not mean the <molecule> consists of <carbon> mixed with intact <water> <molecules>. Nor is it a universal formula: <deoxyribose> has $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. The family is defined through chemical structure and relationships, not that ratio. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00820[IUPAC's definition and origin of the name carbohydrate].

The useful surprise is the variety of roles obtainable from related building blocks. <Glucose> participates in <metabolism>, <ribose> forms part of <RNA>, and linked <glucose> units make structural <cellulose> in a plant <cell wall>. Understanding <carbohydrates> therefore connects cellular <energy>, molecular information, and material structure. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the chemical components of a cell].

= Sucrose
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}}$}

<Sucrose> dissolves in <water> as neutral <molecules>; it is a <nonelectrolyte> in this <solvent>.

= Sugar
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}

In chemical usage, <sugars> commonly means small <carbohydrates>, including single-unit <monosaccharides> and two-unit <disaccharides>. <Glucose>, <ribose>, and <deoxyribose> are <monosaccharides>. <Cellulose> is instead a <polysaccharide> assembled from many <glucose> units. The distinction counts chemically linked units; it does not classify every <carbohydrate> by sweetness or biological role. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 3.2: carbohydrates].

A useful first description of a <monosaccharide> asks how many <carbon> <chemical atoms> it contains and what its open-chain structure is. <Ribose> is an <aldopentose>, a five-<carbon> <aldehyde> <sugar>; <glucose> is an <aldohexose>, with six <carbon> <chemical atoms>. A <Fischer projection> then records the <stereochemistry>, information missing from the <molecular formula>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.1: carbohydrate classification].

= Ribose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$}

<Ribose> is a <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$. Its biologically familiar form is D-ribose, an <aldopentose> whose open-chain <Fischer projection> has $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top, $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom, and <hydroxy groups> on the right at C2, C3, and C4. The opposite horizontal substituents are <hydrogen>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-3-d-l-sugars[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.3: D-ribose and D-glucose configurations].

\Image[pentose-sugar-fischer-comparison.png]
{title=Open-chain <Fischer projections> of D-<ribose> and 2-deoxy-D-<ribose>}
{description=At each cross, horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer and vertical <chemical bonds> point away. Both drawings number the free <sugar> from its <aldehyde> carbon, C1. In <deoxyribose>, C2 has two <hydrogen> substituents and is not a <stereocenter>. These are open-chain reference structures, not a depiction of the predominant <chemical species> in <water>.}

Read the drawing by counting groups: $\mathrm{CHO}$ contributes $\mathrm C\mathrm H\mathrm O$, three $\mathrm{CHOH}$ groups contribute $\mathrm C_3\mathrm H_6\mathrm O_3$, and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ contributes $\mathrm C\mathrm H_3\mathrm O$. Their sum is $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$. The count checks composition; the right/right/right pattern additionally specifies the <stereochemistry> of D-<ribose>.

In <RNA>, <ribose> occurs as a ring within each <nucleotide>, joining a <nucleobase> to the <phosphate>-linked backbone. Its C2 position retains a <hydroxy group>, unlike <deoxyribose> in <DNA>. In a <nucleotide>, the <sugar> positions carry primes, so this is C2′. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al.: nucleotide sugars in RNA and DNA]. Free <ribose> in <water> is also predominantly cyclic, with several ring forms in <chemical equilibrium> and very little open-chain form; the <Fischer projection> is valuable for comparing configurations, not for estimating their abundances. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3819725/[Mason et al., The Conformation of a Ribose Derivative in Aqueous Solution: A Neutron Scattering and Molecular Dynamics Study (2013), introduction].

= Deoxyribose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$}

In the usual <DNA> context, <deoxyribose> means 2-deoxy-D-<ribose>, a five-<carbon> <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. Relative to D-<ribose>, the <hydroxy group> at C2 is replaced by <hydrogen>. The locant 2 matters: it identifies which position loses its <oxygen>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P10.html#P-102.5.3[Favre and Powell, IUPAC Blue Book, P-102.5.3: deoxy sugars] and https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/class/nucle.html[IUPAC: nucleic acids and their component sugars].

The paired <Fischer projections> in <ribose> make the change concrete. C2 has <hydrogen> on both sides, while C3 and C4 retain their <hydroxy groups> on the right. C2 is therefore not a <stereocenter>: exchanging its two identical <hydrogen> substituents cannot produce a distinct configuration. The unchanged C3 and C4 still carry <stereochemical> information.

Replacing $\mathrm{OH}$ by $\mathrm H$ removes one <oxygen> but leaves the total <hydrogen> count unchanged: the <hydrogen> belonging to the removed <hydroxy group> is replaced by a <hydrogen> bonded directly to <carbon>. Thus $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$ becomes $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. This comparison describes structures, rather than a complete <chemical reaction> or its mechanism.

In <DNA>, <deoxyribose> is incorporated as a ring in each <nucleotide>, with <phosphate> connections building the backbone. Its C2′ bears <hydrogen> where <RNA>'s <ribose> bears a <hydroxy group>. This small local difference lets the <sugar> structure identify the two kinds of <nucleic acid>; the <nucleobases> supply their sequence information. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al.: nucleotides as the subunits of DNA and RNA].

= Glucose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_6\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{O}_6$}

<Glucose> is a <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$. Its familiar biological form, D-glucose, is an <aldohexose>. In the open-chain <Fischer projection>, $\mathrm{CHO}$ is at the top and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom; the <hydroxy groups> at C2, C3, C4, and C5 point right, left, right, right. This full pattern distinguishes D-<glucose> from other six-<carbon> <aldehyde> <sugars>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-4-configurations-of-the-aldoses[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.4: configurations of the aldoses].

\Image[glucose-fischer-projection.png]
{title=D-<glucose>: six <carbon> positions and four open-chain <stereocenters>}
{description=Horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer; vertical <chemical bonds> point away. The <hydroxy group> pattern from C2 through C5 is right/left/right/right. $\mathrm{CHO}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ are condensed end groups. The diagram records an open-chain reference structure, not the predominant form in an aqueous <chemical solution>.}

The <molecular formula> can be checked directly: one $\mathrm{CHO}$ group, four $\mathrm{CHOH}$ groups, and one $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ group give six <carbon>, twelve <hydrogen>, and six <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. All four interior <carbon> positions are <stereocenters> in this open-chain structure. Neither their orientations nor the arrangement of <chemical bonds> can be recovered from the <molecular formula> alone.

In <water>, <glucose> exists mainly in six-membered rings in <chemical equilibrium> with a small amount of open-chain <glucose>. Ring closure connects the <hydroxy group> at C5 to the <aldehyde> carbon at C1; the ring contains five <carbon> <chemical atoms> and one <oxygen>, with C6 outside it. No <chemical atoms> are lost, so the <molecular formula> remains $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-5-cyclic-structures-of-monosaccharides-anomers[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.5: cyclic structures of monosaccharides].

<Glucose> is useful both as a substrate in cellular <metabolism> and as a building block of <polysaccharides>. <Cellulose> chains assembled from <glucose> units help support plant <cell walls>. The same small <sugar> thus connects chemical fuel to a structural material; its role depends on how it is transformed or linked. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper, The Cell: A Molecular Approach, the molecular composition of cells].

= Monosaccharide
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}

A <monosaccharide> is a single-unit <carbohydrate> that cannot be split into smaller <sugars> by <hydrolysis>. <Ribose>, <deoxyribose>, and <glucose> are examples. The unit is a whole <molecule> containing many <chemical atoms>; a <disaccharide> instead contains two linked <monosaccharide> units.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/25.1 Classification of Carbohydrates>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §25.1: simple and complex carbohydrates]{ref}

Classification combines <carbon> count with open-chain connectivity. An <aldose> has an <aldehyde> group; a <ketose> has a <ketone> group. Thus <glucose> is an <aldohexose>, while <fructose> is a six-<carbon> <ketose>. These names describe the open-chain reference forms even when cyclic forms predominate in <water>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/25.1 Classification of Carbohydrates>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §25.1: aldoses, ketoses and carbon-count classification]{ref}

\Image[aldose-ketose-open-chain-connectivity.png]
{title=Open-chain D-<glucose> and D-<fructose>: the same <molecular formula>, different <carbonyl group> connectivity}
{description=Original numbered <Fischer projections>. At tetrahedral <stereocenters>, horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer and vertical <chemical bonds> away. The <carbonyl group> <carbon> is not a tetrahedral <stereocenter>. The C5 <hydroxy group> on the right specifies D here; D does not specify the sign of <optical rotation>. These reference structures do not show relative abundances in <water>.}

Check the distinction by counting the groups in the drawings. Open-chain <glucose> contains one $\mathrm{CHO}$, four $\mathrm{CHOH}$ and one $\mathrm{CH_2OH}$ group. Open-chain <fructose> contains two $\mathrm{CH_2OH}$, three $\mathrm{CHOH}$ and one $\mathrm{C{=}O}$ group. The respective <hydrogen> totals are $1+4(2)+3=12$ and $2(3)+3(2)=12$; each structure also has six <carbon> and six <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. Their shared <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$ therefore cannot identify the <carbonyl group> position or the <stereochemistry>.

The beauty is that a familiar <sugar> name can embrace an interconverting family of forms. <Monosaccharide ring-chain interconversion> connects those drawings to <mutarotation>, a change visible with <polarimetry>. Spatial arrangement also matters to biology: an <enzyme>'s <active site> encounters a particular arrangement of <hydroxy groups>, rather than merely an inventory of <chemical atoms>. The existing <enzyme> and <chirality (chemistry)> articles explain that recognition principle.

= Aldopentose
{parent=Monosaccharide}

An <aldopentose> is a five-<carbon> <aldose>: its open-chain reference form has an <aldehyde> group at C1. <Ribose> is an example; <deoxyribose> is its 2-deoxy derivative.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/25.1 Classification of Carbohydrates>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §25.1: aldose and pentose classification]{ref}

For the unmodified open-chain <aldopentose> skeleton, count one $\mathrm{CHO}$, three $\mathrm{CHOH}$ and one $\mathrm{CH_2OH}$ group: $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$. The three interior <carbon> positions, C2–C4, are <stereocenters>. Each allows two configurations, giving $2^3=8$ <stereoisomers>, grouped into four <enantiomer> pairs. The unlike chain ends prevent a <meso compound> identification. D-<ribose> selects the right/right/right <hydroxy group> pattern in the <Fischer projection>; the word <aldopentose> alone does not select that pattern. Compare the existing <ribose> and <deoxyribose> diagrams for the separate question of which <oxygen> is present.

= Aldohexose
{parent=Monosaccharide}

An <aldohexose> is a six-<carbon> <aldose>: its open-chain reference form has an <aldehyde> group at C1. <Glucose> is an example.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/25.1 Classification of Carbohydrates>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §25.1: aldose and hexose classification]{ref}

The unmodified open-chain skeleton has four <stereocenters>, C2–C5, giving $2^4=16$ <stereoisomers>, or eight <enantiomer> pairs. Fixing the C5 <hydroxy group> on the right leaves $2^3=8$ D forms; D-<glucose>'s right/left/right/right pattern selects one. An <aldohexose> label therefore specifies less than a particular <sugar> name, and a <molecular formula> specifies less still. The existing <glucose> article checks its <chemical atom> inventory; <monosaccharide ring-chain interconversion> follows those same numbered <carbon> positions into a ring.

= Monosaccharide ring-chain interconversion
{parent=Monosaccharide}

In <water>, D-<glucose> mainly occupies cyclic forms. The C5 <hydroxy group> adds to the C1 <aldehyde>, forming a cyclic <hemiacetal>; reversible opening restores the <aldehyde>. Reclosure can give either <anomer>. Their changing proportions cause <mutarotation>, the approach of <optical rotation> to an equilibrium value. This pathway involves changing <chemical bonds>, rather than simply rotating a <hydroxy group> about a single <chemical bond>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/25.5 Cyclic Structures of Monosaccharides: Anomers>[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, §25.5: intramolecular hemiacetal formation and final mutarotation discussion]{ref}

\Image[glucose-anomers-ring-chain-connection.png]
{title=Two D-<glucose> <anomers> connect through the same open-chain <aldose>}
{description=Original <Haworth projections> share an open-chain <Fischer projection>. Both rings have the C5 $\mathrm{CH_2OH}$ group up and C2/C3/C4 <hydroxy groups> down/up/down. The C1 <hydroxy group> is down in alpha-D-glucopyranose and up in beta-D-glucopyranose. Ring <carbon>-<hydrogen> <chemical bonds> are omitted. Arrows indicate reversible connectivity, not measured <reaction rates> or relative populations. The <Haworth projection> is symbolic; actual rings are puckered.}

In this conventional <Haworth projection>, C5's $\mathrm{CH_2OH}$ group points up. The alpha <anomer>'s C1 <hydroxy group> points down, on the opposite face; the beta <anomer>'s points up, on the same face. C2/C3/C4 retain down/up/down <hydroxy groups>. These are above/below relationships to a reference ring plane, not a claim that the ring is physically flat.<Nomenclature of Carbohydrates (Recommendations 1996) by Alan D. McNaught/2-Carb-5. Cyclic forms and their representation>[Nomenclature of Carbohydrates (Recommendations 1996) by Alan D. McNaught; IUPAC–IUBMB, Nomenclature of Carbohydrates (1996), 2-Carb-5.4 and 5.7: Haworth representation and nonplanar conformations]{ref}

The <anomeric carbon> is C1, the existing <carbonyl group> <carbon> that becomes a new <stereocenter>; no extra <carbon> <chemical atom> appears. The two <anomers> differ at that centre while retaining the other configurations.<Nomenclature of Carbohydrates (Recommendations 1996) by Alan D. McNaught/2-Carb-6. Anomeric forms; use of α and β>[Nomenclature of Carbohydrates (Recommendations 1996) by Alan D. McNaught; IUPAC–IUBMB, Nomenclature of Carbohydrates (1996), 2-Carb-6.1–6.2: anomeric centre and configurational relationship]{ref}

Trace the ring as C1–C2–C3–C4–C5–O5–C1, where O5 denotes the <oxygen> originally in the C5 <hydroxy group>. It contains five <carbon> <chemical atoms> and one <oxygen>; C6 remains attached outside the ring. The C1 <carbonyl group> <oxygen> becomes the C1 <hydroxy group> <oxygen>. This accounts for every <oxygen>: O5 in the ring and five <hydroxy groups> at C1, C2, C3, C4 and C6. Ring closure preserves $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$. A six-membered ring and a six-<carbon> <sugar> are thus different counts.

= Glucose mutarotation experiment
{parent=Monosaccharide ring-chain interconversion}

An endpoint experiment uses a <polarimeter> with a $1.0\,\mathrm{dm}$ sample cell and supplied $4\%$, $6\%$ and $8\%$ D-<glucose> <chemical solutions>. Collect about $15\,\mathrm{mL}$ of each. Fill the cell first with distilled <water>, dry its windows, move bubbles to the air trap, and retain its orientation. Scan <light intensity> against <polarimeter analyzer> <angle> through $360^\circ$. The protocol fits a <Gaussian function> to the upper third of a symmetric peak to locate its maximum. Rinse with the next sample, repeat for each <glucose> <chemical solution>, and subtract the <water> maximum's <angle> from each sample maximum. This is an operational peak-location method; the <Gaussian function> is not the physical transmission law.<Equilibrium of Sugar Mutarotation by PASCO>[PASCO, Equilibrium of Sugar Mutarotation, p. 1 path length; pp. 2–4 Materials and Equipment and Procedure 1–18]{ref}

The existing <optical activity> and <polarimetry> articles explain the apparatus, <Malus's law> and instrument limitations. For an extension that observes <mutarotation> in time, freshly dissolve a specified crystalline <anomer>, record the mixing time, and repeat the <optical rotation> readout at fixed <concentration>, path length, <wavelength> and recorded <temperature>. Keep <pH> consistent and record the delay before the first reading. These are proposed additions to the endpoint protocol, not its published steps or a claim of measured results. Recheck the <water> blank, repeat the preparation, and compare with a separately equilibrated portion under the same conditions. A stable endpoint alone does not show the time evolution.

Interpret each <concentration> separately. If $\theta$ is blank-corrected <optical rotation>, $l$ is path length and $c$ is total <glucose> <mass> <concentration>, first calculate <specific rotation> $r=\theta/(lc)$. In an additive two-<anomer> approximation, let $r_\alpha,r_\beta$ be reference <specific rotations> under the same conditions and $x_\alpha$ the <mole fraction> of alpha within that pair. Equal <molar masses> make its <mass> fraction equal to $x_\alpha$, so
$$
r=x_\alpha r_\alpha+(1-x_\alpha)r_\beta,\qquad x_\alpha=\frac{r-r_\beta}{r_\alpha-r_\beta}.
$$
This is a population estimate from an assumed response model, not direct imaging of an <anomer>. For a simple consistency check, doubling $c$ at fixed $l$ and unchanged composition doubles $\theta$ in this model while leaving $r$ unchanged. Averaging raw <optical rotations> at different <concentrations> would fail this test; normalize first.

Changing <temperature>, <pH>, contamination, incomplete dissolution, window stress, bubbles or instrumental drift can imitate or obscure a changing signal. Reference <specific rotations> must match the sample conditions. Minor open-chain and other ring forms limit the two-<anomer> approximation; a single <optical rotation> cannot separately determine every population. Also, these <anomers> are <diastereomers>, not an <enantiomer> pair: the equal-and-opposite cancellation formula for <enantiomeric excess> does not apply.

= Aldose
{parent=Monosaccharide}
{wiki}

= Ketose
{parent=Monosaccharide}
{wiki}

= Disaccharide
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}

A <disaccharide> contains two linked <monosaccharide> units. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates[Biology 2e: disaccharides].

= Cellobiose
{parent=Disaccharide}

<cellobiose> consists of two <glucose> units joined by a beta-1,4 linkage. It is a characteristic soluble product in <cellulose> degradation.

= Fructose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$}

= Polysaccharide
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}

A <polysaccharide> is a <polymer> built from many linked <monosaccharide> units. <Cellulose> provides a structural example. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates[Biology 2e: polysaccharides].

= Hemicellulose
{parent=Polysaccharide}

<hemicellulose> denotes a group of plant-wall polysaccharides distinct from <cellulose>. Their association with <cellulose> can limit access, and complementary degrading enzymes can improve deconstruction.

= Starch
{parent=Polysaccharide}

<starch> is a plant storage glucan with characteristic branched and less-branched components. ADP-glucose supplies <glucose> units to important starch-synthesis reactions.

= Polysaccharide biosynthesis
{parent=Polysaccharide}

<polysaccharide biosynthesis> assembles sugar units into a polymer. Activated donors supply units, while the transferases determine linkage, branching and the relation between synthesis and transport.

= Cellulose
{parent=Polysaccharide}
{wiki}

<Cellulose> is a <polysaccharide> of <glucose> units whose chains form strong assemblies in plant <cell walls>. It illustrates a structural role for <carbohydrates>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26928/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the plant cell wall].

= Cellulose microfibril
{parent=Cellulose}

A <cellulose microfibril> is an organized assembly of <cellulose> chains. Surface accessibility and chain packing help determine which glycosidic bonds enzymes can attack.

= Agar
{parent=Polysaccharide}

<Agar> is a <polysaccharide> mixture used to solidify many <cell culture medium> formulations.

= Agarose
{parent=Polysaccharide}

A <polysaccharide> that forms a porous gel on cooling from solution. In <agarose gel electrophoresis>, the <agarose> network provides a size-dependent obstacle to <DNA> migration in an <electric field>.

= Triose phosphate
{parent=Carbohydrate}

A <triose phosphate> is a three-<carbon> <sugar> carrying a <phosphate> group. <Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate> is one example. The <Calvin cycle> supplies a net <triose phosphate> for further <carbohydrate> synthesis. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: the Calvin cycle's carbohydrate product]{ref}.

= Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate
{parent=Triose phosphate}
{wiki}

= G3P
{c}
{synonym}

= Oligosaccharide
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}

An <oligosaccharide> is a short chain of linked <monosaccharides>. Attached <oligosaccharides> can undergo <Golgi cargo processing>.

= Phosphate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Trimetaphosphate
{parent=Phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm{P_3O_9^{3-}}$}

= Cyclotriphosphate
{synonym}

The cyclic <anion> $\mathrm{P_3O_9^{3-}}$ comprising three tetrahedral $\mathrm{PO_4}$ groups linked by three P–O–P bridges. Each <phosphorus> has two terminal <oxygen> atoms. It can bind a <metal> through one terminal <oxygen> from each <phosphorus>.

= Calcium phosphate
{parent=Phosphate}

A family of calcium-containing <phosphate> compounds. <precipitation (chemistry)> of such minerals can replicate soft tissues during <phosphatization>.

= Inorganic phosphate
{parent=Phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm P_i$}

<Inorganic phosphate>, abbreviated $\mathrm P_i$, denotes the free <phosphate> pool in biochemical <chemical equations>, including <ATP> <hydrolysis>. Its constituent <chemical species> depend on <pH> and <protonation>; $\mathrm P_i$ does not specify a single <electric charge> or proton count. See the https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/thermod2/[IUBMB recommendations on biochemical reactant pools].

= Chemical formula
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

A <chemical formula> uses symbols of <chemical elements> and numerical subscripts to record composition. In the <molecular formula> $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ of <water>, the subscript $2$ counts two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>, and the omitted subscript after $\mathrm O$ means one <oxygen> <chemical atom>. A <molecular formula> describes one <molecule>; a <chemical formula> need not describe a discrete <molecule>.

Read a preceding coefficient separately from the subscripts. One $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ contains two <hydrogen> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom>; $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ contains four <hydrogen> and two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> distributed between two <water> <molecules>. In $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$, the second subscript instead puts two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> into each <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Thus $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\ne\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$: the coefficient scales the whole <chemical formula>, while a subscript specifies its composition. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-4-chemical-formulas[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §2.4: chemical formulas].

\Image[chemical-formula-and-coefficient.png]
{title=A coefficient counts whole <molecules>; a subscript counts <chemical atoms> within each <molecule>}
{description=The three columns show one <water> <molecule>, two <water> <molecules>, and one <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Their <hydrogen> counts are $2,4,2$ and their <oxygen> counts are $1,2,2$. Connections are schematic $\mathrm{H-O-H}$ and $\mathrm{H-O-O-H}$, not models of actual <bond angles> or <bond lengths>.}

For crystalline $\mathrm{NaCl}$, the <chemical formula> specifies a $1:1$ ratio of <sodium> <ions> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ to <chlorine> <ions> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$. Its <formula unit> is the accounting entity; the ordinary crystal is an extended arrangement of <ions>, rather than a collection of discrete $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <molecules>. Specifying the entity matters when assigning an <amount of substance>. See <formula unit> and <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/2.6 Ionic and Molecular Compounds> (§2.6: ionic and molecular compounds).

A <molecular formula> gives an atom inventory, not the full arrangement of <chemical bonds> or the <molecular geometry>. For example, $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$ counts six <carbon>, twelve <hydrogen>, and six <oxygen> <chemical atoms> in a <glucose> <molecule>, but does not specify the pattern shown by its <Fischer projection>. Different structures can share a <molecular formula>. This compact inventory is useful for <chemical equation> accounting precisely because it leaves the structural detail to other representations.

The choice of letters has an early explanation in https://web2.lemoyne.edu/~giunta/berzelius.html[Jöns Jacob Berzelius, Essay on the Cause of Chemical Proportions (1813–1814), §III, transcribed by Carmen Giunta]: letters made chemical signs easier to write and print, and formulas compressed information about composition. His numerical notation differed from modern subscripts; the lasting idea was to make proportions readable in ordinary text.

= Degree of unsaturation
{parent=Chemical formula}
{title2=$\mathrm{DBE}$}

= Index of hydrogen deficiency
{synonym}

The number of rings and pi-bond equivalents needed by a conventional closed-shell formula. For a neutral formula containing C, H, N and halogens, it is $(2C+2+N-H-X)/2$; <oxygen> does not change the count. A <carbonyl> contributes one and a triple bond two.

= Molecular formula
{parent=Chemical formula}

A molecular formula gives the number of <chemical atoms> of each <chemical element> in a <molecule>. For <urea>, $\mathrm{CH}_4\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$ counts one <carbon>, four <hydrogen>, two <nitrogen> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom>. It records composition rather than the arrangement of <chemical bonds>.

= Empirical formula
{parent=Molecular formula}

The simplest whole-number <chemical atom> ratio of a substance.

= Formula unit
{parent=Chemical formula}
{wiki}

A <formula unit> is a specified compositional accounting entity for a <chemical substance> that need not consist of discrete <molecules>. For ordinary crystalline $\mathrm{NaCl}$, one <formula unit> counts one <sodium> <ion> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and one <chlorine> <ion> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$; it does not identify an isolated pair within the crystal. https://iupac.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/IUPAC-GB3-2012-2ndPrinting-PDFsearchable.pdf#page=65[Cohen et al., IUPAC Green Book, 3rd ed., §2.10.1(iii)(b), p. 51] defines a <formula unit> as a specified group of <chemical atoms>, rather than a unit of measurement. An <amount of substance> of one <mole> of $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <formula units> therefore corresponds to one <mole> of each kind of <ion>.

= Organic acid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Carboxylic acid
{parent=Organic acid}
{wiki}

= Hexanoic acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3(CH_2)_4CO_2H}$}

The unbranched six-carbon acid $\mathrm{CH_3(CH_2)_4CO_2H}$. Its <carbonyl> <carbon> is included in the six-carbon total.

= Carboxylic acid dimer
{parent=Carboxylic acid}

An association of two neutral acid molecules, commonly a cyclic pair joined by two reciprocal <hydrogen bonds>. Its abundance depends on concentration and competing <solvation>.

= Trifluoroacetic acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}

The acid CF3COOH. Its electron-withdrawing substituent makes it useful for acid-labile protecting-group removal; anhydrous tert-butyl <ester> cleavage need not follow water-assisted acyl hydrolysis.

= Short-chain fatty acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}

A short-chain <fatty acid>, including major microbial fermentation products such as acetate, propionate and butyrate. Chain-length conventions vary with context.

= Oxalic acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{title2=$\mathrm{HOOC{-}COOH}$}
{wiki}

= Oxalate
{parent=Oxalic acid}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_2O_4^{2-}}$}
{wiki}

= Pyruvic acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COCOOH}$}

= Pyruvate
{parent=Pyruvic acid}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COCOO^-}$}

<Pyruvate> is the <conjugate base> of <pyruvic acid>.

= Tartaric acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_4H_6O_6}$}

<Tartaric acid> has two <hydroxy groups> and two <carboxylic acid> groups. Its <meso compound> form illustrates why two <stereocenters> need not make a <chiral molecule>.

= Acetic acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$}

<Acetic acid> is a <weak acid> that partly transfers <protons> to <water>, supplying <hydronium> and negative <ions>. It is therefore a <weak electrolyte> in <water>.

= Acetate ion
{parent=Acetic acid}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}$}

The <conjugate base> of <acetic acid>. It is a <reaction product> in the <conductometric saponification assay>.

= Fatty acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}

A <fatty acid> is a <carboxylic acid> with a <hydrocarbon> chain. Its chain supplies much of the <hydrophobic> region when incorporated into a <triglyceride> or typical <glycerophospholipid>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: fatty acids].

= Docosahexaenoic acid
{parent=Fatty acid}
{title2=$22{:}6\,n\!\!-3$}

= DHA
{c}
{synonym}

<docosahexaenoic acid> is a 22-carbon omega-3 fatty acid with six double bonds. Production and storage can require additional steps beyond an EPA-producing pathway.

= Eicosapentaenoic acid
{parent=Fatty acid}
{title2=$20{:}5\,n\!\!-3$}

= EPA
{c}
{synonym}

<eicosapentaenoic acid> is a 20-carbon omega-3 fatty acid with five double bonds. Engineering its production requires coordinated desaturation, elongation and lipid-carrier handling.

= Alpha-linolenic acid
{parent=Fatty acid}
{title2=$18{:}3\,n\!\!-3$}

<alpha-linolenic acid> is an 18-carbon omega-3 fatty acid with three double bonds. Its presence in conventional plant oils is distinct from substantial synthesis of EPA or DHA.

= Amino acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}

An <amino acid> combines <carboxylic acid> and <amine> functionality in the same <molecule>; the name alone does not specify their separation. An <alpha-amino acid> has its amino <nitrogen> attached to the <carbon> immediately adjacent to the carboxyl <carbon>, the <amino acid alpha carbon>. For example, $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH_2{-}COOH}$ is <glycine>, whereas $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH_2{-}CH_2{-}COOH}$ has an extra intervening <carbon> and is not an <alpha-amino acid>. These <chemical formulas> describe connectivity, not the predominant <protonation> state in <water>.

Most common <proteinogenic amino acids> share the skeleton $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH}(R)\mathrm{{-}COOH}$, with $R$ the <amino acid side chain>. In <glycine>, $R$ is <hydrogen>; in <alanine>, $R=\mathrm{CH_3}$. <Proline> is a useful exception to the displayed primary-<amine> skeleton: its amino <nitrogen> is part of a ring and has a second <carbon> substituent. The shared backbone permits assembly into <polypeptides>, while different <amino acid side chains> give the resulting <proteins> diverse chemistry. <Nomenclature and Symbolism for Amino Acids and Peptides (Recommendations 1983) by IUPAC-IUB Joint Commission on Biochemical Nomenclature>[3AA-1, Table 1: glycine, alanine and proline structures; 3AA-2.2.5, backbone and side chain]{ref}

In a <chemical solution>, <protonation> changes the <electric charges> without changing this backbone. Free <glycine> near neutral <pH> is predominantly $\mathrm{H_3N^+{-}CH_2{-}COO^-}$, a <zwitterion>. Its positive and negative groups are joined within one <molecule>; zero net <electric charge> does not mean absence of local <electric charges>, nor does it mean the uncharged drawing $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH_2{-}COOH}$ predominates. <Glycine protonation> explains how lowering or raising <pH> changes the balance. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.1, opening zwitterion and acid/base interconversion discussion]{ref}

Building a <protein> joins the carboxyl <carbon> of one <amino acid> to the amino <nitrogen> of another through a <peptide bond>, an <amide> linkage. The formal net condensation loses the elements of <water>; cellular <translation> uses activated substrates at a <ribosome>, rather than simply mixing free <amino acids> in <water>. Incorporated units are <amino acid residues>. The interior <amide> groups no longer have the same acid/base behavior as the two free terminal groups of <glycine>, so its free-<molecule> <glycine titration> cannot be copied independently at every chain position. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.4, opening peptide formation and amide resonance discussion]{ref}

<Chirality (chemistry)> distinguishes most common <proteinogenic amino acids> from <glycine>. The other nineteen members of the conventional twenty have a <stereocenter> at their <amino acid alpha carbon>; ordinary <translation> incorporates their L forms. <Glycine>'s two identical <hydrogen> substituents make it achiral. Thus being an <amino acid>, or even an <alpha-amino acid>, does not by itself imply <chirality (chemistry)>. <Nomenclature and Symbolism for Amino Acids and Peptides (Recommendations 1983) by IUPAC-IUB Joint Commission on Biochemical Nomenclature>[3AA-3.2, note: glycine itself is achiral; 3AA-3.3 and 3AA-3.5, protein-source L configuration]{ref}

= Asparagine
{parent=Amino acid}

<asparagine> is an amino acid with a side-chain amide. Its side-chain nitrogen is the usual attachment site in canonical N-linked <protein glycosylation>.

= Histidine
{parent=Amino acid}

An <amino acid> with a side chain containing an imidazole ring. Its metal-coordination properties support purification through a <histidine tag>.

= Methionine
{parent=Amino acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Met},\ \mathrm M$}

Methionine is an <amino acid> represented by Met or M in <protein> sequence notation. In the standard <genetic code>, AUG encodes it, as in the worked <gene expression> example. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[OpenStax Biology 2e, 15.1: the standard genetic code]{ref}

= Alanine
{parent=Amino acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ala},\ \mathrm A$}

Alanine is an <amino acid> represented by Ala or A in <protein> sequence notation. GCU is one of its <codons> in the standard <genetic code>, and supplies the second residue in the worked <gene expression> example. <Biology 2e by Mary Ann Clark and others>[OpenStax Biology 2e, 15.1: alanine codons]{ref}

= Alpha-amino acid
{parent=Amino acid}

An <alpha-amino acid> has amino functionality on the <carbon> next to its carboxyl <carbon>. The skeleton in <amino acid> shows this adjacency explicitly. Numbering the carboxyl <carbon> as C1 makes the adjacent position C2, also called the alpha position. <Nomenclature and Symbolism for Amino Acids and Peptides (Recommendations 1983) by IUPAC-IUB Joint Commission on Biochemical Nomenclature>[3AA-1, Table 1 and its note on hypothetical uncharged formulas; 3AA-2.2.1, acyclic-amino-acid numbering]{ref}

= Proteinogenic amino acid
{parent=Alpha-amino acid}
{wiki}

A <proteinogenic amino acid> is incorporated into <proteins> during <translation>. The conventional twenty include <glycine>, <alanine>, <methionine> and <proline>; they are a subset of <amino acids>, not a definition of the whole family. <Nomenclature and Symbolism for Amino Acids and Peptides (Recommendations 1983) by IUPAC-IUB Joint Commission on Biochemical Nomenclature>[3AA-1, Table 1: alpha-amino acids incorporated into protein under mRNA direction]{ref}

= Tyrosine
{parent=Proteinogenic amino acid}
{title2=$\mathrm{Tyr},\ Y$}

<Tyrosine> has a phenolic side chain. Its hydroxyl can participate in hydrogen bonding and, when the environment is suitable, acid/base chemistry.

= Glutamine
{parent=Proteinogenic amino acid}
{title2=$\mathrm{Gln},\ Q$}

<Glutamine> has a neutral side-chain amide. Its N–H groups can donate <hydrogen bonds> and its carbonyl <oxygen> can accept them; its amide <nitrogen> is not an ordinary amine base.

= Proline
{parent=Proteinogenic amino acid}
{wiki}

<Proline> is a cyclic <alpha-amino acid> whose <amine> <nitrogen> is joined to two <carbon> positions within a five-membered ring. This explains the exception to $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH}(R)\mathrm{{-}COOH}$ in <amino acid>. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.1, primary alpha-amino-acid and secondary proline structures]{ref}

= Lysine
{parent=Proteinogenic amino acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Lys},\ \mathrm K$}

A <proteinogenic amino acid> represented by K or Lys in <protein primary structure>. Its <amino acid side chain> carries predominantly positive <electric charge> near neutral <pH>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/26.1 Structures of Amino Acids>[Organic Chemistry by John McMurry; Table 26.1, lysine and arginine rows; side-chain protonation at pH 7.3]{ref}

<Trypsin> preferentially cleaves after its <amino acid residues>.

= Arginine
{parent=Proteinogenic amino acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Arg},\ \mathrm R$}

A <proteinogenic amino acid> represented by R or Arg in <protein primary structure>. Its <amino acid side chain> carries predominantly positive <electric charge> near neutral <pH>.<Organic Chemistry by John McMurry/26.1 Structures of Amino Acids>[Organic Chemistry by John McMurry; Table 26.1, lysine and arginine rows; side-chain protonation at pH 7.3]{ref}

<Trypsin> preferentially cleaves after its <amino acid residues>.

= Glycine protonation
{parent=Alpha-amino acid}

The three principal aqueous <glycine> forms differ by loss of one <proton> at each step:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{H_3N^+{-}CH_2{-}COOH}+\mathrm H_2\mathrm O&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{H_3N^+{-}CH_2{-}COO^-}+\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+,\\
\mathrm{H_3N^+{-}CH_2{-}COO^-}+\mathrm H_2\mathrm O&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH_2{-}COO^-}+\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+.
\end{aligned}
$$
Call these <glycine> forms $G^+$, $G^0$ and $G^-$, respectively: a <cation>, a <zwitterion> and an <anion>, with net <electric charges> $+e$, $0$ and $-e$, where $e$ is the <elementary charge>. $G^0$ specifically denotes the <zwitterion>, not an uncharged amino/carboxyl drawing. The first dissociation removes the carboxyl <proton>; the second removes a <proton> from the positively charged <nitrogen>. The <chemical equilibrium> changes are reversible, not three permanently separate kinds of <glycine>. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.1, aqueous zwitterion protonation/deprotonation; §26.2, two successive amino-acid dissociations]{ref}

For all calculations and both diagrams here, explicitly stipulate the <pKa> values $\mathrm pK_{a1}=2.35$ and $\mathrm pK_{a2}=9.78$. These are illustrative model inputs, not measurements from the <glycine titration experiment> or a claim of universal constants. Actual <acid dissociation constants> depend on <temperature> and on the convention used for <thermodynamic activity>; apparent <concentration>-based values also depend on <ionic strength>. For comparison, the inspected textbook tabulates $2.34$ and $9.60$ for <glycine>, which would give a different <isoelectric point>. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.1, Table 26.1, glycine row: pKa 2.34, 9.60 and pI 5.97]{ref}

\Image[glycine-protonation-speciation-model.png]
{title=<Glycine protonation> and calculated <chemical species> fractions}
{description=Original three-state ideal <glycine speciation model>, with stipulated <pKa> values $2.35$ and $9.78$. The structures show net <electric charges> $+1$, $0$ and $-1$ in units of the <elementary charge>. Curves are calculated fractions, not measured populations. The vertical markers locate the two <pKa> values and the model <isoelectric point> $6.065$.}

= Glycine speciation model
{parent=Glycine protonation}

In an ideal aqueous <chemical solution>, set $h=[\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+]/c^\circ=10^{-\mathrm{pH}}$, with $c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, and approximate <activity coefficients> by one. Put $K_1=10^{-2.35}$ and $K_2=10^{-9.78}$ for the stipulated <dimensionless> <acid dissociation constants>. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> gives the adjacent-state <ratios>
$$
\frac{[G^0]}{[G^+]}=\frac{K_1}{h},\qquad\frac{[G^-]}{[G^0]}=\frac{K_2}{h}.
$$
With <analytical concentration> $C_T=[G^+]+[G^0]+[G^-]$, normalization gives the three fractions and mean <electric charge> in units of the <elementary charge>:
$$
D=h^2+K_1h+K_1K_2,\qquad
\alpha_+=\frac{h^2}{D},\quad\alpha_0=\frac{K_1h}{D},\quad\alpha_-=\frac{K_1K_2}{D},\qquad
\bar z=\alpha_+-\alpha_-.
$$
All three fractions are nonnegative and sum to one. At <pH> $7$, the model gives about $99.832\%$ <zwitterion>, $0.00224\%$ <cation> and $0.1657\%$ <anion>; $\bar z\approx-0.00163$. Predominantly <zwitterionic> therefore need not mean exactly at the <isoelectric point>.

Setting $\bar z=0$ requires $h^2=K_1K_2$. Taking the <logarithm> yields
$$
\mathrm{pI}=\frac{\mathrm pK_{a1}+\mathrm pK_{a2}}2=\frac{2.35+9.78}2=6.065.
$$
At this model <isoelectric point>, $\alpha_+=\alpha_-\approx0.0001927$ and $\alpha_0\approx0.9996146$: about $0.0193\%$ of each charged form remains. At either <pKa>, the relevant adjacent fractions are equal, but the third fraction is not mathematically zero. This three-state model neglects the minor uncharged form $\mathrm{H_2N{-}CH_2{-}COOH}$; macroscopic <pKa> values alone do not determine its fraction separately from the <zwitterion>.

= Glycine titration
{parent=Glycine protonation}

Adding <sodium hydroxide> to acidified <glycine> successively removes its two titratable <protons>. Two broad <buffer solution> regions appear around the two <pKa> values, separated by a steep <pH> rise near the <isoelectric point>. This is useful evidence for two separated <acid-base chemistry> steps even though the changing <chemical species> are invisible. In the approximation that added <hydroxide> only changes <glycine protonation>, $b=1-\bar z$ is the added base <amount of substance> per initial <amount of substance> of <glycine>. Then $b\approx0.5$, $1$ and $1.5$ correspond to $\mathrm pK_{a1}$, $\mathrm{pI}$ and $\mathrm pK_{a2}$; endpoints are not exactly pure <chemical species>. <Organic Chemistry by John McMurry>[§26.2, Figure 26.2: calculated two-leg titration and half-equivalent interpretation]{ref}

\Image[glycine-titration-mean-charge-model.png]
{title=Calculated <glycine titration> and mean <electric charge> models}
{description=Upper panel: solid curve includes <self-ionization of water> and <dilution> for $20\,\mathrm{mL}$ of $0.100\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <glycine> with one equivalent of <hydrochloric acid>, titrated with $0.100\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <sodium hydroxide>. Dashed curve retains only the <glycine> proton inventory. Lower panel: $\bar z$ against <pH> from the <glycine speciation model>. Both are stipulated ideal models, not laboratory data.}

For the solid-curve model, start with $n_G=0.00200\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <glycine> in $V_0=0.0200\,\mathrm L$ and exactly $n_G$ of fully dissociated <hydrochloric acid>; this fixes the <chloride> inventory, but does not force every <glycine> <molecule> to remain $G^+$. Let the <sodium hydroxide> <concentration> be $C_B=0.100\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and $b=n_{\mathrm{NaOH}}/n_G$. Assume <volume (thermodynamics)> is additive and no other <ions> are present; stipulate $K_w=10^{-14}$ for <self-ionization of water> at a <temperature> of $25\,\mathrm{{}^\circ C}$. Then
$$
V=V_0+\frac{bn_G}{C_B},\qquad C_T=\frac{n_G}{V},\qquad
[\mathrm{Na^+}]=bC_T,\qquad[\mathrm{Cl^-}]=C_T.
$$
Balancing all positive and negative <electric charges> requires
$$
c^\circ\left(h-\frac{K_w}{h}\right)+C_T\left(b+\bar z-1\right)=0.
$$
Solving this equation gives the solid curve. It remains finite at $b=0$ and beyond $b=2$, where ignoring free <hydronium> and <hydroxide> would fail. At the first <pKa>, the full model gives $b\approx0.436$, rather than $0.5$: appreciable free <hydronium> makes that half-equivalent shortcut inaccurate. At the second <pKa>, $b\approx1.502$. Known acid inventory, <dilution>, and <thermodynamic activity> assumptions are therefore part of any quantitative interpretation, not optional corrections inferred from the curve's shape.

= Glycine titration experiment
{parent=Glycine titration}

The Amrita Virtual Lab procedure uses $0.1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ <glycine>, <hydrochloric acid> and <sodium hydroxide>, two <burettes>, a <pH> meter, beaker and stirrer. A <pipette> transfers $20\,\mathrm{mL}$ of <glycine> into a $100\,\mathrm{mL}$ beaker. Calibrate the meter with standard <buffer solutions> at <pH> $4$, $7$ and $10$, then record the starting <pH>. Add <hydrochloric acid> in $0.3\,\mathrm{mL}$ increments, recording <pH> after each addition until $1.6$. Rinse the <electrode> in distilled <water>, take a separate fresh $20\,\mathrm{mL}$ portion, and add <sodium hydroxide> in $0.3\,\mathrm{mL}$ increments until <pH> $12.5$. Its observed readout is paired added <volume (thermodynamics)> and <pH>, plotted as an <acid-base titration> curve; it does not measure each <glycine> form directly. <Titration Curves of Aminoacids, Amrita Virtual Lab/Procedure>[Materials Required, items 1–8; Working steps, items 1–9: separate acid and base titrations]{ref}

These are two titrations from the starting <glycine> <chemical solution>, rather than the single acidified-sample protocol assumed by the solid curve in <glycine titration>. Retain each portion's initial <volume (thermodynamics)> and the signed added acid/base <amount of substance>; do not concatenate raw <burette> readings and assume the result begins at $b=0$. For this procedure's $0.00200\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <glycine>, each $0.3\,\mathrm{mL}$ addition supplies $0.000030\,\mathrm{mol}$ of acid or base, a $0.015$ increment relative to the <glycine> inventory. This calculated resolution is not a reported measurement.

Useful additional controls are a fresh repeat portion, independently checked acid/base <concentrations>, a <water> blank, and a calibration-check <buffer solution> after the run. Stir without striking the <glass electrode>, keep its sensing region immersed and wait for stable readings; record <temperature> and background salts. Reduce addition size near a steep <pH> change. These are suggested controls for testing the model, beyond the published steps. A <water> blank can reveal contamination but must not be subtracted directly from <pH>, which is a <logarithm>.

The standards at <pH> $4$, $7$ and $10$ do not bracket either endpoint of this protocol. Extreme readings therefore require independently checked instrument performance, and alkaline samples are sensitive to atmospheric <carbon dioxide> uptake. <Electrode> drift, incomplete mixing, <volume (thermodynamics)> error, <dilution> and changing <ionic strength> can shift apparent <pKa> values. A titration constrains the acid/base model; it cannot establish <chirality (chemistry)> or separate every microscopic <protonation> form. The two original diagrams show calculated predictions to compare with measured points, not reconstructed experimental data.

