= Periodic table
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}

The <periodic table> arranges <chemical elements> in increasing <atomic number>, across successive <periodic table periods>. Its columns are <periodic groups>, numbered 1–18. The layout places recurring outer <electron configurations> above one another, making similarities in <chemical bonds> and <chemical reactions> easier to recognize. Ordering is by <proton> count, not by <mass>; the <mass number> of a particular <isotope> does not determine its box. See the https://iupac.org/what-we-do/periodic-table-of-elements/[official IUPAC periodic table and numbering conventions].

For the first eighteen neutral <chemical atoms> in their <ground states>, a compact description counts <electrons> in successive <electron shells>. Thus <carbon> has $(2,4)$, meaning two <electrons> in the first <electron shell> and four in the second; the sum $2+4=6$ agrees with its <atomic number>. <Sodium> has $(2,8,1)$, whose sum is $11$. These are occupancy counts, not circular trajectories of <electrons>.

\Image[first-three-periods.png]
{title=The first three <periodic table periods>: <atomic numbers> and neutral <ground state> <electron shell> occupancies}
{description=Colours distinguish <periodic group> columns only, without encoding numerical property trends. The spaces in columns 3–12 preserve the eighteen-column <periodic table> layout: no <chemical elements> occupy those positions in these three <periodic table periods>. Each cell gives a <chemical element>'s symbol, <atomic number>, and <electron shell> counts.}

Reading each row from left to right gives:
* <Periodic table period> 1: <hydrogen> $(1)$ and <helium> $(2)$.
* <Periodic table period> 2: <lithium> $(2,1)$, <beryllium> $(2,2)$, <boron> $(2,3)$, <carbon> $(2,4)$, <nitrogen> $(2,5)$, <oxygen> $(2,6)$, <fluorine> $(2,7)$, and <neon> $(2,8)$.
* <Periodic table period> 3: <sodium> $(2,8,1)$, <magnesium> $(2,8,2)$, <aluminium> $(2,8,3)$, <silicon> $(2,8,4)$, <phosphorus> $(2,8,5)$, <sulfur> $(2,8,6)$, <chlorine> $(2,8,7)$, and <argon> $(2,8,8)$.

The counting outline is short: the occupied <electron subshells> in this range are $1s$, $2s$, $2p$, $3s$, and $3p$, filled in that order for these neutral <ground states>. Each $s$ <electron subshell> holds up to two <electrons> and each $p$ <electron subshell> up to six, giving $2$, $2+6=8$, and $2+6=8$ along these three rows. This $2,8,8$ summary applies only through <argon>; it is not a general rule that each <electron shell> fills completely before the next begins, nor a claim that the third <electron shell> can hold only eight <electrons>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, electronic structure and electron configurations].

The recurring outer counts make the arrangement useful. <Lithium> and <sodium> each have one <valence electron>, whereas <fluorine> and <chlorine> each have seven <valence electrons>. Such similarities help anticipate <chemical reactions>, without making all properties identical throughout a <periodic group>. <Helium> is an instructive exception to an eight-<electron> shortcut: its two <electrons> complete the first <electron shell>, so it belongs above <neon> and <argon> in <periodic group> 18. <Hydrogen> shares column 1 with <lithium> and <sodium> but has its own distinct chemistry. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-5-the-periodic-table[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, periodic-table organization].

= Actinide
{parent=Periodic table}

A member of the <actinide> series of chemical elements. Their <oxidation states>, ionic sizes and radioactive <isotopes> matter in <nuclear fuel> and waste chemistry.

= Lanthanide
{parent=Periodic table}

A member of the <lanthanide> element series, whose ionic-size and redox trends reflect the filling and incomplete shielding of the 4f shell.

= Ytterbium
{parent=Lanthanide}
{title2=$\mathrm{Yb}$}

= Europium
{parent=Lanthanide}
{title2=$\mathrm{Eu}$}

= Periodic trend
{parent=Periodic table}

= Atomic radius
{parent=Periodic trend}

A conventional measure of atomic extent. Covalent, metallic, ionic and van der Waals definitions differ, so one universal hard-sphere radius should not be assumed.

= Inert-pair effect
{parent=Periodic trend}

Relative reluctance of a heavier main-group ns electron pair to participate in the higher oxidation state. Retained pairs can nevertheless influence structure through mixing and stereochemical activity.

= Lanthanide contraction
{parent=Periodic table}

The reduction in size across the lanthanide series associated with incomplete screening by $4f$ electrons; it contributes to size similarities and contractions in subsequent $5d$ elements.

= Group
{disambiguate=periodic table}
{parent=Periodic table}
{wiki}

= Periodic group
{synonym}

= Periodic table group
{synonym}

A <periodic group> is a vertical column of the <periodic table>. For example, <fluorine> and <chlorine> are in <periodic group> 17, with seven <valence electrons> in each neutral <ground state> <chemical atom>. A shared outer <electron configuration> supports similarities in <chemical reactions>, rather than equality of every property.

= Period
{disambiguate=periodic table}
{parent=Periodic table}
{wiki}

= Periodic table period
{synonym}

A <periodic table period> is a horizontal row of the <periodic table>, read in increasing <atomic number>. <Periodic table period> 2 runs from <lithium>, $Z=3$, to <neon>, $Z=10$: $10-3+1=8$ <chemical elements>. In these neutral <ground states>, the second <electron shell> gains <electrons> while the first retains two.

= Boron
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm B$, $Z=5$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> five.

= Boron-10
{parent=Boron}
{title2=${}^{10}_{5}\mathrm B$}

An <isotope> of <boron> with 5 <protons> and 5 <neutrons>. Its <nuclear spin> concerns the coupled nuclear <quantum state>, not the electronic configuration.

= Fluorine
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm F$, $Z=9$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> nine.

= Aluminium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Al}$, $Z=13$}
{wiki}

= Aluminum
{synonym}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> thirteen.

= Aluminium oxide
{parent=Aluminium}
{title2=$\mathrm{Al_2O_3}$}

= Alumina
{synonym}

The oxide with formula $\mathrm{Al_2O_3}$. Crystal structure, porosity and surface chemistry distinguish its different material forms.

= Phosphorus
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm P$, $Z=15$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> fifteen.

= White phosphorus
{parent=Phosphorus}

A molecular allotrope consisting of tetrahedral $\mathrm P_4$ molecules. Every vertex has three P–P bonds and one <lone pair>; its $60^\circ$ P–P–P angles produce substantial angle strain.

= Sulfur
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm S$, $Z=16$}
{wiki}

= Sulphur
{synonym}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> sixteen.

= Tetrasulfur dication
{parent=Sulfur}
{title2=$\mathrm{S_4^{2+}}$}

$\mathrm{S_4^{2+}}$, a square-planar <sulfur> ring with a useful six-pi-electron aromatic description. Its 22 <valence electrons> are partitioned differently from a five-vertex <Zintl cluster>.

= Chlorine
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Cl}$, $Z=17$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> seventeen.

= Noble gas
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}

A <chemical element> in group 18 of the <periodic table>. <Argon> is a familiar example: its <chemical inertness> under many ordinary conditions makes it useful when a process needs a gas that does not readily react with the material being processed. <Chemical inertness> is a statement about conditions, rather than an assertion that no compound can exist.

= Krypton
{parent=Noble gas}
{wiki}
{title2=Kr}

= Radon
{parent=Noble gas}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Rn}$, $Z=86$}

= Argon
{parent=Noble gas}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ar}$, $Z=18$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> eighteen. <Argon> is a colourless <noble gas>. It can provide a protective atmosphere around hot, reactive materials, for example in <welding>, where excluding <oxygen> limits <oxidation>. Commercial production separates it by <distillation> of liquid air.<Periodic Table by Royal Society of Chemistry/Argon — Element information, properties and uses>[Periodic Table by Royal Society of Chemistry; Fact box, Uses and Natural abundance: atomic number, group, protective atmosphere and liquid-air distillation]{ref}

Its discovery shows why a small disagreement can matter more than a reassuringly repeatable result. Two preparations can each give consistent measurements while producing different <mixtures>. The <Rayleigh-Ramsay argon density experiment> investigated just such a discrepancy. <Gas density> links the weighing to a mean <molar mass>; a separate <separation process> tests which component accounts for it.

= Argon-40
{parent=Argon}
{title2=$^{40}_{18}\mathrm{Ar}$}

<Argon-40> is a stable <isotope> with 18 <protons> and 22 <neutrons>. Radiogenic <argon-40> accumulated from <potassium-40> is a daughter product used in <potassium-argon dating>.

= Rayleigh-Ramsay argon density experiment
{c}
{parent=Argon}

In his 1895 lecture, <Lord Rayleigh> compared <gas> masses in a globe under matched <pressure> and <temperature>. Removing <oxygen> from air left gas about half a percent heavier than <nitrogen> prepared entirely from chemical sources. Several preparation routes reproduced the difference; <hydrogen> contamination was a concern. An eight-month storage test re-established the filling conditions: simply reweighing a sealed globe could not test a change in <molar mass>. <William Ramsay> concentrated the residual gas by circulating the mixture over hot <magnesium>, which consumed <nitrogen>. A spectral test checked remaining <nitrogen>. Agreement of spectra from this route and the alternative spark-and-alkali separation supported a common residual gas.<Argon by Lord Rayleigh>[Argon, Royal Institution Proceedings 14 (1895), 524–538: gas-weight table; storage test; magnesium circulation; nitrogen spectrum test and comparison of isolation routes]{ref}

\Image[argon-density-comparison-and-separation.png]
{title=Different preparation routes, a controlled gas weighing, and residual-gas separation}
{description=Original functional diagram of the <Rayleigh-Ramsay argon density experiment>, not a scale reconstruction. The weighing comparison and subsequent chemical separation answer different questions. The <gas density> comparison requires common <pressure> and <temperature>; the hot-<magnesium> treatment concentrates the unconsumed component. The diagram shows operations, not a laboratory construction plan.}

The density difference alone identifies neither a unique <chemical element> nor its concentration. A heavier contaminant, a lighter reference contaminant, or unmatched filling conditions can all shift a weighing. A <separation process> and an <optical spectrum> provide additional observations. This combination makes the experiment powerful: the anomaly motivates another operation whose result can be checked independently.

= Argon detection in a binary gas mixture
{parent=Argon}
{title2=$x=\frac{\rho/\rho_N-1}{M_A/M_N-1}$}

Consider an original simplified model containing only <nitrogen> and <argon>, with <argon> <mole fraction> $x$. Let their <molar masses> be $M_N$ and $M_A$. Adding the component <masses> gives the mixture's mean <molar mass>
$$
\overline M=(1-x)M_N+xM_A.
$$
For an <ideal gas> mixture at the same <pressure> and <temperature> as a pure-<nitrogen> reference, the <gas density> ratio is
$$
r=\frac{\rho}{\rho_N}=\frac{\overline M}{M_N}
=1+x\left(\frac{M_A}{M_N}-1\right),\qquad
\boxed{x=\frac{r-1}{M_A/M_N-1}.}
$$
The inversion requires known identities, the binary-mixture assumption and $M_A\ne M_N$. A density ratio cannot uniquely recover an arbitrary multicomponent <mixture>. Nor should $x$ be interpreted as the <argon> fraction of untreated air: the model sample has only the two specified components.

Choose round model values $M_N=28\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$ and $M_A=40\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$. For $x=0.010$, the calculated relative density excess is
$$
\boxed{r-1=0.010\left(\frac{40}{28}-1\right)
=0.00428571\ldots\simeq0.429\%.}
$$
Thus a one-percent <mole fraction> does not mean a one-percent <gas density> change. In a chosen $2.00\,\mathrm L$ globe at $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$ and $300\,\mathrm K$, the <ideal gas law> gives approximately $2.24508\,\mathrm g$ for pure <nitrogen> and $2.25471\,\mathrm g$ for this mixture: only $9.62\,\mathrm{mg}$ separates the weighings. These are model calculations with rounded <molar masses>, not historical measurements.

\Image[argon-mixture-density-and-temperature-confounding.png]
{title=A small admixture changes gas density, but temperature can imitate the signal}
{description=Calculated binary-mixture model with stipulated <molar masses> $28$ and $40\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$. The left panel shows the linear <gas density> contrast. The right compares three hypothetical globe fillings: a reference, one-percent <argon> at the same <temperature>, and pure <nitrogen> cooled enough to give the identical <mass>. No historical data are plotted.}

A direct control calculation explains why the filling conditions matter. At fixed <pressure>, pure <nitrogen> at
$$
T'=\frac{300\,\mathrm K}{1+0.00428571\ldots}
\simeq298.720\,\mathrm K
$$
has the same <gas density> as that mixture at $300\,\mathrm K$. A roughly $1.28\,\mathrm K$ mismatch can therefore imitate the entire stipulated composition signal. For small changes, the <ideal gas law> gives
$$
\frac{d\rho}{\rho}=\frac{dp}{p}+\frac{d\overline M}{\overline M}-\frac{dT}{T}.
$$
An independently measured <pressure> and <temperature>, matched weighing procedure, and checks for residual vapour distinguish composition from these alternative causes. The model explains the need for controls; it does not supply the complete corrections for a real gas-weighing instrument.

= Sodium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Na}$}

Sodium is the <chemical element> of <atomic number> eleven. Carbon-fusion channels in <carbon burning> include ${}^{12}\mathrm C+{}^{12}\mathrm C\longrightarrow{}^{23}\mathrm{Na}+p$, producing sodium-23 and a <proton>. Its abundance in expelled stellar material depends on later reactions and mixing, rather than on this one production channel alone.

= Calcium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ca}$}
{wiki}

= Calcium ion
{parent=Calcium}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$}

The divalent cation $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$. Changes in its free intracellular concentration participate in <calcium signaling>; total cellular calcium includes substantial bound and sequestered pools.

= Calcium-40
{parent=Calcium}
{title2=$^{40}_{20}\mathrm{Ca}$}

<Calcium-40> is an even-even <nucleus> with the closed-shell values $Z=N=20$. Its extra stability illustrates <nuclear shell> corrections beyond a smooth <semi-empirical mass formula>.

= Calcium hydroxide
{parent=Calcium}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2}$}
{wiki}

<Calcium hydroxide> supplies <calcium> <cations> and <hydroxide> <anions> on <dissolution> in <water>. The ideal one-to-two <stoichiometry> relates <molar solubility> to the dissolved <concentrations>; <solubility product measurement by titration> explains how the <hydroxide> inventory is measured.

= Potassium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{K}$}
{wiki}

= Potassium-40
{parent=Potassium}
{wiki}
{title2=$^{40}_{19}\mathrm K$}

<Potassium-40> is a long-lived radioactive <isotope> with 19 <protons> and 21 <neutrons>. It can decay to <calcium-40> through <beta-minus decay>, or to <argon-40> through <electron capture> and a rare <beta-plus decay>. The argon-producing branch supports <potassium-argon dating>.

= Atomic number
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$Z$}
{wiki}

The <atomic number> $Z$ is the number of <protons> in an <atomic nucleus>, and fixes the <chemical element>. It is a count, with no units. An <atomic nucleus> with six <protons> is always <carbon>, regardless of how many <neutrons> accompany them or how many <electrons> surround it. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00499[IUPAC Gold Book, atomic number].

Three independent bookkeeping questions become easy once their symbols are kept separate. Let $N$ count <neutrons>, $n_e$ count <electrons>, $A$ be the <mass number>, and $Q$ be the net <electric charge>. A <proton> carries <electric charge> $+e$ and an <electron> carries $-e$, where $e$ is the positive <elementary charge>; a <neutron> carries zero net <electric charge>. Hence
$$
A=Z+N,\qquad N=A-Z,\qquad Q=(Z-n_e)e,\qquad n_e=Z-Q/e.
$$
The reasoning is just counting: add <protons> and <neutrons> for $A$, and subtract negative <electron> contributions from positive <proton> contributions for $Q$. A neutral <chemical atom> has $Q=0$, so $n_e=Z$. For a positively charged <ion>, fewer <electrons> remain. Notation ${}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm X$ gives the <mass number> at upper left and <atomic number> at lower left, with $\mathrm X$ standing for the <chemical element>; a right superscript such as $+$ specifies the <electric charge> in units of $e$. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-3-atomic-structure-and-symbolism[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, atomic structure and symbolism].

For neutral <carbon-12>, ${}^{12}_{6}\mathrm C$, the <atomic number> gives six <protons>, the subtraction $N=12-6=6$ gives six <neutrons>, and neutrality gives six <electrons>. Neutral <carbon-13>, ${}^{13}_{6}\mathrm C$, instead has $N=13-6=7$ <neutrons>, with the same six <protons> and six <electrons>. Thus these two <isotopes> occupy the same <carbon> box in the <periodic table>.

For neutral <sodium> of <mass number> $23$, ${}^{23}_{11}\mathrm{Na}$, the counts are eleven <protons>, $23-11=12$ <neutrons>, and eleven <electrons>. Removing one <electron> gives the <ion> ${}^{23}_{11}\mathrm{Na}^{+}$: $Q/e=1$, so $n_e=11-1=10$. Both its <atomic number> and its twelve <neutrons> stay unchanged. This is <ionization>, not conversion into the <chemical element> <neon>, even though a neutral <neon> <chemical atom> also has ten <electrons>.

\Image[atomic-number-isotopes-and-ions.png]
{title=<Atomic number> fixes <chemical element> identity: changing <isotopes> changes <neutrons>, while forming a <sodium> <ion> changes <electrons>}
{description=The dots are counting tokens, with <protons> and <neutrons> in an <atomic nucleus> inventory and <electrons> in a separate inventory. Their layout does not depict physical positions, sizes, or orbits. Neutral <carbon-12> and <carbon-13> differ by one <neutron>; neutral <sodium> of <mass number> 23 and its singly positive <ion> differ by one <electron>.}

This bookkeeping is useful whenever a symbol must be translated into a physical inventory. <Mass number> answers how many <protons> plus <neutrons> there are; <electric charge> answers the imbalance between <protons> and <electrons>; <atomic number> alone answers which <chemical element> it is. Keeping those questions distinct prevents an <isotope> or an <ion> from being mistaken for a different <chemical element>.

= Beryllium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Be}$}
{wiki}

Beryllium is the <chemical element> with atomic number four. Its isotope <beryllium-7> provides an important route to primordial <lithium-7>.

= Beryllium-10
{parent=Beryllium}
{title2=${}^{10}_{4}\mathrm{Be}$}

An <isotope> of <beryllium> with 4 <protons> and 6 <neutrons>. Its <nuclear spin> concerns the coupled nuclear <quantum state>, not the electronic configuration.

= Beryllium-8
{parent=Beryllium}
{title2=${}^{8}\mathrm{Be}$}

= Beryllium-7
{parent=Beryllium}
{title2=$^7\mathrm{Be}$}

Beryllium-7 contains four <protons> and three <neutrons>. It forms in reactions including $^3\mathrm{He}+{}^4\mathrm{He}\to{}^7\mathrm{Be}+\gamma$ and becomes <lithium-7> through <electron capture>. In the early universe its ionization state affects when capture occurs, so the final primordial lithium inventory includes the earlier beryllium inventory rather than just lithium present when nuclear burning ends.

= Lithium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Li}$}
{wiki}

Lithium is the <chemical element> with atomic number three. Its stable isotopes include lithium-6 and <lithium-7>. Lithium observed in old stellar atmospheres is an important but processing-sensitive test of <Big Bang nucleosynthesis>.

= Trilithium
{parent=Lithium}
{title2=$\mathrm{Li_3}$}

The Li3 molecule, with three valence electrons and a bound distorted triangular structure. The equilateral open-shell arrangement is subject to Jahn-Teller coupling; electron counting alone does not establish dissociation energetics.

= Dilithium
{parent=Lithium}
{title2=$\mathrm{Li_2}$}

The Li2 molecule. Its two valence electrons occupy a primarily 2s-derived bonding orbital; filled 1s-derived bonding and antibonding core levels do not give an additional net simple bond-order contribution.

= Lithium-7
{parent=Lithium}
{title2=$^7\mathrm{Li}$}

Lithium-7 has three <protons> and four <neutrons>. In <Big Bang nucleosynthesis> it can form directly or through <beryllium-7>, which later undergoes <electron capture>. Competition between direct lithium production, lithium destruction and beryllium production produces a valley in the primordial lithium abundance versus <baryon-to-photon ratio>. Its measured stellar surface abundance need not equal its birth abundance because stellar transport and nuclear burning can remove lithium.

= Nuclide
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=${}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm X$}
{wiki}

A nuclear species specified by <atomic number>, <mass number>, and nuclear <energy> state. <Isotopes> compare <nuclides> with the same <atomic number>; changing only the nuclear <energy> state does not create a different <isotope>.

= Isotope
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=${}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm X$}
{wiki}

<Isotopes> of a <chemical element> have the same <atomic number> $Z$, hence the same number of <protons>, but different <neutron numbers> $N$ and therefore different <mass numbers> $A=Z+N$. For example, every <neon> <atomic nucleus> contains ten <protons>: <neon-20>, <neon-21>, and <neon-22> contain ten, eleven, and twelve <neutrons>, respectively. They occupy one <chemical element> box in the <periodic table>, rather than three boxes.

\Image[isotope-nucleus-neon-neutron-counts.png]
{title=<Neon> <isotopes>: the same ten <protons> with different <neutron numbers>}
{description=Every dot counts one <nucleon>. The arrays inventory <atomic nuclei>; they do not model the positions or sizes of <protons> and <neutrons>. The <mass numbers> 20, 21, and 22 are exact counts.}

Three labels answer different questions. The <chemical element> fixes $Z$; the <isotope> specifies which <neutron number> accompanies that $Z$; the <ion> label specifies the net <electric charge>. Neutral ${}^{22}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}$ and the <ion> ${}^{22}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}^{+}$ both contain ten <protons> and twelve <neutrons>; they contain ten and nine <electrons>, respectively. Ordinary <ionization> changes the <electron> count, leaving the <isotope> unchanged. Changing the <neutron number> can change <nuclear binding energy> and the possibility of <radioactive decay>. <Isotopic abundances> can help distinguish material sources, while <radioactive decay> can additionally supply chronological information.

A <mass spectrum> makes the distinction experimentally visible: a single <chemical element> can give separate <ion> peaks at different <mass-to-charge ratios>. Its bulk <atomic weight> is a <weighted mean> governed by <isotopic abundances>, rather than the location of one compulsory peak. The historical <mass spectrograph> experiment and the synthetic <neon> <mass spectrum> below illustrate these complementary observations.

= Carbon-11
{parent=Isotope}
{title2=${}^{11}\mathrm C$}

A radioactive carbon <isotope>. Its short <half-life> makes <radioactive decay> storage relevant to waste-gas management; no biological mechanism is needed for the retention calculation.

= Oxygen-18
{parent=Isotope}
{title2=$^{18}\mathrm O$}

<Oxygen-18> is the stable <oxygen> isotope with mass number 18. <oxygen> from enriched <water> can trace the source of an <oxygen> atom in a reaction product, provided subsequent <oxygen> exchange is excluded.

= Stable isotope analysis
{parent=Isotope}

Measurement of isotopic composition using non-radioactive isotopes. Food-web and dietary interpretations require an appropriate baseline and allowance for tissue formation and physiological fractionation.

= Isotopologue
{parent=Isotope}
{wiki}

A molecular species differing from another only in its isotopic composition. Different <reduced masses> alter vibrational and rotational spacings even when the underlying electronic potential is nearly the same.

= Stable isotope labeling
{parent=Isotope}

<stable isotope labeling> uses nonradioactive <isotopes> to distinguish molecular populations. Paired labeled and unlabeled samples can be compared by <mass spectrometry>, subject to labeling completeness, mixing and measurement-response controls.

= Isotopic composition
{parent=Isotope}

The relative <isotopic abundances> in a sample.

= Mass number
{parent=Isotope}
{title2=$A=Z+N$}
{wiki}

The <mass number> $A$ is the total number of <nucleons>, namely <protons> plus <neutrons>, in an <atomic nucleus>:
$$
A=Z+N,\qquad N=A-Z.
$$
It is an exact positive <integer> with no units, rather than a measured <mass>. <Carbon-12> has $A=12$ because its <atomic nucleus> contains six <protons> and six <neutrons>. No improvement in a <mass> measurement can turn that count into $11.999$ or $12.001$.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/mass number>{ref}

The left superscript in ${}^{22}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}$ denotes <mass number> $22$, not an <atomic mass> of exactly $22\,\mathrm u$. For <neon-22>, the displayed reference <atomic mass> is about $21.991385114\,\mathrm u$. Even a <weighted mean> of <mass numbers>, such as $20.1877$ for the illustrative <neon> population below, is a population average, not the <mass number> of an individual <atomic nucleus>.<Atomic Weights and Isotopic Compositions for Neon>{ref}

= Neutron number
{parent=Mass number}
{title2=$N=A-Z$}
{wiki}

The number of <neutrons> in an <atomic nucleus>. Two <isotopes> of the same <chemical element> differ in <neutron number> while sharing an <atomic number>.

= Atomic mass
{parent=Isotope}
{title2=$m_{\rm a}$}
{wiki}

The <rest mass> of a <chemical atom> in its <ground state>, a <physical quantity> expressed in <SI> <mass> units or the <unified atomic mass unit>. Here $m_i$ means the <atomic mass> of a neutral <chemical atom> of <isotope> $i$, not the <mass> of its bare <atomic nucleus> and not a population average.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/atomic mass>{ref}

<Atomic mass> includes <electrons> and depends on <nuclear binding energy>; it is not just <mass number> times a universal <nucleon> <mass>. The <atomic mass> of neutral <carbon-12> in its <ground state> is exactly $12\,\mathrm u$ by definition of the <unified atomic mass unit>. That exactness establishes the reference scale, rather than making the <atomic masses> of all <isotopes> exact <integers> in the same unit.

= Dalton
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Atomic mass}
{title2=$\mathrm{Da}=\mathrm u$}
{wiki}

= Unified atomic mass unit
{synonym}

The <unified atomic mass unit> and <dalton (unit)> are names for the same <mass> unit:
$$
1\,\mathrm u=1\,\mathrm{Da}=\frac{m_{\rm a}({}^{12}\mathrm C)}{12},
$$
where the <carbon-12> <chemical atom> is free, at rest, and in its nuclear and electronic <ground state>. Its conversion to <SI> <mass> units is determined experimentally. See <The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure> (Table 8, footnote (f), printed p. 146: dalton and unified atomic mass unit).

= Nuclear mass defect
{parent=Atomic mass}
{title2=$\Delta m=Zm_p+Nm_n-M_{\rm nuc}$}

The <mass> deficit of a bound <atomic nucleus> relative to its separated <protons> and <neutrons>. If their <rest masses> are $m_p$ and $m_n$, the <atomic nucleus> has <rest mass> $M_{\rm nuc}$, and the positive <nuclear binding energy> is $B$, then
$$
\Delta m=Zm_p+Nm_n-M_{\rm nuc}=\frac{B}{c^2},
$$
with $c$ the <speed of light>. Releasing <energy> when the <nucleons> bind reduces the <rest mass> of the bound system without changing the exact count $A=Z+N$. A neutral <atomic mass> also includes $Z$ <electron> <rest masses>, reduced by the <energy> required to separate those bound <electrons>. Consequently $A-m_{\rm a}/\mathrm u$ is not the definition of the <nuclear mass defect>: the reference unit and constituent <rest masses> must also be accounted for.

= Relative atomic mass
{parent=Atomic mass}
{title2=$A_{\rm r}=\bar m/\mathrm u$}
{wiki}

= Atomic weight
{synonym}

For a <chemical element> in a specified population, its <relative atomic mass> or <atomic weight> is the <dimensionless> ratio $A_{\rm r}=\bar m/\mathrm u$, with $\bar m$ the mean neutral <atomic mass> weighted by <isotopic abundance>. For the illustrative <neon> population, $A_{\rm r}\approx20.1800$, whereas $\bar m\approx20.1800\,\mathrm u$ has <mass> units.<Column Descriptions for Atomic Weights and Isotopic Compositions>{ref}

= Standard atomic weight
{parent=Relative atomic mass}
{title2=$A_{\rm r}^{\circ}$}
{wiki}

A recommended <atomic weight> applicable to normal materials, rather than the result for every possible enriched sample. The tabulated <neon> value $20.1797(6)$ and the arithmetic result above need not agree in every last displayed digit. Some <chemical elements> have intervals of <standard atomic weight> because their <isotopic abundances> vary across normal materials.<Standard Atomic Weights 2024>{ref}

= Isotopic abundance
{parent=Isotope}
{title2=$f_i$}

An <isotopic abundance> is an atom fraction within a specified population of one <chemical element>.<Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book), second edition/isotopic abundance>{ref} If $n_i$ counts its <chemical atoms> of <isotope> $i$, use
$$
f_i=\frac{n_i}{\sum_j n_j},\qquad f_i\geq0,\qquad\sum_i f_i=1.
$$
The denominator includes every <isotope> of that <chemical element> included in the stated population, not every <chemical atom> of every <chemical element> in the sample. Thus an <oxygen> atom fraction among all <chemical elements> is a different quantity.

For an illustrative neutral <neon> population, take the displayed central <isotopic abundances> $f_{20}=0.9048$, $f_{21}=0.0027$, and $f_{22}=0.0925$ from the reference table, so $0.9048+0.0027+0.0925=1$. Its mean <atomic mass> is the <weighted arithmetic mean>
$$
\begin{aligned}
\bar m&=\sum_i f_i m_i\\
&=\bigl(0.9048\times19.9924401762+0.0027\times20.993846685+0.0925\times21.991385114\bigr)\,\mathrm u\\
&=20.18004638052026\,\mathrm u\approx20.1800\,\mathrm u.
\end{aligned}
$$
These are rounded reference inputs with uncertainties, not exact universal <isotopic abundances>; the long decimal shows their arithmetic result, not measurement precision.<Atomic Weights and Isotopic Compositions for Neon>{ref}

The population convention matters. If instead $w_i$ is the fraction of total neutral-atom <mass> supplied by <isotope> $i$, then $w_i=f_i m_i/\bar m$ and
$$
\bar m=\left(\sum_i\frac{w_i}{m_i}\right)^{-1}.
$$
This is a <weighted harmonic mean> in the <mass> fractions. Using $\sum_i w_i m_i$ with those <mass> fractions would answer a different question. The conversion follows directly by counting <chemical atoms> and adding their <masses>.

= Uranium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{U}$, $Z=92$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 92. Its long-lived radioactive isotopes illustrate actinide production by the <rapid neutron-capture process>.

= Alpha uranium
{parent=Uranium}
{title2=$\alpha\text{-}\mathrm U$}

The orthorhombic low-temperature phase of <uranium> metal. Its anisotropic thermal and irradiation response makes <crystal grain> <crystallographic texture> important for dimensional stability.

= Depleted uranium
{parent=Uranium}

<uranium> with less <uranium-235> than natural <uranium>. Addition to an immobilised <plutonium> inventory can dilute <uranium-235> subsequently formed by <plutonium-239> <radioactive decay>; chemical coexistence is not irreversible protection against deliberate separation.

= Uranium-239
{parent=Uranium}
{title2=$^{239}_{92}\mathrm U$}

An unstable <uranium> <isotope> produced by <neutron capture> on <uranium-238>. Two successive <beta-minus decays>, through <neptunium-239>, lead to <plutonium-239>.

= Uranium-238
{parent=Uranium}
{title2=$^{238}_{92}\mathrm U$}

A <fertile material> whose <neutron capture> produces <uranium-239>. It can undergo neutron-induced fission at sufficiently high incident neutron energies, while slow-neutron capture can instead initiate breeding of <plutonium-239>.

= Uranium-236
{parent=Uranium}
{title2=$^{236}_{92}\mathrm U$}

= Uranium-235
{parent=Uranium}
{title2=$^{235}_{92}\mathrm U$}

A <uranium> <isotope> that is a principal <fissile material> in thermal-neutron reactors. Capturing a <neutron> produces excited <uranium-236>.

= Lead
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Pb}$, $Z=82$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 82. Lead isotopes are important heavy products of the <slow neutron-capture process> in suitable neutron-exposure conditions.

= Barium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ba}$, $Z=56$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 56. Enrichment in barium can reveal transferred <slow neutron-capture process> products, as in a <barium star>.

= Californium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Cf}$}

= Californium-252
{parent=Californium}
{title2=$^{252}\mathrm{Cf}$}

A <californium> isotope used as a <neutron> calibration source for the <Sudbury Neutrino Observatory>.

= Silicon
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Si}$, $Z=14$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 14. <Oxygen burning> produces silicon-group nuclei; subsequent <silicon burning> is a reaction network approaching an iron-group mixture.

= Polysilicon
{parent=Silicon}

= Polycrystalline silicon
{synonym}

Polycrystalline <silicon>, containing many crystalline grains. Deposited <polysilicon> can form structural layers in <surface micromachining>; grain structure, doping and residual stress influence its properties.

= Magnesium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Mg}$, $Z=12$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 12. Magnesium isotopes occur among products of <carbon burning> and alpha-capture neutron-source reactions.

= Neon
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ne}$, $Z=10$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 10. <Neon burning> uses photodisintegration and alpha capture, while neon-22 can provide neutrons through $^{22}\mathrm{Ne}(\alpha,n){}^{25}\mathrm{Mg}$.

= Neon-20
{parent=Neon}
{title2=${}^{20}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}$}

The <neon> <isotope> with ten <protons> and ten <neutrons>.

= Neon-21
{parent=Neon}
{title2=${}^{21}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}$}

The <neon> <isotope> with ten <protons> and eleven <neutrons>.

= Neon-22
{parent=Neon}
{title2=${}^{22}_{10}\mathrm{Ne}$}

The <neon> <isotope> with ten <protons> and twelve <neutrons>.

= Cobalt
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Co}$, $Z=27$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 27. Cobalt-56 is the intermediate radioactive nucleus in the <nickel-56 decay chain>.

= Cobalt-60
{parent=Cobalt}
{wiki}
{title2=${}^{60}_{27}\mathrm{Co}$}

= Nickel
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ni}$, $Z=28$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 28. Radioactive nickel-56 is produced in explosive burning; the <nickel-56 decay chain> contributes energy to supernova emission.

= Nickel-56 decay chain
{parent=Nickel}
{title2=$^{56}\mathrm{Ni}\longrightarrow{}^{56}\mathrm{Co}\longrightarrow{}^{56}\mathrm{Fe}$}

The radioactive chain converts nickel-56 through cobalt-56 into iron-56, releasing energy that can power supernova light curves. <Electron capture> and, where allowed, <beta-plus decay> convert protons into neutrons; gamma rays and charged decay products deposit some of the released energy. Ionization and escape of decay radiation affect the observed heating.

= Manganese
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Mn}$, $Z=25$}

= Iron
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Fe}$, $Z=26$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 26. Iron-group <nuclear binding energy> and <silicon burning> are central to the termination of ordinary energy-producing stellar fusion.

= Oxygen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm O$}
{wiki}

= Oxygen-17
{parent=Oxygen}
{title2=${}^{17}_{8}\mathrm O$}

An <isotope> of <oxygen> with 8 <protons> and 9 <neutrons>. Its <nuclear spin> concerns the coupled nuclear <quantum state>, not the electronic configuration.

= Oxygen singlet D atom
{parent=Oxygen}
{title2=$\mathrm O({}^1D)$}

An electronically excited atomic oxygen state produced in part by <ozone> <photodissociation>. Reaction with <water> can yield two <hydroxyl radicals>, while collisional quenching returns the atom to its triplet ground term without the same <radical> production. Its short-lived steady balance is distinct from the lifetime of <ozone> or <water>.

= Atomic oxygen
{parent=Oxygen}

= Electronically excited oxygen atom
{parent=Atomic oxygen}
{title2=$\mathrm O({}^1D)$}

An electronically excited atomic-oxygen state that can be quenched collisionally or react with species such as water. Quenching back to the ground state and radical production compete, so the photochemical source rate need not equal the radical source rate.

= Anoxia
{parent=Oxygen}

Absence of available <oxygen> in a stated environment. It can change decay and mineralization pathways but does not stop all microbial activity.

= Singlet oxygen
{parent=Oxygen}

<Singlet oxygen> is an electronically excited form of molecular oxygen with reactivity distinct from ground-state oxygen. Energy transfer from triplet chlorophyll can generate it during photosynthetic excitation stress.

= Oxygen-16
{parent=Oxygen}
{title2=$^{16}\mathrm O$}

= Molecular oxygen
{parent=Oxygen}
{title2=$\mathrm O_2$}

The diatomic form of <oxygen>. Modern terrestrial abundance is maintained largely by oxygenic <photosynthesis>, balanced by chemical and biological sinks.

= Nitrogen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm N$}
{wiki}

= Nitrogen-15
{parent=Nitrogen}

= Nitrogen-14
{parent=Nitrogen}
{title2=${}^{14}_{7}\mathrm N$}

The <nitrogen> <isotope> with seven <protons> and seven <neutrons>.

= Carbon
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

= Carbon-14
{parent=Carbon}
{wiki}
{title2=$^{14}\mathrm C$}

<Carbon-14> has six <protons> and eight <neutrons>. Atmospheric production is dominated by $^{14}\mathrm N(n,p){}^{14}\mathrm C$ using secondary <cosmic-ray> <neutrons>. It undergoes <beta-minus decay> to <nitrogen-14>, with a physical <half-life> of approximately $5730$ years.

= Diamond
{parent=Carbon}
{wiki}

= Graphite
{parent=Carbon}
{wiki}

A layered <solid> form of <carbon>, with <covalent bonds> joining <chemical atoms> within each layer.

= Graphite intercalation compound
{parent=Graphite}
{wiki}

A layered material with guest species inserted between <graphite> sheets. Alkali-metal guests can donate <electron> density to graphitic bands, altering carrier <concentration>, spacing and scattering.

= Potassium graphite
{parent=Graphite intercalation compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{KC_8}$}

A <potassium graphite> intercalation compound, with KC-eight a common stage-one composition. Charge transfer from <potassium> to the graphitic pi system can enhance <electrical conductivity>.

= Graphene
{parent=Carbon}
{wiki}

<Graphene> is a single layer of <carbon> <chemical atoms> arranged in a <honeycomb lattice>. A nearby gate can select predominantly <electron> or <electron hole> conduction, as demonstrated by the <graphene exfoliation and field-effect experiments>.<Electric Field Effect in Atomically Thin Carbon Films by Konstantin Novoselov and others>[pp.1–2, mechanically cleaved films, gate and Hall response]{ref}

A useful question is how geometry changes motion. The <nearest-neighbour model of graphene> starts with three equivalent bonds from each site and two sites per primitive cell. Interference between those three hopping paths makes two <energy bands> meet; near their meeting, <energy> grows linearly with distance in <wave vector>. The resulting <Dirac cones> explain why an atomic sheet can imitate one aspect of a relativistic particle while remaining an ordinary material with contacts, defects and boundaries.

= Graphene zone-boundary band separation
{parent=Graphene}

The local <energy> difference between the conduction and <valence bands> along the boundary of the first <Brillouin zone>. In a nearest-neighbour model it vanishes at the corner valleys and reaches $2t$ at edge midpoints; this angular variation is compatible with a globally gapless material.

= Liquid-phase exfoliation
{parent=Graphene}

Separating a layered material into dispersed thin flakes by mechanical processing in a liquid. For <graphite>, liquid choice, shear or sonication, classification and recovery jointly control the <graphene> flake distribution. Preserving the lattice does not guarantee a high monolayer yield or a clean final electrode.<High-yield production of graphene by liquid-phase exfoliation of graphite by Yenny Hernandez and others>[2008, preparation and characterization]{ref}

= Reduced graphene oxide
{parent=Graphene}

= rGO
{c}
{synonym}

<graphene> <oxide> that has undergone partial removal of oxygen-containing groups; residual functional groups and lattice defects remain.

= Bilayer graphene
{parent=Graphene}
{wiki}

Two coupled <graphene> layers. Their electronic bands depend on relative stacking and external electrostatic conditions, rather than on layer count alone.

= Twisted bilayer graphene
{parent=Bilayer graphene}
{wiki}

<Bilayer graphene> with a relative rotation between the layers. Hybridization of displaced <Dirac cones> creates angle-dependent bands and optical resonances.

= Nearest-neighbour model of graphene
{parent=Graphene}
{title2=$E_\pm(\mathbf k)=\pm t|f(\mathbf k)|$}

Take a planar <honeycomb lattice> with two sites $A,B$ per primitive cell and one localized <atomic orbital> per site. In this explicitly idealized <tight-binding model>, all three nearest-neighbour hopping matrix elements are $-t$, with $t>0$; there is no on-site offset or further-neighbour hopping. Ignore <electron> interactions and treat physical <spin> as a separate degeneracy. Let the bond length be $b$, and choose the three vectors from an $A$ site to its neighbours as
$$
\boldsymbol\delta_1=b(0,1),\qquad
\boldsymbol\delta_2=b(\sqrt3/2,-1/2),\qquad
\boldsymbol\delta_3=b(-\sqrt3/2,-1/2).
$$
The <Bloch state> amplitudes on $A,B$ form a two-component <vector>. A consistent phase convention gives the <Hamiltonian>
$$
H(\mathbf k)=-t\begin{pmatrix}0&f(\mathbf k)\\f(\mathbf k)^*&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
f(\mathbf k)=\sum_{j=1}^3e^{i\mathbf k\cdot\boldsymbol\delta_j}
=e^{ibk_y}+2e^{-ibk_y/2}\cos(\sqrt3bk_x/2).
$$
Its characteristic equation is $E^2=t^2|f|^2$, giving the two displayed <energy bands>. The two-component structure comes from the two sites, rather than two values of physical <spin>. This is a particularly small matrix model in which a crystal's geometry creates an unexpected low-energy law.

\Image[graphene-honeycomb-two-band-dirac-cone.png]
{title=A honeycomb lattice produces two touching electronic bands}
{description=Original nearest-neighbour model with the defined bond vectors. The surface shows the calculated bands near one zero of $f$, with distance in units of inverse bond length and energy in units of the hopping magnitude. These are model energies, not measured band data.}
{source=graphene-honeycomb-two-band-dirac-cone.py}

Historically, <Philip Russell Wallace> used a <tight-binding model> to investigate the <electronic band structure> of <graphite> in 1947. The material in that paper was layered <graphite>; the single-sheet model here isolates one simpler part of the problem.<The Band Theory of Graphite by Philip Russell Wallace>[Publisher abstract: tight-binding bands and Brillouin zones of graphite; published 1 May 1947]{ref}

= Dirac valleys in graphene
{c}
{parent=Nearest-neighbour model of graphene}
{title2=$\mathbf K_\tau=(\tau4\pi/(3\sqrt3b),0),\quad\tau=\pm1$}

At either displayed <wave vector>, the three complex hopping amplitudes add to zero: $1+2\cos(2\pi/3)=0$. Therefore the <energy bands> touch. There are two inequivalent <electronic valleys>; other equivalent corners are related by reciprocal-lattice translations. Setting $\mathbf k=\mathbf K_\tau+\mathbf q$ and retaining first-order terms gives
$$
H_\tau(\mathbf q)\simeq\hbar v_F(\tau q_x\sigma_x+q_y\sigma_y),\qquad
v_F=\frac{3tb}{2\hbar},\qquad E_\pm\simeq\pm\hbar v_F|\mathbf q|,
$$
where $\sigma_x,\sigma_y$ are <Pauli matrices>. For example, at $\mathbf K_+$, $f\simeq-3b(q_x-iq_y)/2$. The matrix has the form of a massless two-dimensional <Dirac Hamiltonian>. Here $v_F$ is a material-model <velocity>, rather than the vacuum <speed of light>; this description does not change an <electron>'s vacuum <rest mass>. It describes the <dispersion relation> of electronic excitations near a band crossing.

= Sublattice pseudospin in graphene
{parent=Dirac valleys in graphene}
{title2=$\langle\boldsymbol\sigma\rangle=(\cos\phi,\sin\phi,0)$}

At $\mathbf K_+$ and positive <energy>, a normalized <eigenvector> for $\mathbf q=|\mathbf q|(\cos\phi,\sin\phi)$ is $(1,e^{i\phi})/\sqrt2$. Its <Pauli matrix> expectation values give the displayed direction. This <pseudospin> records the relative amplitudes and phase on the two sublattices. Real <spin> supplies another label; confusing these two labels would double-count states and misinterpret the two-band <Hamiltonian>.

= Carrier density in a graphene cone
{parent=Dirac valleys in graphene}
{title2=$n=\operatorname{sgn}(E_F)E_F^2/[\pi(\hbar v_F)^2]$}

Assume zero <temperature>, the linear <Dirac cone>, and both real-<spin> states and both <electronic valleys>. Define $n$ as excess <electron> number per unit <area>, negative for an excess of <electron holes>, relative to neutrality. A filled circular region of radius $k_F$ in <wave vector> space contains
$$
|n|=4\frac{\pi k_F^2}{(2\pi)^2}=\frac{k_F^2}{\pi},\qquad |E_F|=\hbar v_F k_F.
$$
Thus the excess <carrier density> grows quadratically with the <Fermi energy>, unlike the constant <density of states> of a parabolic two-dimensional band. Differentiating gives the positive <density of states> per unit <area>, $D(E)=2|E|/[\pi(\hbar v_F)^2]$. Both the geometry of occupied states and the fourfold degeneracy matter to the coefficient.

= Cyclotron mass in a graphene cone
{parent=Dirac valleys in graphene}
{title2=$m_c=|E_F|/v_F^2$}

A massless-cone description permits a nonzero <cyclotron effective mass>. For a circular constant-<energy> contour of <area> $S(E)=\pi E^2/(\hbar v_F)^2$, the semiclassical definition gives
$$
m_c=\frac{\hbar^2}{2\pi}\left|\frac{dS}{dE}\right|=\frac{|E_F|}{v_F^2}=\frac{\hbar\sqrt{\pi|n|}}{v_F}.
$$
The vanishing mass parameter in a <Dirac Hamiltonian> and the finite cyclotron response away from neutrality are different statements. This makes the <temperature> dependence of <Shubnikov-de Haas oscillations> a useful test of the dispersion rather than a measurement of an <electron>'s vacuum <rest mass>.

= Graphene exfoliation and field-effect experiments
{parent=Graphene}
{title2=2004–2005}

<Andre Geim>, <Konstantin Novoselov> and collaborators mechanically cleaved <graphite>, identified thin flakes, and fabricated multi-terminal <Hall bars> on oxidized <silicon>. A substrate gate changed the <carrier density>; longitudinal <electrical resistance> and the <Hall coefficient> supplied different checks. The 2004 report included one-, two- and three-layer devices and described a few-layer semimetal interpretation. Layer identification and electrical control were separate experimental achievements.<Electric Field Effect in Atomically Thin Carbon Films by Konstantin Novoselov and others>[pp.1–2 and Figs.1–2: preparation, thickness selection and electrical measurements]{ref}

The 2005 monolayer study used an <optical microscope>, <electron microscopy> and <atomic force microscopy> to select monolayers. It then measured <Shubnikov-de Haas oscillations> as <temperature>, gate voltage and <magnetic field> varied. Their damping inferred a density-dependent <cyclotron effective mass>; comparison with oscillation frequency tested a linear <dispersion relation>. A comparison with two-layer devices also distinguished their <quantum Hall effect> sequences. These are complementary tests of the monolayer model, rather than consequences of finding a thin-looking flake alone.<Two-Dimensional Gas of Massless Dirac Fermions in Graphene by Konstantin Novoselov and others>[pp.1–3, Figs.2–4: monolayer selection, oscillation damping and bilayer comparison]{ref}

= Gate control of graphene carrier density
{parent=Graphene}
{title2=$V_g-V_0=en/c_g+E_F/e$}

Consider an ideal uniform <graphene> sheet connected to an <electron> reservoir, with an insulated gate. Let $c_g>0$ be geometric <capacitance> per unit <area>, $e>0$ the <elementary charge>, and $V_0$ the neutral-point offset. Neglect <temperature>, trapped <electric charge> and spatial inhomogeneity. A change in gate voltage pays for both the electrostatic potential drop, $en/c_g$, and the change in the sheet's <Fermi energy>, $E_F/e$. Here $E_F$ is measured relative to the band crossing; it is negative for excess <electron holes>.

Combine this relation with the <carrier density in a graphene cone>. Write $u=|E_F|/e$, measured in volts, $V=|V_g-V_0|$, and $\alpha=e^3/[\pi(\hbar v_F)^2c_g]$. Then
$$
V=u+\alpha u^2,\qquad u=\frac{2V}{1+\sqrt{1+4\alpha V}},\qquad
n=\operatorname{sgn}(V_g-V_0)\frac{e^2u^2}{\pi(\hbar v_F)^2}.
$$
The positive root is unique because $u+\alpha u^2$ is strictly increasing for $u\ge0$. The often useful geometric approximation $n\simeq c_g(V_g-V_0)/e$ neglects the <Fermi energy> term. It overestimates $|n|$ in this model, especially near neutrality where relatively few states are available.

For a deliberately chosen model with $c_g=0.010\,\mathrm{F/m^2}$, $v_F=10^6\,\mathrm{m/s}$, $V_0=0$ and $V_g=1\,\mathrm V$, the formulas give **$E_F\simeq0.252\,\mathrm{eV}$ and $n\simeq4.67\times10^{12}\,\mathrm{cm^{-2}}$**. The purely geometric value would be $6.24\times10^{12}\,\mathrm{cm^{-2}}$. These are chosen-model predictions, not measurements of the historical devices. Numerical conversion uses the exact <Planck constant> and <elementary charge>.<Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>[Exact h and e entries; material parameters in this example are stipulated]{ref}

\Image[graphene-gate-control-and-quantum-capacitance.png]
{title=Gate control changes graphene's carrier population and differential capacitance}
{description=Original functional device sketch and calculated zero-temperature curves for the explicitly chosen model. Signed $n$ counts excess electrons; negative $n$ counts holes. The dashed density curve neglects the change in Fermi energy. These are not historical transport data.}
{source=graphene-gate-control-and-quantum-capacitance.py}

= Quantum capacitance of a graphene cone
{parent=Gate control of graphene carrier density}
{title2=$c_q=2e^2|E_F|/[\pi(\hbar v_F)^2]$}

The <quantum capacitance> per unit <area> is $c_q=e^2D(E_F)$. Differentiating the gate equation gives
$$
\frac{1}{c_{\rm diff}}=\frac{1}{c_g}+\frac{1}{c_q},\qquad
c_{\rm diff}=e\frac{dn}{dV_g}.
$$
Thus the available electronic states act as a second contribution in series with geometric <capacitance>. At exact neutrality the ideal zero-<temperature> model has $c_q=c_{\rm diff}=0$; actual finite-<temperature> or spatially nonuniform samples need a broader description. A capacitance measurement can therefore probe the <density of states>, rather than geometry alone.

= Carbon nanotube
{parent=Carbon}
{wiki}

= Ideal carbon nanotube tenacity
{parent=Carbon nanotube}

A strength estimate formed by dividing the maximum axial <force> supported by load-bearing carbon bonds by the tube <mass> per length. Equal bond loading and a defect-free, fully participating shell are strong assumptions; network fibres can be limited by intertube shear and sliding.

= Carbon nanotube forest
{parent=Carbon nanotube}

A dense population of approximately aligned <carbon nanotubes> growing from a support. Collective crowding can favor alignment even when the support does not epitaxially select every tube orientation.

= Kataura plot
{parent=Carbon nanotube}
{c}
{wiki}

A plot of optical transition energies against <carbon nanotube> diameter, usually organized by wrapping indices and electronic type. It relates optical and resonant <Raman spectroscopy> observations to nanotube structure.

= Multi-walled carbon nanotube
{parent=Carbon nanotube}

A <carbon nanotube> comprising several nested graphitic walls. Each shell can have its own wrapping indices; their difference alone is not a structural defect.

= Nanotube atom-count estimate
{parent=Multi-walled carbon nanotube}

An area-based estimate treating each nanotube shell as a <graphene> cylinder. For <graphene> atomic area density $n_s$ and shell radii $r_j$, the carbon count per axial length is $N_C/\ell=2\pi n_s\sum_jr_j$. Defects and partial shells change the estimate.

= Single-walled carbon nanotube
{parent=Carbon nanotube}

A <carbon nanotube> comprising one rolled <graphene> wall. The wrapping indices determine its allowed circumferential electronic wave vectors and therefore its metallic or semiconducting band structure.

= Chirality-sorted carbon nanotube
{parent=Single-walled carbon nanotube}

An enriched population selected by wrapping indices or electronic type. Sorting can narrow optical transition distributions or remove metallic leakage paths from semiconductor networks. Purity, yield, residual sorting agents and the processed-device environment remain separate performance variables.

= Nanotube optical transition
{parent=Single-walled carbon nanotube}
{title2=$E_{ii}$}

An optical excitation between nanotube electronic subbands, often labelled $E_{ii}$ for prominent axial-polarization transitions. One-dimensional <Van Hove singularities> enhance the <joint density of states>, but <selection rules>, <oscillator strengths> and strong <excitons> interactions determine actual absorption peaks. <Dielectric> environment, bundling, doping and defects can change those peaks.

= Nanotube zone folding
{parent=Single-walled carbon nanotube}

An approximation that samples the <graphene> bands on wave vectors satisfying $\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf C_h=2\pi\ell$, where $\mathbf C_h$ is the <carbon nanotube chiral vector>. It produces one-dimensional subbands and the ideal metallicity criterion $n-m\equiv0\pmod3$; curvature and interactions can modify its predictions.

= Carbon-nanotube Dirac mass
{parent=Nanotube zone folding}
{title2=$m_{\rm eff}=\hbar|q_\perp|/v_F$}

Circumferential momentum quantization of a <graphene> <Dirac cone> produces nanotube dispersion $E_\pm(q_\parallel)=\pm\sqrt{(\hbar v_Fq_\parallel)^2+(\hbar v_Fq_\perp)^2}$. Comparing with a massive one-dimensional <Dirac Hamiltonian> identifies the displayed <effective mass> and gap $2m_{\rm eff}v_F^2$. This is an emergent band mass, not a changed vacuum <electron> rest mass. A quantization line through the valley gives zero mass in the ideal zone-folding model; curvature, strain and interactions can change that idealization.

= Carbon nanotube chiral vector
{parent=Single-walled carbon nanotube}
{title2=$\mathbf C_h$}

The <graphene> translation $\mathbf C_h=n\mathbf a_1+m\mathbf a_2$ that becomes a tube circumference when its endpoints are identified. It determines wrapping indices and diameter $|\mathbf C_h|/\pi$; it is perpendicular to the tube axis.

= Carbon nanotube chiral angle
{parent=Carbon nanotube chiral vector}
{title2=$\theta$}

The angle of the wrapping vector from the zigzag direction of <graphene>. For chiral indices $0\le m\le n$, $\theta=\tan^{-1}[\sqrt3m/(2n+m)]$, ranging from zero for zigzag tubes to 30 degrees for armchair tubes. Together with diameter, it determines the discrete wrapping geometry, but does not alone determine the observed colour of a nanotube suspension.

= Semiconducting carbon nanotube
{parent=Single-walled carbon nanotube}

In ideal <nanotube zone folding>, a <single-walled carbon nanotube> with indices $(n,m)$ satisfying $n-m\not\equiv0\pmod3$. Circumferential quantization misses the <Dirac valleys in graphene>, opening a band gap.

= Carbon-12
{parent=Carbon}
{title2=$^{12}\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

= Carbon-13
{parent=Carbon}
{title2=$^{13}\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

The <carbon> <isotope> with six <protons> and seven <neutrons>. A neutral <carbon-13> <chemical atom> has six <electrons>.

= Helium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{He}$}
{wiki}

= Helium atom
{parent=Helium}

A neutral <helium atom> contains two <electrons> bound to a <nucleus> of charge $+2e$. Even with a fixed <nucleus> and nonrelativistic kinetics, the repulsion $e^2/(4\pi\epsilon_0r_{12})$ prevents exact separation into two hydrogenic one-electron problems. Its ground configuration is $1s^2$ with total <spin> zero, while distinct occupied orbitals allow both <singlet helium states> and <triplet helium states>.

= Helium metastability
{parent=Helium atom}

The low $1s2s$ levels of a <helium atom> lack an allowed direct electric dipole decay to the ground $1s^2\,{}^1S_0$ level. The triplet is excluded by the <spin selection rule>, and the singlet-to-singlet $S$ transition is excluded by <parity> and orbital selection. Weaker radiative channels and collisions can still de-excite these states; metastable does not mean infinitely lived.

= Singlet-triplet exchange splitting in helium
{parent=Helium atom}
{title2=$E_S-E_T=2K_{ab}$}

For fixed orthonormal orbitals $a,b$, symmetrizing or antisymmetrizing $a(1)b(2)$ gives electron-repulsion expectations $J_{ab}+K_{ab}$ and $J_{ab}-K_{ab}$. Antisymmetry of the complete electronic state therefore places the singlet above the triplet by $2K_{ab}$, with $K_{ab}$ the positive Coulomb <exchange integral>. Orbital relaxation and further correlation refine this comparison. Exchange splitting here is a consequence of <Fermi statistics> and the electrostatic interaction, not a separate classical spin-spin force.

= Triplet helium state
{parent=Helium atom}
{title2=$S=1$}

= Orthohelium
{synonym}

A <helium atom> electronic state with total <spin> one. Its symmetric <spin> function requires an antisymmetric spatial <wavefunction>, excluding two <electrons> in the same spatial orbital. The three <spin> projections are magnetic substates of the same <spin> multiplet, not three separately distinguishable particles.

= Singlet helium state
{parent=Helium atom}
{title2=$S=0$}

= Parahelium
{synonym}

A <helium atom> electronic state with total <spin> zero. Its antisymmetric two-spin function requires a symmetric spatial <wavefunction>. Its approximate spectroscopic family is distinct from the triplet family under spin-conserving <electric dipole transitions>.

= Singly excited helium state
{parent=Helium atom}

A bound-state configuration with one <electron> predominantly in $1s$ and the other in a different orbital $n\ell$. The two assignments of identical <electrons> to these orbitals must be combined with the appropriate <exchange symmetry of two identical spin-one-half fermions>. Symmetric spatial combinations accompany the <singlet state>; antisymmetric combinations accompany the <triplet state>.

= Helium-3
{parent=Helium}
{title2=$^3\mathrm{He}$}
{wiki}

This stable <helium> isotope contains two <protons> and one <neutron>. In solar proton-proton burning it is an intermediate: two such nuclei can form <helium-4> and return two <protons>, or one can capture <helium-4> to enter the second chain branch. The balance between production and destruction determines the <helium-3 equilibrium abundance> and its <helium-3 relaxation time>.

= Helium-4
{parent=Helium}
{title2=$^4\mathrm{He}$}

Helium-4 is the stable helium isotope with two protons and two neutrons; its nucleus is an alpha particle. Its strong binding makes it the principal neutron-containing product of primordial <Big Bang nucleosynthesis>.

= Hydrogen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

= Metallic hydrogen
{parent=Hydrogen}
{wiki}

Metallic hydrogen is a high-pressure conducting state of <hydrogen>. Its fluid form in the deep interiors of <gas giants> supplies a conducting medium for a <planetary dynamo>, unlike the iron-alloy conducting cores of <terrestrial planets>.

= Deuterium
{parent=Hydrogen}
{title2=$^2\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

This stable <hydrogen> isotope has one <proton> and one <neutron>. In the <Proton–proton chain> it is produced by the slow weak proton-pair reaction and is rapidly consumed by <proton> capture to make <helium-3>. Its short nuclear lifetime inside a stellar core allows a <deuterium quasi-equilibrium in proton-proton burning> approximation.

= Hydrogen anion
{parent=Hydrogen}
{title2=$\mathrm H^-$}
{wiki}

= Negative hydrogen ion
{synonym}

A negative hydrogen ion is a <hydrogen> nucleus with two bound electrons. Its weakly bound extra electron permits absorption at visible and infrared wavelengths in cool stellar atmospheres.

= Halogen
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}

= Iodine
{parent=Halogen}
{title2=$\mathrm I$}
{wiki}

= Molybdenum
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=Mo}

= Gold
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Au}$}
{parent=Chemical element}

= Cadmium
{title2=$\mathrm{Cd}$}
{parent=Chemical element}

= Silver
{title2=$\mathrm{Ag}$}
{parent=Chemical element}

= Molecular nitrogen
{title2=$\mathrm{N}_2$}
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Molecular hydrogen
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2$}
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Solid solution
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Solid-solution strengthening
{parent=Solid solution}

Resistance to <dislocation> motion caused by substitutional or interstitial solutes and their interactions with the <dislocation> <strain> field and core.

= Vegard's law
{c}
{parent=Solid solution}
{title2=$a(x)\simeq(1-x)a_A+xa_B$}

An approximate linear interpolation of a substitutional <solid solution>'s <lattice parameter> with atomic composition. Converting the inferred <atomic fraction> to <mass fraction> requires molar masses. Ordering, nonideal mixing, <strain> and multiple phases can invalidate a simple two-endpoint interpolation.

= Solid-solution limit
{parent=Solid solution}

The composition boundary beyond which one homogeneous phase cannot accommodate additional solute under the stated conditions. Phase separation or a new structure can appear beyond that range.

= Charge compensation in a crystal
{parent=Solid solution}

Coupled changes in <ions>, <oxidation states> or vacancies that preserve charge balance when a lattice substitution changes formal charge.

= Isovalent substitution
{parent=Solid solution}

Replacement of a lattice <ion> by another of the same formal charge. <charge neutrality> alone does not ensure unlimited solubility; size and coordination preferences also matter.

= Terminal solid solution
{parent=Solid solution}

A <solid solution> adjoining a pure-component end of a composition <phase diagram>. Its solute content can be small but need not be zero. A phase label such as (Bi) therefore does not by itself mean exactly pure <bismuth>.

= Aluminium gallium arsenide
{parent=Solid solution}
{title2=$\mathrm{Al}_x\mathrm{Ga}_{1-x}\mathrm{As}$}
{tag=Semiconductor}

= AlGaAs
{c}
{synonym}

A <semiconductor> <solid solution> whose composition changes its <band gap>.

= Chemical compound
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Lithium sulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{Li_2S}$}

= Iron disulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{FeS_2}$}

The compound with formula $\mathrm{FeS_2}$. Its conversion with metallic <lithium> consumes four Li per formula unit and can form <iron> and two <lithium> sulfide units; that <electron> inventory sets an ideal <battery> capacity.

= Lead sulfate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{PbSO_4}$}

The sparingly soluble sulfate $\mathrm{PbSO_4}$. Its precipitation can retain <lead> in <anode slime> in sulfate-based <copper> <electrorefining>, but an adherent layer can also contribute to <passivation>.

= Nickel aluminide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ni_3Al}$}

The intermetallic compound $\mathrm{Ni_3Al}$, commonly with the <L12 structure>. In <superalloys> substitution changes its precise composition.

= Barium fluoride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{BaF_2}$}

An ionic compound with the <fluorite structure> at ordinary conditions.

= Calcium fluoride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{CaF_2}$}

An ionic compound with the <fluorite structure> at ordinary conditions.

= Boron carbide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{B_4C}$}

A <boron>–carbon ceramic used as a <neutron> absorber, commonly represented approximately by $\mathrm{B_4C}$. <neutron> capture products and irradiation damage affect its usable lifetime.

= Barium titanate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{BaTiO_3}$}
{wiki}

An oxide with a <perovskite structure>. Near room temperature its tetragonal phase has switchable <electric polarization> and is <ferroelectric>. Its higher-temperature cubic phase is <paraelectric>.

= Sulfur dioxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{SO_2}$}

The <molecule> $\mathrm{SO_2}$ is emitted by volcanism and combustion and can be oxidized into <sulfuric acid>/<sulfate>. Direct gas deposition competes with gas-phase and aqueous <oxidation>. The precursor-gas lifetime and the resulting particle lifetime are different quantities.

= Zirconium hydride
{parent=Chemical compound}

A hydrogen-containing zirconium phase that can precipitate from hydrogen-bearing <zirconium alloys>. Brittle hydride platelets and their orientation affect cracking resistance; their behavior differs from dissolved hydrogen.

= Ammonia borane
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{NH_3BH_3}$}

A <Lewis acid-base adduct> in which the <ammonia> nitrogen <lone pair> donates into the empty boron-centered acceptor orbital of <borane>. A <coordinate covalent bond> links nitrogen and boron.

= Carbon monosulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{CS}$}

A heteronuclear <diatomic molecule> consisting of <carbon> and <sulfur>. Its permanent <electric dipole moment> permits an electric-dipole <rotational spectrum>. The <linear rigid rotor> model relates its <rotational constant> to its <reduced mass> and bond length.

= Phosphonium salt
{parent=Chemical compound}

A salt containing a tetracoordinate positively charged phosphorus centre. Deprotonation of a suitable <carbon> substituent gives a <phosphorus ylide>.

= Phosphonate ester
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Phosphonoacetate
{parent=Phosphonate ester}

A phosphonate with an adjacent ester-bearing <carbon> that can supply the <carbon> <nucleophile> in a Horner–Wadsworth–Emmons <olefination>.

= Cyclic compound
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Sodium triacetoxyborohydride
{parent=Chemical compound}

$\mathrm{NaBH(OAc)_3}$, a <hydride> reagent often used for iminium-selective <reductive amination>.

= 5-Aminohexanoic acid
{parent=Chemical compound}

The compound $\mathrm{HO_2C(CH_2)_3CH(NH_2)Me}$. Regioselected ring expansion of 2-methylcyclopentanone followed by <lactam> hydrolysis is a constitutional synthetic disconnection.

= Molybdenum(III) chloride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\alpha\text{-}\mathrm{MoCl_3}$}

In the alpha form, MoCl3 has edge-sharing octahedral layers distorted by Mo–Mo dimer formation. Metal-centred <electron> pairing contrasts with the more localized d electrons of CrCl3.

= Molybdenum(II) chloride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{MoCl_2}$}

The solid empirical compound MoCl2 contains octahedral Mo6 cluster units with inner face-capping chlorides and intercluster outer <chloride> links. An informative connectivity formula is $\mathrm{Mo_6Cl_8Cl_2Cl_{4/2}}$.

= Borane
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Boron hydride
{synonym}

A <boron> <hydride>. Many <boranes> have <multicentre bonding>, so their composition and cage geometry cannot be reconstructed using only independent ordinary two-centre B–B bonds.

= Dodecaborate dianion
{parent=Borane}
{title2=$[\mathrm{B_{12}H_{12}}]^{2-}$}

$[\mathrm{B_{12}H_{12}}]^{2-}$ has 26 skeletal electrons and a <closo> icosahedral B12 framework.

= Carborane
{parent=Borane}

A borane-derived cluster containing skeletal <carbon> vertices. In a simple neutral count, a CH vertex contributes three skeletal electrons while BH contributes two.

= Metallacarborane
{parent=Carborane}

A carborane-derived cage with one or more metal vertices. An external ligand such as cyclopentadienyl is not counted as five additional cage vertices.

= Cobaltacarborane
{parent=Metallacarborane}

A <metallacarborane> containing a <cobalt> vertex. CpCo can replace BH in a suitable isolobal skeletal-electron count.

= Dicarbollide ligand
{parent=Carborane}
{title2=$[\mathrm{C_2B_9H_{11}}]^{2-}$}

The open-face dianion $[\mathrm{C_2B_9H_{11}}]^{2-}$ can bind through its five-membered open face as an $\eta^5$, six-electron donor. Capping this face with a metal fragment completes a 12-vertex <metallacarborane>; two faces can also support a bis(dicarbollide) metal donor.

= Closo carborane
{parent=Carborane}

A closed deltahedral <carborane>. Thermal conversion of <nido> precursors may change <hydrogen> content or cage size as well as <carbon> positions.

= Nido carborane
{parent=Carborane}

An open <carborane> cage with the <nido> skeletal-pair count. Mild alkyne–<borane> reactions can preserve adjacency of newly incorporated <carbon> atoms.

= Hexaborane(12)
{parent=Borane}
{title2=$\mathrm{B_6H_{12}}$}

$\mathrm{B_6H_{12}}$, a six-vertex <arachno> <borane> with nine skeletal pairs. Bridge deprotonation gives the <undecahydrohexaborate anion>.

= Undecahydrohexaborate anion
{parent=Borane}
{title2=$[\mathrm{B_6H_{11}}]^-$}

$[\mathrm{B_6H_{11}}]^-$, an <arachno> <borane> anion with nine skeletal pairs. A localized BH3-adduct drawing does not describe its lowest-energy fluxional cage adequately.

= Octahydropentaborate anion
{parent=Borane}
{title2=$[\mathrm{B_5H_8}]^-$}

$[\mathrm{B_5H_8}]^-$, the bridge-deprotonated conjugate base of <pentaborane(9)>. Its five-vertex framework has seven skeletal pairs and is <nido>.

= Pentaborane(9)
{parent=Borane}
{title2=$\mathrm{B_5H_9}$}

$\mathrm{B_5H_9}$, a <nido> <borane> with a square-pyramidal <boron> skeleton. A bridging <proton> can be removed to form $[\mathrm{B_5H_8}]^-$.

= Phosphorus pentafluoride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{PF_5}$}

$\mathrm{PF_5}$ is trigonal bipyramidal at its equilibrium minimum. Its twelve small-amplitude vibrational coordinates transform as $2A_1\prime+3E\prime+2A_2\prime\prime+E\prime\prime$ under D3h.

= Dioxetane
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_2H_4O_2}$}

A four-membered peroxide ring containing two adjacent <oxygen> atoms and two <carbon> atoms. The parent 1,2-dioxetane has formula $\mathrm{C_2H_4O_2}$; its <ground state> is normally described as a closed-shell singlet.

= Isoprene
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_2=C(CH_3)CH=CH_2}$}

= Dinitrogen pentoxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{N_2O_5}$}

A reservoir linked to nitrogen dioxide and nitrate radical by reversible association. Conversion to nitric acid, especially through heterogeneous uptake on wet particles, can remove reactive nitrogen from the gas-phase family.

= Peroxyacetyl nitrate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3C(O)OONO_2}$}

= PAN
{c}
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{synonym}

An organic nitrogen reservoir formed by association of a peroxyacetyl radical with nitrogen dioxide. Thermal dissociation can release reactive nitrogen again; its temperature sensitivity helps distinguish transport through cool air from rapid recycling in warmer air.

= Trimethylaluminium
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{Al_2(CH_3)_6}$}

The <aluminium> methyl compound commonly forming $\mathrm{Al_2Me_6}$ with four terminal <methyl groups> and two electron-deficient methyl bridges. Variable-temperature NMR distinguishes slow and fast site exchange.

= Boron trichloride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{BCl_3}$}

Molecular $\mathrm{BCl_3}$ has a trigonal-planar sigma framework and ligand-to-boron <pi donation>. Association energetics differ from those of <aluminium chloride>.

= Peroxy acid
{parent=Chemical compound}

A compound with an acidic peroxide group; peroxycarboxylic acids contain $\mathrm{-C(=O)OOH}$ and can transfer <oxygen> to an <alkene>.

= Meta-chloroperoxybenzoic acid
{parent=Peroxy acid}

A commonly used <epoxidation> reagent; its carbons are not incorporated into the alkene-derived <epoxide>.

= Nitrile
{parent=Chemical compound}

A compound containing a carbon-attached $\mathrm{-C\equiv N}$ group. Organomagnesium addition can generate an anionic <nitrogen> intermediate whose hydrolysis yields a <ketone>.

= Maleonitrile
{parent=Nitrile}
{title2=$(Z)\text{-}\mathrm{NCCH{=}CHCN}$}

The Z isomer of $\mathrm{NCCH{=}CHCN}$. A stereospecific Diels–Alder reaction retains the cis relationship between its two <nitrile> substituents.

= Benzonitrile
{parent=Nitrile}

$\mathrm{PhC\equiv N}$, the phenyl-substituted <nitrile>.

= Carboxylic anhydride
{parent=Chemical compound}

A compound with the grouping $\mathrm{R-C(=O)-O-C(=O)-R\prime}$. A carboxylate-derived leaving group can be expelled during <nucleophilic acyl substitution>.

= Acetic anhydride
{parent=Carboxylic anhydride}

$\mathrm{(CH_3CO)_2O}$. It can give a persistent low-temperature tetrahedral addition complex with a <grignard reagent>, allowing methyl-ketone isolation after aqueous work-up under controlled conditions.

= Diazo compound
{parent=Chemical compound}

An organic compound containing a <diazo group>. <diazomethane> is the simplest member.

= Carbonyl compound
{parent=Chemical compound}

A compound containing the carbonyl functional group, $\mathrm{C{=}O}$, such as a <ketone> or aldehyde.

= Trimethyl phosphate
{parent=Chemical compound}

The phosphate triester $\mathrm{OP(OMe)_3}$, containing three P–O–C linkages and one terminal P–O group. Terminal and alkoxy P–O bonds must be distinguished.

= Phosphine oxide
{parent=Chemical compound}

An $\mathrm{R_3PO}$ compound with approximately tetrahedral <phosphorus>. Its short, strongly polar P–O bond can be described using ionic contributions and <oxygen> lone-pair donation into P–R antibonding orbitals, without requiring large <phosphorus> 3d participation.

= Trimethylphosphine oxide
{parent=Phosphine oxide}

The <phosphine oxide> $\mathrm{Me_3PO}$, with three P–C bonds and one terminal P–O bond.

= Pyrrole
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_4H_5N}$}

A five-membered aromatic heterocycle containing NH and four CH groups. The <nitrogen> <lone pair> participates in its six-electron aromatic system.

= Pyridine-2,5-dicarboxylic acid
{parent=Chemical compound}

A <pyridine> ring bearing <carboxylic acid> substituents at positions 2 and 5. Removing both acidic protons gives a dianion with a neighbouring N,O <chelation> site and a remote <carboxylate> site.

= Catechol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_6H_4(OH)_2}$}

The ortho <isomer> of dihydroxybenzene. Removal of both hydroxyl protons gives catecholate.

= Cyclooctatetraene
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_8H_8}$}

An eight-membered cyclic <conjugated system> with eight $\pi$ <electrons>. A planar bond-alternating model has the <D4h molecular point group>. Constraints on planarity matter when discussing its <molecular geometry> and electronic stability.

= Zirconia
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{ZrO_2}$}

= Titanium dioxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{TiO_2}$}

= Cerium dioxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{CeO_2}$}

= Ceria
{synonym}

= Graphite fluoride
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Carbon monofluoride
{synonym}

A fluorinated carbon material often written CFx. Fluorination disrupts extended graphitic conjugation and introduces carbon–fluorine bonding.

= Organohalide
{parent=Chemical compound}

An organic compound with a carbon–halogen bond. Alkyl, allylic and vinylic attachment differ in geometry and substitution reactivity.

= Aryl halide
{parent=Organohalide}

An <organohalide> with the halogen attached directly to an aromatic ring <carbon>. Its substitution chemistry differs from an alkyl halide because the C–halogen bond is attached to an sp2 <carbon>.

= Bromobenzene
{parent=Aryl halide}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_6H_5Br}$}

$\mathrm{C_6H_5Br}$, an aryl bromide that can be converted to an aryl <grignard reagent>. Bromine directs electrophilic aromatic substitution preferentially to the ortho and para positions while deactivating the ring overall.

= Bromocyclohexane
{parent=Organohalide}

$\mathrm{C_6H_{11}Br}$. <magnesium> insertion provides a cyclohexyl organometallic reagent, while base-induced elimination provides <cyclohexene>.

= Phosphorus tribromide
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Organic compound
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Organoboron compound
{parent=Organic compound}

An organic compound containing a <carbon>–<boron> bond.

= Boroxine
{parent=Organoboron compound}

A six-membered alternating <boron>–<oxygen> ring, commonly $\mathrm{(RBO)_3}$. Three-coordinate <boron> has a vacant p orbital that accepts <oxygen> lone-pair donation. This stabilizing delocalization does not imply the same <aromaticity> or equal bond polarity as benzene.

= Organohalogen compound
{parent=Organic compound}

An <organic compound> containing a carbon-halogen bond. The bonding environment matters: a <vinylic halide> has the halogen directly attached to a double-bonded carbon, whereas an <allylic halide> has it attached to the adjacent saturated carbon.

= Allylic halide
{parent=Organohalogen compound}

= Vinylic halide
{parent=Organohalogen compound}

= Vinyl chloride
{parent=Vinylic halide}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_2=CHCl}$}

= Organometallic compound
{parent=Chemical compound}

A compound containing a direct bond between a metal and carbon.

= Vinylaluminate
{parent=Organometallic compound}

An anionic organoaluminium compound with a <carbon>–<aluminium> bond at an <alkene> <carbon>.

= Organoaluminium compound
{parent=Organometallic compound}

An organometallic compound containing a <carbon>–<aluminium> bond.

= Aluminoxane
{parent=Organoaluminium compound}

An <aluminium>–<oxygen> compound containing Al–O–Al connectivity, often represented compositionally as $\mathrm{(RAlO)_n}$. Electron-deficient <aluminium> can achieve four-coordinate environments through cage assembly and multiply bridging <oxygen>.

= Arene chromium tricarbonyl complex
{parent=Organometallic compound}

A neutral <piano-stool complex> $\mathrm{Cr}(\eta^6\!\text{-arene})(\mathrm{CO})_3$, conventionally <chromium>(0), with 18 valence <electrons>. <arene> substitution changes <electron> density and carbonyl stretching frequencies.

= Gilman cuprate
{parent=Organometallic compound}
{c}

A lithium cuprate conventionally represented as LiCuR₂. Its actual structure can be an aggregate and depends on ligand identity, solvent and salts.

= Alkylidene
{parent=Organometallic compound}

A carbon fragment doubly bonded to a metal in the present organometallic usage. Metal–alkylidene exchange with an <alkene> underlies <olefin metathesis>.

= Organocuprate
{parent=Organometallic compound}

An organocopper reagent capable of carbon-group transfer. Suitable <organocuprates> can favour <conjugate addition> to an enone over direct addition to its <carbonyl>.

= Allyltitanium reagent
{parent=Organometallic compound}

An allylic organotitanium species that can add its allyl group to a <carbonyl compound>, furnishing a homoallylic <alcohol> after <hydrolysis>.

= Oxatitanacyclobutane
{parent=Organometallic compound}

A four-membered <titanium>–<oxygen>–<carbon>–<carbon> intermediate in <carbonyl> olefination. Fragmentation yields a <carbon>–<carbon> double bond and a <titanium>–<oxygen> product.

= Titanacyclobutane
{parent=Organometallic compound}

A four-membered metallacycle containing <titanium> and three <carbon> atoms. Cycloreversion can exchange an <alkene> and a <titanium> alkylidene; other eliminations become available with suitable substituents.

= Titanocene methylidene
{parent=Organometallic compound}

The reactive early-metal alkylidene $\mathrm{Cp_2Ti{=}CH_2}$. <carbonyl> methylenation proceeds through an <oxatitanacyclobutane>; <alkene> capture gives a <titanacyclobutane>. Its <oxygen> affinity makes <ester> methylenation feasible.

= Schwartz reagent
{parent=Organometallic compound}
{c}

Zirconocene chloride <hydride>, $\mathrm{Cp_2Zr(H)Cl}$, used for <hydrozirconation>.

= Methylaluminoxane
{parent=Organometallic compound}

An oligomeric aluminium–oxygen reagent used to alkylate and ionize <metallocene> <polymerization> <catalysts>.

= Metallacyclopropane
{parent=Organometallic compound}

A three-membered <metal>–<carbon>–<carbon> bonding description obtained in the strong alkene-reduction limit. It emphasizes <metal>–<carbon> sigma bonding and reduced internal C–C bond order.

= Metallacyclopropene
{parent=Organometallic compound}

A three-membered metal–carbon–carbon ring description with substantial metal–carbon sigma bonding and reduced alkyne bond order.

= Ammonium chloride
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Organosilicon compound
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Silsesquioxane
{parent=Organosilicon compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{(RSiO_{3/2})_n}$}

A <silicon>–<oxygen> framework with ideal fully condensed composition $\mathrm{(RSiO_{3/2})_n}$. Each <silicon> has one organic <substituent> and three <siloxane> bridges. Incompletely condensed cages retain <silanol> groups.

= T7 trisilanol cage
{parent=Silsesquioxane}
{c}

A corner-deficient cage $\mathrm{R_7Si_7O_9(OH)_3}$. Removing one <silicon> corner from a T8 cage leaves three silanol-bearing <silicon> sites, three further sites adjacent to those, and a unique opposite site: symmetry classes 3:3:1.

= T10 silsesquioxane cage
{parent=Silsesquioxane}
{c}

A cage $\mathrm{R_{10}Si_{10}O_{15}}$ with a common pentagonal-prismatic <silicon> skeleton and an <oxygen> bridge on each edge.

= T8 silsesquioxane cage
{parent=Silsesquioxane}
{c}

A cage $\mathrm{R_8Si_8O_{12}}$ represented by a <cube> of eight <silicon> vertices with one bridging <oxygen> on each of twelve edges.

= Silanone
{parent=Organosilicon compound}

A compound containing a <silicon>–<oxygen> <double bond>, $\mathrm{R_2Si=O}$. Ordinary small-substituent <silanones> are highly reactive; oligomerization to <siloxane> linkages is favoured over persistence as freely dissolved monomers.

= Siloxane
{parent=Organosilicon compound}

An Si–O–Si linkage, or a compound containing such linkages. Cyclic and polymeric <siloxanes> have strongly polar bonds and flexible bond angles; their bonding should not be reduced to a dominant oxygen-2p/silicon-3d pi interaction.

= Trimethylsilyl group
{parent=Organosilicon compound}

The substituent $\mathrm{Si(CH_3)_3}$, often abbreviated $\mathrm{SiMe_3}$. Its steric and electronic effects should be separated when interpreting regioselectivity.

= Silanol
{parent=Organosilicon compound}

An Si–OH group or a compound containing it. Condensation of two silanol groups can form an Si–O–Si bond and water.

= Silanolate ligand
{parent=Silanol}

A <coordination ligand> containing deprotonated <silanol> <oxygen>, $\mathrm{Si-O^-}$, which binds a <metal> through <oxygen>. Three <silanol> groups on a corner-deficient <silsesquioxane> can give a facial tridentate trianionic donor set.

= Chlorosilane
{parent=Organosilicon compound}

An organosilicon compound containing Si–Cl bonds. Hydrolysis can produce silanols, whose condensation builds siloxane linkages.

= Phosphorus pentachloride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{PCl_5}$}

The isolated molecular form is an ideal trigonal bipyramid with <D3h molecular point group> symmetry. Its solid-state structures need not consist of the same isolated molecules.

= Buckminsterfullerene
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm C_{60}$}

The ideal $\mathrm C_{60}$ carbon cage has <Ih molecular point group> symmetry. The highest symmetry-protected orbital degeneracy is five; spin degeneracy is counted separately.

= Phosphazene
{parent=Chemical compound}

A <phosphazene> contains phosphorus–<nitrogen> bonding often represented with formal P=N units. Ionic bonding and <negative hyperconjugation> provide a modern account; formal double-bond drawings do not require phosphorus $3d$ pi bonding.

= Polyphosphazene
{parent=Phosphazene}

A polymer with an alternating phosphorus–nitrogen backbone and typically two substituents at phosphorus. Polar bonding and negative hyperconjugation differ from a continuously conducting conjugated carbon backbone.

= Poly\[bis(ethoxy)phosphazene\]
{parent=Polyphosphazene}

A phosphazene polymer with two ethoxy substituents at each phosphorus, commonly prepared by alkoxide substitution of linear polydichlorophosphazene.

= Polydichlorophosphazene
{parent=Polyphosphazene}

A linear phosphazene polymer with two chlorine substituents per phosphorus, used as a precursor for macromolecular nucleophilic substitution.

= Cyclotriphosphazene
{parent=Phosphazene}

A <cyclotriphosphazene> contains an alternating six-membered $\mathrm{P_3N_3}$ ring. Substitution at its phosphorus atoms can generate geminal, non-geminal and spiro derivatives.

= Hexachlorocyclotriphosphazene
{parent=Cyclotriphosphazene}

The cyclic trimer $\mathrm{P_3N_3Cl_6}$, a precursor to linear polydichlorophosphazene under controlled ring-opening conditions.

= Enone
{parent=Chemical compound}

An <enone> has a carbonyl conjugated to a <carbon>–<carbon> double bond, $\mathrm{O{=}C{-}C{=}C}$. Its beta <carbon> can undergo <conjugate addition>.

= Finasteride
{parent=Chemical compound}

<Finasteride> is an unsaturated 4-azasteroid inhibitor of steroid <5α-reductase>. Enzyme-generated reduction and <enolate> capture of oxidized cofactor give a covalent cofactor–inhibitor adduct; the protein itself need not be covalently modified.

= Testosterone
{parent=Chemical compound}

<Testosterone> is a steroid with a conjugated C3-ketone/C4=C5 fragment. <5α-reductase> adds hydride at C5 and a <proton> at C4 to make <dihydrotestosterone>.

= Dihydrotestosterone
{parent=Chemical compound}

<Dihydrotestosterone> is the 5α-reduced steroid formed from <testosterone>. Reduction saturates the C4=C5 bond while retaining the C3 ketone and the other steroid functional groups.

= Enol
{parent=Chemical compound}

An <enol> has an OH group bonded to a <carbon> of a C=C bond. Protonation and pi-bond rearrangement can interconvert it with a carbonyl compound through <keto-enol tautomerism>.

= Enolization
{parent=Enol}

Interconversion of a <carbonyl compound> with its <enol> or enolate-derived forms. Reprotonation of a planar intermediate can epimerize an alpha <stereocentre>.

= Chelated enol
{parent=Enol}

An <enol> stabilized by an intramolecular O–H···O interaction, commonly closing a six-membered arrangement in a 1,3-dicarbonyl compound. <proton> exchange between equivalent <enol> forms can average <NMR> environments.

= Silyl enol ether
{parent=Enol}

A <silyl enol ether> has the connectivity $\mathrm{C{=}C{-}O{-}SiR_3}$. Nucleophilic attack at <silicon> can cleave the Si–O bond and generate an <enolate> without adding to the original <carbonyl> <carbon>.

= Silyl ketene acetal
{parent=Silyl enol ether}

An O-silylated <ester> <enolate>, with two oxygen substituents on one carbon of the enol double bond.

= Enol carbonate
{parent=Enol}

= Tetramethylammonium
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{NMe_4^+}$}

<Tetramethylammonium> is $\mathrm{NMe_4^+}$, often a counterion that delivers an anion in a soluble salt.

= Propene
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_3H_6}$}

<Propene> is $\mathrm{CH_2{=}CHCH_3}$. Protonation of an anionic allyl ligand can release it from a metal.

= Acetonitrile
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{MeCN}$}

<Acetonitrile>, $\mathrm{CH_3CN}$, can coordinate through <nitrogen> as a neutral two-electron donor. Bound <acetonitrile> can be displaced by a stronger donor or an intramolecular chelating group.

= Tert-butyl group
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{^{t}Bu}$}

A <tert-butyl group> is $\mathrm{(CH_3)_3C{-}}$, a branched substituent that can produce substantial <steric hindrance>.

= Phenyl group
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ph}$}

A <phenyl group> is $\mathrm{C_6H_5{-}}$, obtained formally by removing one hydrogen from benzene.

= Di-tert-butylphenylphosphine
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{P(^{t}Bu)_2Ph}$}

<Di-tert-butylphenylphosphine> is the bulky mixed alkyl/aryl phosphine $\mathrm{P(^{t}Bu)_2Ph}$. Its tert-butyl substituents strongly influence steric interactions at a metal.

= Trimethylphosphine
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{PMe_3}$}

<Trimethylphosphine> is $\mathrm{PMe_3}$, an electron-releasing <phosphine ligand>. Comparison of its carbonyl complexes with other phosphines helps separate steric and electronic trends.

= Triphenylphosphine
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{PPh_3}$}

<Triphenylphosphine> is $\mathrm{PPh_3}$, a neutral <phosphine ligand> whose phenyl substituents affect both steric demand and electronic donation.

= Nitrite
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{NO_2^-}$}

<Nitrite> is the bent anion $\mathrm{NO_2^-}$. It can act as an <ambidentate ligand>, giving N-bound <nitro ligands> or O-bound <nitrito ligands>.

= KDPG
{parent=Chemical compound}
{c}

<KDPG> is 2-keto-3-deoxy-6-phosphogluconate, a sugar-phosphate intermediate that can serve as a ligand for <RccR>. Binding can change transcriptional regulation without changing the regulator concentration.

= Glyoxylate
{parent=Chemical compound}

<glyoxylate> is a two-carbon product of <isocitrate lyase> and a substrate for <malate synthase>. Its pool size and regulatory effects depend on pathway activity and metabolic context.

= Isopentenyl diphosphate
{parent=Chemical compound}

= IPP
{c}
{synonym}

<isopentenyl diphosphate> is an activated five-carbon precursor for isoprenoid biosynthesis. Rescue by supplied IPP identifies a bypassable metabolic requirement only when uptake and continued growth are demonstrated.

= Quinone
{parent=Chemical compound}

A <quinone> has an oxidized conjugated carbonyl system that can participate in redox chemistry. Some added quinones intercept electrons in photosynthetic preparations, potentially creating routes absent from unmodified cells.

= Ferricyanide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_6]^{3-}$}

<ferricyanide> is a redox-active iron-cyanide complex commonly used as an external electron acceptor in biological assays. Reduction shows electron accessibility under assay conditions, not necessarily a native physiological export pathway.

= Inorganic pyrophosphate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{PP_i}$}

<inorganic pyrophosphate> contains two phosphate units joined by a phosphoanhydride linkage. Its hydrolysis can favor net nucleotide-sugar activation when the relevant reactions are coupled.

= TTF-TCNQ
{c}
{parent=Chemical compound}

A quasi-one-dimensional organic charge-transfer conductor with coupled electronic and structural ordering. A phonon anomaly near $2k_F$ was observed as its low-temperature <Peierls transition> was approached.<Phonon dispersion and Kohn anomaly in TTF-TCNQ by Gen Shirane and others>[Abstract: temperature-dependent phonon anomaly near 2kF]{ref}

= Intermetallic
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Intermetallic compound
{synonym}

A compound formed from metallic <chemical elements>, with a crystal and electronic structure distinct from a simple variable-composition metallic mixture.

= CeAl3
{c}
{parent=Intermetallic}
{title2=$\mathrm{CeAl}_3$}

An <intermetallic compound> and an early example of a <heavy fermion material> identified by low-temperature calorimetry and electrical transport.

= 1,4-dioxane
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Ethylbenzene
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Clathrate compound
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

A clathrate compound has a host structure with cavities containing guest atoms or molecules. The host and guest motions can have very different stiffnesses and characteristic frequencies. Cage-like germanium frameworks with metal guests provide examples with low-energy <rattling modes>.

= Ba4Na2Ge25
{c}
{parent=Clathrate compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ba_4Na_2Ge_{25}}$}

A germanium cage compound with barium and sodium guests. Its low-temperature heat capacity supplies an example of a <rattling mode> in addition to the leading electronic and acoustic contributions.<Superconductivity in the Filled Cage Compounds Ba6Ge25 and Ba4Na2Ge25 by Friedrich Malte Grosche and others>[Figure 3: calorimetric example]{ref}

= Aluminium chloride
{parent=Chemical compound}

The compound $\mathrm{AlCl_3}$. In an ideal fully dissociated aqueous-electrolyte bookkeeping model, formula concentration $c$ supplies $c$ of triply charged aluminium ions and $3c$ of singly charged chloride ions, giving <ionic strength> $I=6c$. Hydrolysis, ion association and nonideal activities can make this simple model inadequate for concentrated real solutions.

= Gadolinium antimonide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{GdSb}$}

= Gadolinium sulfate octahydrate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{Gd_2(SO_4)_3\cdot8H_2O}$}

The hydrated <chemical compound> used in the <Giauque-MacDougall magnetic cooling experiment>.

= Gallium arsenide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{GaAs}$}
{tag=Semiconductor}

= GaAs
{c}
{synonym}

A compound <semiconductor>.

= GaAs four-band model
{parent=Gallium arsenide}
{c}

A zone-centre effective-mass description retaining the s-like <conduction band>, heavy-hole and light-hole branches of the p-like $j=3/2$ valence manifold, and the spin-orbit-split $j=1/2$ valence branch. Each named branch includes <spin> multiplicity; four bands does not mean four individual quantum states.

= Tetrachloroethylene
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_2Cl_4}$}

= Perchloroethylene
{synonym}

A chlorine-containing <liquid> used as the target material in the <Homestake experiment>.

= Cadmium chloride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{CdCl_2}$}
{wiki}

= Oxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Lead(IV) oxide
{parent=Oxide}

PbO2, with a formal Pb(IV) state. A common polymorph has a rutile-type extended structure with six-coordinate lead.

= Lead(II) oxide
{parent=Oxide}

PbO, with a formal Pb(II) state and retained 6s2 pair. Its layered structures and asymmetric local environments contrast with a simple molecular monoxide model.

= Magnetite
{parent=Oxide}
{title2=$\mathrm{Fe_3O_4}$}
{wiki}

= Silver halide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Silver bromide
{parent=Silver halide}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{AgBr}$}

= Carbonyl sulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{OCS}$}

A polar <linear molecule> with composition OCS.

= Silicon dioxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{SiO_2}$}
{wiki}

= Coesite
{parent=Silicon dioxide}

A structural polymorph of <silicon dioxide>. In a <coesite>–<stishovite> transition calculation, the entropy-change sign must use the same conversion direction as the specific-volume change.

= Stishovite
{parent=Silicon dioxide}

A dense structural polymorph of <silicon dioxide>. Comparing its unit-cell volume per formula unit with that of a coexisting phase supplies the specific-volume difference needed in a <Clapeyron equation> calculation.

= Quartz
{parent=Silicon dioxide}
{wiki}

= Silica gel
{parent=Silicon dioxide}
{wiki}

A porous form of <silicon dioxide> used as an <adsorption> medium in <thin-layer chromatography>.

= Zinc sulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{ZnS}$}
{wiki}

= Sphalerite
{parent=Zinc sulfide}
{title2=$\mathrm{ZnS}$}
{wiki}

= Zinc blende
{synonym}

= Sodium thiosulfate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{Na_2S_2O_3}$}

= Sodium hydride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{NaH}$}

An <ionic compound> of <sodium> <cations> and <hydrogen anions>. Its <oxidation state> assignments are $+1$ for <sodium> and $-1$ for <hydrogen>.

= Sulfuric acid
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm H_2\mathrm{SO}_4$}

A <chemical acid> used to supply the acidic conditions of a <permanganate-iron titration>.

= Manganese dioxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{MnO}_2$}

The <manganese> <oxidation state> is $+4$ when each <oxygen> is assigned $-2$.

= Fluorescein
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Tetradecyltrimethylammonium bromide
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Sodium hydroxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{NaOH}$}

= Potassium hydroxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{tag=Base (chemistry)}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{KOH}$}

= Tert-amyl bromide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_5\mathrm{H}_{11}\mathrm{Br}$}

= Tertiary amylbromide
{synonym}

= 2-Bromo-2-methylbutane
{synonym}

The <chemical compound> used in the <conductometric determination of activation energy>. Its <chemical formula> and alternative name <2-bromo-2-methylbutane> are listed by the <NIST Chemistry WebBook, SRD 69/Butane, 2-bromo-2-methyl->{ref}.

= Benzene
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_6$}

<Benzene> has a planar ring of six <carbon> <chemical atoms>, each bearing one <hydrogen>. Its $\pi$ <electron density> is distributed over the ring; the alternating <covalent bonds> in one <resonance structure> do not describe alternating actual <bond lengths>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/15-2-structure-and-stability-of-benzene[McMurry: benzene structure]{ref}

= Benzylic carbon
{parent=Benzene}

An sp3 <carbon> directly attached to an <aromatic ring>.

= Phenol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_5\mathrm{OH}$}

<Phenol> is <benzene> with one ring <hydrogen> replaced by a <hydroxy group>. That O–H group supplies the <hydrogen bond donor> in the experiment under <hydrogen bond acceptor>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/17-1-naming-alcohols-and-phenols[McMurry: phenol naming and structures]{ref}

= Sodium chloride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{NaCl}$}

<Sodium chloride> contains <sodium> <cations> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and <chlorine> <anions> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ in a $1:1$ ratio; its <formula unit> has zero net <electric charge>.

= Glycerol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_3\mathrm{H}_8\mathrm{O}_3$}

<Glycerol> has three <carbon> <chemical atoms> and three $\mathrm{OH}$ groups. It supplies the scaffold of <triglycerides> and <glycerophospholipids>, but is not itself a long <hydrocarbon> chain. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: glycerol].

= Allene
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

An <allene> contains the unit $\mathrm{C=C=C}$ with two consecutive <carbon>–<carbon> <covalent bonds> of order two. Suitable terminal <substituents> give <axial chirality>.

= Hydrocarbon
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

A <hydrocarbon> contains only <carbon> and <hydrogen>. A <fatty acid> also contains <oxygen>; calling its long chain a <hydrocarbon> region does not make the whole <fatty acid> a <hydrocarbon>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02889[IUPAC Gold Book: hydrocarbons].

= Pentalene
{parent=Hydrocarbon}

A fused pair of five-membered <carbon> rings with formula $\mathrm{C_8H_6}$. Its neutral conjugated framework has eight pi <electrons>. Coordination and reduction alter its <electron> distribution and allow several metal-binding <hapticities>.

= Syn and anti pentalene coordination
{parent=Pentalene}

Two <metal> fragments attached to the fused ring system are syn when they lie on the same face and anti when they lie on opposite faces. A direct <metal>–<metal> bond between adjacent fragments generally requires the syn arrangement.

= Dihydropentalene
{parent=Pentalene}

A $\mathrm{C_8H_8}$ hydrocarbon with two more hydrogen atoms than <pentalene>; double-bond isomers are possible. One and two <deprotonations> give chemically distinct hydropentalenide and <pentalenide> charge states.

= Hydropentalenyl ligand
{parent=Pentalene}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_8H_7^-}$}

The monoanionic $\mathrm{C_8H_7^-}$ <coordination ligand> obtained by removing one proton from <dihydropentalene>. An aromatic five-membered portion can bind a <metal> in an eta-five mode while the fused ring retains an uncoordinated <alkene>.

= Pentalenide
{parent=Pentalene}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_8H_6^{2-}}$}

The dianion $\mathrm{C_8H_6^{2-}}$, with ten pi <electrons> over the fused framework. The two overlapping five-membered rings must not be counted as two independent cyclopentadienyl donors.

= Benzvalene
{parent=Hydrocarbon}

A cage isomer of <benzene> with one <carbon>–<carbon> <double bond>, conventionally described as tricyclo\[3.1.0.0(2,6)\]hex-3-ene. Its ideal cage geometry belongs to the <C2v molecular point group>. <Molecular symmetry> depends on the full three-dimensional cage rather than the apparent <symmetry> of a planar skeletal projection.

= Three-radialene
{parent=Hydrocarbon}

= \[3\]Radialene
{synonym}

The <hydrocarbon> with a three-membered <carbon> ring and an exocyclic methylene <double bond> at each vertex. The ideal planar structure belongs to the <D3h molecular point group>.

= Fulvene
{parent=Hydrocarbon}

The conjugated <hydrocarbon> with a five-membered ring bearing two endocyclic double bonds and one exocyclic methylene <double bond>. Its ideal planar structure has the <C2v molecular point group>.

= Alternant hydrocarbon
{parent=Hydrocarbon}

A conjugated <hydrocarbon> whose pi-site graph is bipartite: its sites can be divided into two sets with every retained resonance interaction joining opposite sets. The definition refers to the selected nearest-neighbour Hückel model, not simply to a visually alternating Lewis drawing.

= Alkane
{parent=Hydrocarbon}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_n\mathrm{H}_{2n+2}$}

An acyclic saturated <hydrocarbon>. The formula applies to a connected neutral alkane with $n\geq1$ carbon atoms.

= Tert-amyl group
{parent=Alkane}

The 2-methylbutan-2-yl group, attached through its tertiary <carbon>.

= Heptane
{parent=Alkane}

A saturated seven-carbon hydrocarbon, $\mathrm{C_7H_{16}}$; the normal isomer is a weakly coordinating solvent.

= Isobutane
{parent=Alkane}

A small branched hydrocarbon with no conventional hydrogen-bond acceptor site. Its transfer into water lacks the compensating polar interactions available to oxygen- or nitrogen-containing comparators.

= Pentacene
{parent=Hydrocarbon}
{wiki}

= Para-terphenyl
{parent=Hydrocarbon}

= Toluene
{parent=Hydrocarbon}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_7H_8}$}
{wiki}

= Ethane
{parent=Hydrocarbon}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_2H_6}$}

= Hexane
{parent=Hydrocarbon}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_6H_{14}}$}

= Ester
{parent=Chemical compound}

The <carboxylic acid>-derived <ester> linkages in a <triglyceride> or typical <glycerophospholipid> have the form $\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}O{-}R'}$. Here the <acyl group> is attached through <oxygen> to the <glycerol>-derived part. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P6.html[IUPAC Blue Book, P-65.6: esters].

= Ethyl methacrylate
{parent=Ester}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_2{=}C(Me)CO_2Et}$}

$\mathrm{CH_2{=}C(Me)CO_2Et}$, an alpha-methyl-substituted acrylate. Conjugate addition installs a chain whose <carbon> next to the <ester> retains the methyl substituent.

= Beta-ketoester
{parent=Ester}

A compound with a <ketone> <carbonyl> at the beta position relative to an <ester> <carbonyl>.

= Cinnamate ester
{parent=Ester}

An <ester> containing the conjugated fragment Ph-CH=CH-C(=O)-O-R. Its trans <alkene> commonly has a large vicinal proton coupling, while <HMBC> can connect an alkoxy methylene to the <ester> <carbonyl>.

= Ethyl pentanoate
{parent=Ester}

$\mathrm{EtO_2CCH_2CH_2CH_2CH_3}$.

= Methyl acrylate
{parent=Ester}

$\mathrm{CH_2=CHCO_2Me}$, an activated alkene useful as a <dienophile> and Michael acceptor.

= Vinyl pentanoate
{parent=Ester}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_2=CHOC(O)(CH_2)_3CH_3}$}

= Vinyl acetate
{parent=Ester}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_2=CHOCOCH_3}$}

= Nitrate ester
{parent=Ester}
{title2=$\mathrm{R-ONO_2}$}

A covalent organic nitrate group, distinct from a nitro group $\mathrm{R-NO_2}$ and from the nitrate anion.

= Lactone
{parent=Ester}

A cyclic <ester>. Intramolecular alcohol-to-carbonyl attack followed by elimination can close a <lactone> ring.

= 3,3-Dimethylphthalide
{parent=Lactone}

A benzene-fused five-membered <lactone> containing a gem-dimethyl-substituted saturated ring <carbon>. It can result from double methyl addition to one <carbonyl> of <dimethyl phthalate> followed by ring closure.

= Conjugated ester
{parent=Ester}

= Methyl acetate
{parent=Ester}

= Methyl ester
{parent=Ester}

= Methyl butanoate
{parent=Methyl ester}

$\mathrm{MeO_2CCH_2CH_2CH_3}$.

= Dimethyl phthalate
{parent=Methyl ester}

The dimethyl <ester> of benzene-1,2-dicarboxylic acid, with adjacent <ester> groups on a <benzene> ring.

= tert-Butyl ester
{parent=Ester}

= Sulfonate ester
{parent=Ester}

An ester of a sulfonic acid. In an appropriate alkyl sulfonate, substitution at carbon can expel a resonance-stabilized sulfonate leaving group.

= Sultone
{parent=Sulfonate ester}

A cyclic sulfonate ester. Alkoxide attack at a suitable primary carbon bound to the ring oxygen can open the ring by carbon–oxygen cleavage, leaving a sulfonate group.

= Allyl acetate
{parent=Ester}

The ester CH3COOCH2CH=CH2. Its terminal vinyl hydrogens are chemically nonequivalent and have distinct cis and trans couplings to the internal vinyl proton.

= Methyl crotonate
{parent=Ester}

The methyl ester of but-2-enoic acid. Its conjugated acyl group has different carbonyl and vinylic environments from an ester whose alkene lies in an allyl alcohol-derived group.

= Diethyl malonate
{parent=Ester}

An ester with a methylene group between two carbonyls. Its conjugate base is stabilized by delocalization involving both carbonyl groups.

= Ethyl acetoacetate
{parent=Ester}
{wiki}

= Ethyl acetate
{parent=Ester}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COOC_2H_5}$}

An <ester> consumed in the <conductometric saponification assay>, where <hydrolysis> under basic conditions produces <ethanol> and an <acetate ion>.

= Methanol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Choline
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Disulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Thiol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{R-SH}$}
{wiki}

A <thiol> is a <chemical compound> of the form $\mathrm{R-SH}$, with an organic group $R$ attached to <sulfur>. The <conjugate base> is $\mathrm{R-S^-}$; <cysteine> and <mercaptoethanol> supply examples of <thiol> groups. Two <thiol> groups can undergo <oxidation> to form a <disulfide bond>. https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/T06359[IUPAC Gold Book: thiols]{ref}

= Thiophenol
{parent=Thiol}
{title2=$\mathrm{PhSH}$}

<Thiophenol> is $\mathrm{PhSH}$. Its <conjugate base> is thiophenolate, $\mathrm{PhS^-}$, a <sulfur>-centered <nucleophile>.

= Ellman's reagent
{c}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= DTNB
{c}
{synonym}

<Ellman's reagent> is 5,5'-dithiobis(2-nitrobenzoic acid), abbreviated <DTNB>, a <disulfide> used to quantify accessible <thiols>. Reaction releases the colored TNB species measured by <absorbance> at <wavelength> $412\,\mathrm{nm}$. The <disulfide bond> experiment above explains why a <thiol> measurement can become an indirect bridge count only after controlled <reduction>. https://assets.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/LSG/manuals/MAN0011216_Ellmans_Reag_UG.pdf[Pierce Biotechnology: identity and use of Ellman's reagent]{ref}

= Diol
{parent=Chemical compound}

A <diol> has two <hydroxy groups>. Its two groups can serve as joining ends in <polyaddition>; the rest of its <molecule> can already be a long chain.

= Isocyanate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{R{-}N=C=O}$}

An <isocyanate> bears an $\mathrm{R{-}N=C=O}$ group. A <molecule> bearing two such groups can react with a <diol> to form <polyurethane> connections.

= Urea
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$(\mathrm{NH}_2)_2\mathrm{CO}$}
{wiki}

Urea is the <chemical compound> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm{CH}_4\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$ and structure $(\mathrm{NH}_2)_2\mathrm{CO}$. See the https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C57136&Mask=20[NIST Chemistry WebBook, urea: formula and structure]. Concentrated urea is used to promote <protein denaturation> in the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>.

= 2-Mercaptoethanol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{HOCH}_2\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{SH}$}
{wiki}

= Mercaptoethanol
{synonym}

2-Mercaptoethanol is the <chemical compound> $\mathrm{HOCH}_2\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{SH}$; see the https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C60242&Units=SI[NIST Chemistry WebBook, 2-mercaptoethanol: structure and names]. In the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>, it breaks <protein disulfide bonds>, while the <peptide bonds> of the <protein backbone> remain intact. This separates a change in crosslinks from cutting the <polypeptide chain>.

= Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{tag=Coenzyme}
{wiki}

= NADH
{c}
{parent=Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide}
{title2=$\mathrm{NADH}$}

<NADH> is the reduced form of <nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide>.

= NAD+
{c}
{parent=Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide}
{title2=$\mathrm{NAD}^{+}$}

<NAD+> is the oxidized form of <nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide>; <NADH> is its reduced form.

= Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

<Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate> is a <cofactor> used in <metabolism>. Its oxidized and reduced forms, <NADP+> and <NADPH>, carry <electrons> between coupled <chemical reactions>. In <oxygenic photosynthesis>, <light-dependent reactions> produce <NADPH> for the <Calvin cycle>, which returns <NADP+> for reuse. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: recycling NADP+ and NADPH]{ref}.

= NADP+
{c}
{parent=Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm{NADP}^{+}$}

= Oxidized NADP
{synonym}

<NADP+> is the oxidized form of <nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate>. It can accept <electrons> to form <NADPH>; the <light-dependent reactions> supply this conversion during <oxygenic photosynthesis>.

= NADPH
{c}
{parent=Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm{NADPH}$}

<NADPH> is the reduced form of <nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate>. It carries <electrons> used in synthesis; in the <Calvin cycle> it becomes the oxidized form <NADP+>, available for reuse by <light-dependent reactions>. <NADPH> and <ATP> serve different roles even though both connect <light> capture to <carbon fixation>.

= Chloromethane
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH}_3\mathrm{Cl}$}
{wiki}

= Methyl chloride
{synonym}

An organochlorine gas with biological as well as possible nonbiological sources. Its specialized biological production is a <secondary metabolic byproduct> example.

= Dimethyl sulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$(\mathrm{CH}_3)_2\mathrm S$}
{wiki}

= DMS
{c}
{synonym}

An organic sulfur gas emitted through biological processing, including marine production associated with sulfur-containing metabolites. It is a <secondary metabolic byproduct> example and a possible <atmospheric biosignature gas> whose detectability depends on production and photochemical destruction.

= Ozone
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm O_3$}
{wiki}

A triatomic form of <oxygen>, produced photochemically from <molecular oxygen>. The <ozone layer> absorbs ultraviolet radiation; ozone can trace atmospheric oxygen without being directly emitted by metabolism.

= Tropospheric ozone
{parent=Ozone}

<Ozone> in the <troposphere> is a secondary chemical pollutant and a radiatively active gas. Its abundance reflects photochemical production, chemical loss, <dry deposition> and transport. The <photodissociation> of <nitrogen dioxide> alone is part of a null <ozone> cycle unless <peroxy radicals> regenerate <nitrogen dioxide> without consuming <ozone>.

= Ozone titration
{parent=Tropospheric ozone}

Local <ozone> consumption by the reaction $\mathrm{NO+O_3\to NO_2+O_2}$. Reducing fresh NO emissions can raise local measured <ozone> even while decreasing regional <ozone> production. This distinction matters when interpreting precursor-control experiments and observations.

= Photochemical ozone production
{parent=Tropospheric ozone}

Net daylight <ozone> production occurs when <peroxy radicals> oxidize NO to NO2 without using <ozone>, followed by NO2 <photodissociation> and oxygen-atom addition to O2. The competing reaction NO + O3 to NO2 + O2 only recycles odd <oxygen>. Radical initiation, termination, precursor loading and transport determine the net rate.

= VOC-limited ozone production
{parent=Photochemical ozone production}
{c}

A regime in which precursor/<radical> supply and NOx-dependent <radical termination> make net <ozone> production more responsive to additional reactive organic compounds than to additional <nitrogen oxides>. Reducing <nitrogen oxides> can initially raise <ozone> in some such conditions. Diagnose the chemical regime before extrapolating an emission reduction to every location.

= NOx-limited ozone production
{parent=Photochemical ozone production}
{c}

A regime in which additional available <nitrogen oxides> tends to increase net <photochemical ozone production> when other factors are fixed. Reducing <nitrogen oxides> generally suppresses that production, but local <ozone> <concentration> also depends on transport, deposition and NO titration.

= Ozone isopleth diagram
{parent=Photochemical ozone production}

A contour plot of a specified <ozone> outcome against the loadings of <nitrogen oxides> and <volatile organic compounds>. The ridge separating opposite NOx sensitivities distinguishes an <NOx-limited ozone-production regime> from a <VOC-limited ozone-production regime>. The outcome may be peak <concentration>, a time-integrated measure or a model production rate; axes and averaging conditions must be stated. A schematic diagram is not a calibrated emissions-response prediction.

= VOC-limited ozone-production regime
{parent=Photochemical ozone production}
{c}

A regime with abundant <nitrogen oxides> in which volatile-organic oxidation and radical supply limit net <tropospheric ozone> production. Reducing <volatile organic compounds> lowers production. A small NOx reduction can initially increase <ozone> by weakening OH termination and NO titration; sufficiently large reductions can cross into an <NOx-limited ozone-production regime>. Local <concentration> and regional emission sensitivities need not coincide.

= NOx-limited ozone-production regime
{parent=Photochemical ozone production}
{c}
{title2=$\mathrm{NO}_x$}

A regime in which additional <nitrogen oxides> promote net <tropospheric ozone> production because NO availability limits peroxy-radical oxidation of NO. Reducing NOx then generally lowers <ozone> production, subject to transport and chemical history. At much larger NOx burdens, OH removal by nitric-acid formation can instead produce a <VOC-limited ozone-production regime>.

= Nitrous oxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$}
{wiki}

A nitrogen oxide produced substantially by microbial nitrogen cycling on modern <Earth>. Its atmospheric abundance and lifetime make it a candidate <atmospheric biosignature gas>, with context-dependent abiotic alternatives.

= Phosphine
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{PH}_3$}
{wiki}

Phosphine is $\mathrm{PH}_3$. Its abundance in giant-planet observable atmospheres can depend on transport from deeper layers faster than local chemical destruction.

= Vanadium(II) oxide
{title2=$\mathrm{VO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Vanadium monoxide
{synonym}

= Titanium monoxide
{title2=$\mathrm{TiO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Acetylene
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_2\mathrm{H}_2$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Hydrogen cyanide
{title2=$\mathrm{HCN}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Carbon dioxide
{title2=$\mathrm{CO}_2$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= CO2
{c}
{synonym}

= Polymeric carbon dioxide
{parent=Carbon dioxide}

A nonmolecular extended carbon–oxygen solid. A corner-sharing tetrahedral model has four O neighbours per C and two C neighbours per bridging O, preserving CO2 stoichiometry.

= Amorphous carbonia
{parent=Polymeric carbon dioxide}

= Carbonia
{synonym}

An amorphous nonmolecular form of carbon dioxide. It is distinct from molecular dry ice and from assuming a unique crystalline polymorph.<Amorphous silica-like carbon dioxide by Mario Santoro and others>[Abstract: spectroscopy and diffraction characterization]{ref}

= Carbon monoxide
{title2=$\mathrm{CO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Ammonia
{title2=$\mathrm{NH}_3$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Methane
{title2=$\mathrm{CH}_4$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Methanogenesis
{parent=Methane}
{wiki}

Methanogenesis is biological production of <methane> by anaerobic microorganisms. Waterlogged soil and lake sediments can favour it. Warming or thaw changes substrate availability, but production is distinct from atmospheric emission because oxidation and transport intervene.

= Water
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Water vapor
{parent=Water}
{wiki}

<Water vapor> is gaseous <water>. Its absorption and emission contribute to atmospheric <radiative transfer> and the <greenhouse effect>.

= Self-ionization of water
{parent=Water}
{wiki}

Two <water> <molecules> can exchange a <proton>, producing <hydronium> and <hydroxide> <ions>: $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\rightleftharpoons\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^++\mathrm{OH}^-$. Even pure <water> therefore supplies <ions>; extreme <dilution> eventually invalidates the <strong acid> assumption that it supplies all the <hydronium>.

= Ice
{parent=Water}
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}(s)$}
{wiki}

= Snowflake
{parent=Ice}
{wiki}

A snowflake is an <ice> crystal or an aggregate of <ice> crystals grown in atmospheric conditions. Its morphology depends on vapour supersaturation, transport, <temperature> and crystal anisotropy. Not every snowflake is dendritic.

= Dendritic growth of a snowflake
{parent=Snowflake}

An exposed tip receives vapour more efficiently than a sheltered surface and can amplify its lead, producing <crystal dendrites> and side branches. The planar thermal <solidification> mechanism is an analogy, not a complete vapour-growth model. The <hexagonal ice crystal anisotropy> selects six preferred directions; an isotropic linear instability alone does not select six arms.

= Propidium iodide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=PI}

<Propidium iodide> is a <nucleic acid> stain used in a <flow cytometric DNA-content assay>; <ribonuclease A> removes interfering <RNA>.<Cell Cycle Analysis by Propidium Iodide Staining by UCL Institute of Child Health Flow Cytometry Core Facility>[Cell Cycle Analysis by Propidium Iodide Staining, p. 2, Procedure steps 6–9]{ref}

= Ubiquinone
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$Q$}

A mobile <electron carrier> in the <mitochondrial respiratory chain>.

= Succinate
{parent=Chemical compound}

A <substrate> of <succinate dehydrogenase> in the <citric acid cycle>.

= Alcohol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki=Alcohol_(chemistry)}

<Alcohols> such as <ethanol> and <glycerol> contain <hydroxy groups> on <carbon> <chemical atoms> with only single <covalent bonds>.

= Ethylene glycol
{parent=Alcohol}

The diol with structure $\mathrm{HOCH_2CH_2OH}$. In a metal-nanostructure <polyol process> it can serve as a high-boiling <solvent> and part of the reducing chemistry; reaction conditions and additives determine the actual <reduction> and growth rates.

= Secondary alcohol
{parent=Alcohol}

An <alcohol> whose hydroxyl-bearing saturated <carbon> has two <carbon> substituents and one <hydrogen>.

= Primary alcohol
{parent=Alcohol}

An <alcohol> whose hydroxyl-bearing saturated <carbon> has one <carbon> substituent; methanol is often treated as a special closely related case.

= Tertiary alcohol
{parent=Alcohol}

An <alcohol> whose hydroxyl-bearing carbon is bonded to three carbon substituents. Its oxygenated carbon carries no directly attached proton, so it lacks an ordinary carbon-proton <HSQC> cross peak.

= 4-Bromobutan-1-ol
{parent=Alcohol}

$\mathrm{Br(CH_2)_4OH}$, a bifunctional four-carbon building block. The terminal groups are not vicinal; synthetic protection permits selective use of the <bromide> or <alcohol>.

= Halohydrin
{parent=Alcohol}

A compound with a halogen and hydroxyl group on adjacent carbons. <alkoxide> formation can permit intramolecular <SN2 reaction> to an <epoxide>.

= Bromohydrin
{parent=Halohydrin}

A <halohydrin> with <bromine> on the carbon adjacent to OH. Its relative <stereochemistry> can select between properly aligned <epoxide> closure and <semipinacol rearrangement>.

= 2-Phenylethanol
{parent=Alcohol}

= 1-Propanol
{parent=Alcohol}

= Propargylic alcohol
{parent=Alcohol}

A <propargylic alcohol> has its hydroxyl-bearing <carbon> adjacent to a <carbon>–<carbon> triple bond. Its alkoxide can coordinate a metal hydride and direct <alkyne> reduction.

= Cyclohexane
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= p-xylene
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Coenzyme A
{parent=Chemical compound}
{tag=Coenzyme}

= Acetyl-CoA
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Oxaloacetate
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Isocitrate
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Alpha-ketoglutarate
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Succinyl-CoA
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Fumarate
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Flavin adenine dinucleotide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{tag=Coenzyme}

= FAD
{c}
{synonym}

= FADH2
{c}
{parent=Flavin adenine dinucleotide}
{title2=$\mathrm{FADH}_2$}

= Ubiquinol
{parent=Chemical compound}

= QH2
{c}
{synonym}

= Malonate
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Potassium hydrogen phthalate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{KHC_8H_4O_4}$}

A salt used as a <primary standard> for <titrant standardization>; its acid-bearing <anion> consumes one <hydroxide> per formula unit in the specified <neutralization>.

= Phenolphthalein
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

A <pH indicator> used in the inspected <titrant standardization> protocol. Its visible color signals a <titration endpoint> rather than defining the <equivalence point>.

= Phosphoric acid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{H_3PO_4}$}

A <chemical acid> with successive <protonation> states; it is one component of the inspected institutional <acid-base titration> experiment.

= Acetone
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$C_3H_6O$}
{wiki}

= Chloroform
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$CHCl_3$}
{wiki}

= Iodide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm I^-$}
{wiki}

An <iodide> is an <iodine> <anion> with <electric charge> $-e$, where $e$ is the <elementary charge>.

= Trypan blue
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Calcite
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Pigment
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Mica
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Glass
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Glass phase separation
{parent=Glass}

Formation of compositionally distinct regions in a <glass>. A minor, less durable region can change release behaviour even when the bulk composition is unchanged.

= Devitrification
{parent=Glass}

Formation of crystalline regions from an initially glassy material. Its effect on waste-form durability depends on the phases formed and the composition left in the residual <glass>.

= Glass network modifier
{parent=Glass}

A constituent that changes network connectivity or charge balance. Alkali ions can be exchanged during aqueous <glass> alteration without initially releasing the entire silicate network.

= Glass network former
{parent=Glass}

A constituent participating in the connected <glass> network. <silicon> and <boron> can be network formers in borosilicate waste <glass>; local coordination depends on composition.

= Borosilicate glass
{parent=Glass}

A <glass> containing boron- and silicon-based network formers. Waste-glass durability depends on the full composition and contacting solution, not solely on the presence of these two elements.

= Glass transition
{parent=Glass}

A kinetic crossover at which structural or segmental relaxation becomes too slow for the observation timescale. Its apparent temperature depends on thermal history and measurement rate.

= Glass transition temperature
{parent=Glass transition}
{title2=$T_g$}

The temperature assigned to a <glass transition> under specified measurement conditions.

= Fox equation
{parent=Glass transition temperature}
{c}

The empirical relation $1/T_g=w_1/T_{g1}+w_2/T_{g2}$ for some homogeneous <polymer> mixtures or copolymers, with temperatures in kelvin and mass fractions $w_i$.

= Brass
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Liquid air
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Dichlorophenolindophenol
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= DCPIP
{c}
{synonym}

<DCPIP> is an artificial <electron acceptor> used in the <chloroplast dye-reduction experiment>. Under its assay conditions, the oxidized dye is blue and its <reduction> removes that blue color.

= Ketone
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

A <ketone> has a <carbonyl group> whose <carbon> is bonded to two other <carbon> positions.

= Butan-2-one
{parent=Ketone}

$\mathrm{CH_3COCH_2CH_3}$.

= Acetophenone
{parent=Ketone}

$\mathrm{PhCOCH_3}$.

= Cyclopentanone
{parent=Ketone}

= 1,3-diketone
{parent=Ketone}

Two <ketone> <carbonyls> separated by one <carbon>. The intervening C–H bonds are relatively acidic, and the <enol> can be stabilized by <pi conjugation> and an intramolecular <hydrogen bond>.

= Pinacolone
{parent=Ketone}

The <ketone> $\mathrm{(CH_3)_3C{-}C(=O){-}CH_3}$. Only the methyl side supplies alpha <hydrogens> for ordinary <enolate> formation.

= Xanthone
{parent=Ketone}

A dibenzo-gamma-<pyrone> framework with two outer <benzene> rings, an endocyclic <ether> <oxygen> and an exocyclic <ketone> <oxygen>. The outer rings are strongly aromatic; the central ring has a more polarized, partial aromatic description.

= 3-Chlorobutan-2-one
{parent=Ketone}

The <ketone> $\mathrm{CH_3COCH(Cl)CH_3}$. Its methine and attached methyl show reciprocal <vicinal coupling>, while the acetyl methyl can appear as a singlet.

= Adjacent diketone
{parent=Ketone}

A compound with neighbouring ketone carbonyl groups. Two carbonyl unsaturations can account for the entire index of hydrogen deficiency without any alkene.

= Pentane-2,3-dione
{parent=Ketone}

An unsymmetrical adjacent diketone with structure CH3COCOCH2CH3. Its two carbonyl carbons need not be separately assignable from a shift list alone.

= Indenone
{parent=Ketone}

A benzene-fused five-membered unsaturated cyclic ketone.

= Cyclohexanone
{parent=Ketone}

A six-membered cyclic <ketone>.

= Methyl vinyl ketone
{parent=Ketone}

= 1,4-Benzoquinone
{parent=Ketone}
{wiki}

= Para-quinone
{synonym}

A planar conjugated molecule with two opposite carbonyl groups and molecular <point group> $D_{2h}$. A simple eight-site <Hückel method> model uses one perpendicular $p$ orbital on each of its six carbon and two oxygen atoms.

= Hemiacetal
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

A <hemiacetal> has an <hydroxy group> and an $\mathrm{OR}$ group on the same saturated <carbon>; $R$ is attached through <carbon>.

= Hemiketal
{parent=Hemiacetal}

A <hemiketal> is the addition product of an <alcohol> and a <ketone>, with <hydroxy group> and alkoxy substituents on the former <carbonyl> <carbon>. Intramolecular formation can temporarily tether an <alcohol> to a <ketone> before another <chemical reaction> changes the carbon skeleton.

= Lactol
{parent=Hemiacetal}

A cyclic hemiacetal in equilibrium with an open-chain hydroxy carbonyl compound. Different lactol anomers can converge to the same planar aldehyde before nucleophilic addition.

= Oligomycin
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

Inhibits mitochondrial <ATP synthase>; used to distinguish <ATP>-linked respiration from residual <oxygen> consumption.

= FCCP
{c}
{parent=Chemical substance}

A chemical used to produce <respiratory uncoupling> by carrying <protons> across the <inner mitochondrial membrane>.

= Antimycin A
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

An inhibitor of <respiratory complex III>.

= Glycerophosphate
{parent=Chemical substance}

<Glycerophosphate> denotes a phosphate ester of <glycerol>; added <glycerophosphate> served as a <substrate> in the <rat-liver acid-phosphatase latency experiment>.

= Iodoacetone
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COCH_2I}$}

= Barium platinocyanide
{parent=Chemical substance}

A <chemical compound> used as a <fluorescence> screen in the <Röntgen X-ray discovery experiment>.
